\nFirst diagonal (units):4.\n\nSecond diagonal (tens): lower part of cell to the left (Row2, first column's lower:2) and upper part of cell above (Row1, second column's upper:0). So 2+0=2.\n\nThird diagonal (hundreds): upper part of Row2 first column (1) and lower part of Row1 first column (6). 1+6=7.\n\nFourth diagonal (thousands): upper part of Row1 first column (0).\n\nSo the result is 724. Hmm, but this contradicts the correct answer. \n\nWait, maybe the diagonals are ordered differently. Perhaps the first diagonal is the thousands, then hundreds, etc. No, that would make it 0724, which is 724 still. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I have the rows and columns mixed up. If the first number is on the right and the second on the top, but that should not affect the result because multiplication is commutative. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made an error in the problem itself. Let me check with another method.\n\nUsing the standard algorithm:\n\n\u2003\xa012  \n×\xa062  \n------  \n\u200324  (12×2)  \n720  (12×60, shifted one position to the left)  \n------  \n744  \n\nYes, that gives 744. Therefore, there must be an error in the lattice method steps I took. Maybe I miscounted the diagonals. Alternatively, perhaps the lattice method requires a different arrangement. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps I need to add the diagonals from the top left. Let me try adding from the opposite direction.\n\nStarting from the top-left corner's upper part (0):\n\nFirst diagonal (thousands):0.\n\nSecond diagonal (hundreds):0 (from top-left lower) +1 (from bottom-left upper). Wait, this is getting too convoluted. Maybe I should accept that I might have made a mistake in the lattice method and rely on the correct answer from other methods.\n\nSince all other methods give 744, I think the correct answer is 744. The lattice method must have been applied incorrectly by me, perhaps due to a miscalculation in the setup or addition. \n\nTherefore, after verifying through distributive property, standard mu