    {
      "index": 0,
      "text": "\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 96 and 19. Let me think about how to do this step by step. Hmm, multiplying two numbers, right? 96 times 19. I remember that multiplying by a two-digit number can be broken down into parts. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. Let me recall, the distributive property is when you split one of the numbers into smaller parts to make the multiplication easier.\n\nSo, 19 is close to 20, which might be easier to work with. Maybe I can think of 19 as 20 minus 1. That way, 96 times 19 would be the same as 96 times (20 - 1). Using the distributive property, that would be 96*20 minus 96*1. Let me write that down:\n\n96 \u00d7 19 = 96 \u00d7 (20 - 1) = (96 \u00d7 20) - (96 \u00d7 1)\n\nAlright, now I need to calculate each part separately. Let's do 96 times 20 first. Since 20 is 2 times 10, maybe I can break that down further. 96 \u00d7 2 is 192, and then multiplying by 10 gives 1920. So, 96 \u00d7 20 is 1920.\n\nThen, 96 \u00d7 1 is just 96. So subtracting that from 1920 would be 1920 - 96. Let me do that subtraction. Hmm, 1920 minus 96. Maybe I can subtract 100 first and then add back 4 because 96 is 4 less than 100. So, 1920 - 100 is 1820, then adding 4 gives 1824. Wait, let me check that again. If I take away 100 from 1920, that's 1820, but since I took away 4 more than I needed to, I should add those 4 back. So yes, 1820 + 4 = 1824. That seems right.\n\nAlternatively, I could do the subtraction directly: 1920 minus 96. Let's see, 1920 minus 90 is 1830, then minus 6 more is 1824. Yep, same result. Okay, so that part checks out.\n\nSo putting it all together, 96 \u00d7 19 = 1824. Let me just make sure I didn't make any mistakes in my calculations. Let me try another method to verify. Maybe the standard algorithm for multiplication.\n\nMultiplying 96 by 19 using the standard method:\n\nFirst, write 96  \n\u00d719  \n________\n\nMultiply 96 by 9 (the units digit of 19):  \n9 \u00d7 6 is 54, write down 4 and carry over 5.  \n9 \u00d7 9 is 81, plus the carried 5 is 86. So that gives 864.\n\nThen multiply 96 by 1 (the tens digit of 19), but since it's the tens place, we need to shift one position to the left, so it's actually 10. So 96 \u00d7 10 is 960.\n\nNow add the two results together: 864 + 960. Let's add them:\n\n864 + 960:  \n4 + 0 = 4  \n6 + 6 = 12, write down 2 and carry over 1.  \n8 + 9 = 17, plus the carried 1 is 18.\n\nSo that gives 1824. Same result as before. Okay, that's a good sign.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do another check using another method. Let's see, perhaps using the difference of squares or some other trick, but I'm not sure if that applies here. Alternatively, I can add 96 nineteen times, but that would be tedious. Let me see if there's another way.\n\nAlternatively, maybe breaking down 96 into 90 + 6. Let's try that.\n\nSo 96 \u00d7 19 = (90 + 6) \u00d7 19 = 90\u00d719 + 6\u00d719.\n\nCalculating 90\u00d719: 90\u00d710=900, 90\u00d79=810, so 900 + 810 = 1710.\n\nThen 6\u00d719: 6\u00d710=60, 6\u00d79=54, so 60+54=114.\n\nAdding those together: 1710 + 114. Let's see, 1710 + 100 = 1810, then +14 is 1824. Again, same answer. Okay, so three different methods all give me 1824, so I feel confident that's the correct answer.\n\nWait, let me just confirm once more with a calculator-like approach. 96 multiplied by 20 is 1920, subtract 96 gives 1824. Yep, that's straightforward. I think that's solid.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use prime factors? Let's see if that's feasible.\n\nPrime factors of 96: 96 is 16 \u00d7 6, which is 2^5 \u00d7 3.\n\nPrime factors of 19: 19 is a prime number.\n\nSo multiplying them together: 2^5 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 19. Let's compute that step by step.\n\n2^5 is 32. 32 \u00d7 3 is 96. 96 \u00d7 19. Wait, that just brings us back to the original problem. Hmm, so maybe that's not helpful here. Alternatively, group differently.\n\n2^5 \u00d7 (3 \u00d7 19) = 32 \u00d7 57. Let's compute 32 \u00d7 57. Hmm, 30\u00d757 + 2\u00d757. 30\u00d757 is 1710, 2\u00d757 is 114, so total 1710+114=1824. There we go, that also works. So that's another method leading to the same result.\n\nAlright, so all these different methods are converging on 1824, so I think that's definitely the correct product of 96 and 19. I don't see any mistakes in my reasoning, and multiple verification steps confirm the answer. So I can confidently say the answer is 1824.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 96 and 19 is \\boxed{1824}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 96 and 19, we can use the distributive property by breaking down 19 into 20 - 1:\n\n\\[\n96 \\times 19 = 96 \\times (20 - 1) = (96 \\times 20) - (96 \\times 1)\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate \\(96 \\times 20\\):\n\n\\[\n96 \\times 20 = 96 \\times 2 \\times 10 = 192 \\times 10 = 1920\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate \\(96 \\times 1\\):\n\n\\[\n96 \\times 1 = 96\n\\]\n\nSubtract the second result from the first:\n\n\\[\n1920 - 96 = 1824\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   96 \\\\\n\\times 19 \\\\\n\\hline\n   864 \\quad (\\text{96} \\times 9) \\\\\n+ 960 \\quad (\\text{96} \\times 10) \\\\\n\\hline\n  1824 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAnother verification method using prime factors:\n\n\\[\n96 \\times 19 = (2^5 \\times 3) \\times 19 = 32 \\times 3 \\times 19 = 96 \\times 19\n\\]\n\nBreaking it down further:\n\n\\[\n32 \\times (3 \\times 19) = 32 \\times 57 = (30 \\times 57) + (2 \\times 57) = 1710 + 114 = 1824\n\\]\n\nAll methods confirm the result is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1824}\n\\]",
