2400, 400 + 80 = 480, and 30 + 6 = 36. Wait, no, maybe that's complicating it. Alternatively, 2430 + 486. Adding the hundreds: 2400 + 400 = 2800. Then the tens and ones: 30 + 86 = 116. So 2800 + 116 = 2916. Yeah, that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do it another way to verify. Let's try the vertical multiplication again. \n\nMultiplying 81 by 6 first:\n\n81\n×\xa036\n─────\n486 (this is 81×6)\n+2430 (this is 81×30, shifted one position to the left)\n─────\n2916\n\nYes, adding 486 and 2430 gives 2916. \n\nAnother way to check could be using another method, like the difference of squares or some other trick, but I don't think that applies here. Alternatively, maybe breaking down the numbers into factors. Let's see, 81 is 9 squared, so 9×9, and 36 is 6 squared, 6×6. But not sure if that helps multiply them directly. \n\nAlternatively, 81 × 36. Since 36 is 4×9, so 81×4×9. 81×4 is 324, then 324×9. Let's see, 324×9: 300×9=2700, 24×9=216, so 2700+216=2916. That matches the previous result. \n\nHmm, so that works too. So 81 times 36 is indeed 2916. Let me see if there's another way. Maybe using 80 + 1 times 36? So (80 +1)(30 +6). Wait, that's expanding the terms. Let's try that:\n\n(80 +1)(30 +6) = 80×30 + 80×6 + 1×30 + 1×6. \n\nCalculating each term:\n\n80×30 = 2400\n\n80×6 = 480\n\n1×30 = 30\n\n1×6 = 6\n\nAdding all those: 2400 + 480 = 2880; 2880 +30=2910; 2910 +6=2916. \n\nSame result again. Okay, so multiple methods are giving me 2916, so I think that's the correct answer. Maybe one more check with a calculator approach? Well, since I can't use a calculator, but let me do the multiplication step by step again. \n\n81 multiplied by 36. Let's consider 36 as 36. So 81*36. Let me write it as 81*36. If I do 81*36, then 80*36 is 2880, plus 1*36 is 36, so 2880+36=2916. Yep, same answer. \n\nAlright, so all different methods of breaking it down give me 2916, so I feel confident that the product of 81 and 36 is 2916.\n"]
