t.\n\nAll methods check out. I think that's thorough enough. I can't see any errors here. Maybe I can use a calculator to verify, but since this is a mental math problem, I should rely on my calculations. Alternatively, I can reverse the multiplication: 65*50 instead of 50*65, which should give the same result because multiplication is commutative.\n\nLet me do 65*50. Using the standard method:\n\n\u2003\xa050\n×\xa065\n------\n?\n\nMultiply 50 by 5 (units place): 50*5=250.\n\nMultiply 50 by 6 (tens place, so actually 60): 50*60=3000. Then add 250 + 3000 = 3250. Same answer again.\n\nAlright, that's consistent. I think I'm confident that the product of 50 and 65 is indeed 3250. No mistakes detected in the calculations, and multiple methods all converge to the same result. So I think that's the correct answer.\n"]  6%|▌         | 29/500 [28:41<6:25:09, 49.07s/it]
