      "text": "\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 43 and 23. Let me think about how to do this step by step. First, I remember that multiplying two numbers means adding one number to itself as many times as the other number says. But since these are both two-digit numbers, maybe there's a better way than just adding 43 twenty-three times. \n\nHmm, maybe I can use the standard multiplication algorithm. Let me recall how that works. You line up the numbers vertically, right? So:\n\n\u2003\u00a043\n\u00d7\u00a023\n------\n\u00a0\n\nStarting with the units place of the bottom number, which is 3. Multiply that by 43. So 3 times 43. Wait, actually, no, maybe I should break it down more. Let me write it out properly.\n\nFirst, multiply 43 by the units digit of 23, which is 3. That should be 43 \u00d7 3. Let me calculate that. 40 times 3 is 120, and 3 times 3 is 9. So 120 + 9 = 129. So that gives me 129.\n\nThen, I need to multiply 43 by the tens digit of 23, which is 2. But since it's in the tens place, it's actually 20. So 43 \u00d7 20. Let me do that. 43 \u00d7 2 is 86, and then adding a zero gives 860. Wait, is that right? Let me check again. 43 multiplied by 20 is the same as 43 multiplied by 2 times 10, so yes, 86 \u00d710 = 860. Okay, that seems correct.\n\nNow, I have to add those two results together: 129 + 860. Let me add them. 129 + 860. 120 + 860 is 980, and then add the remaining 9 to get 989. Wait, hold on. Wait, 129 + 860. Let's see: 860 + 100 is 960, plus 29 is 989. Yeah, that's right. So the total is 989?\n\nWait, but let me confirm that again because sometimes when I do mental math I might make a mistake. Let me try another method to cross-verify.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the distributive property: (40 + 3)(20 + 3). Let's expand that. \n\nFirst, 40\u00d720 = 800.\n\nThen, 40\u00d73 = 120.\n\nNext, 3\u00d720 = 60.\n\nLastly, 3\u00d73 = 9.\n\nNow add all those together: 800 + 120 is 920, plus 60 is 980, plus 9 is 989. Okay, same result. So that seems to confirm it. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do it another way. Let's see, 43 times 23. If I think of 23 as 20 + 3, then 43\u00d720 + 43\u00d73. Which is exactly what I did before. So that gives the same numbers. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using some other multiplication trick. Like, 43 times 23. Since 23 is close to 25, which is a quarter of 100, but I don't know if that helps. Alternatively, maybe 43 times 23 is the same as 23 times 43, so maybe I can switch them if that's easier. Let me try 23 times 43.\n\n23\n\u00d743\n-----\n23\u00d73 = 69\n\n23\u00d740 = 920\n\nAdding 69 + 920 = 989. Yep, same answer again. \n\nHmm, seems like all methods are pointing to 989. Maybe I can check using a calculator, but since I don't have one here, I'll try another verification. Let me divide 989 by one of the numbers and see if I get the other. Let's divide 989 by 43 and see if I get 23.\n\nSo, 43 into 989. Let me do the division:\n\n43 \u00d7 20 = 860. Subtract that from 989: 989 - 860 = 129. \n\nThen, 43 \u00d7 3 = 129. So 20 + 3 = 23. Perfect, that works out. So 43 \u00d723 is indeed 989.\n\nAlternatively, divide 989 by 23. Let's see:\n\n23 \u00d7 40 = 920. Subtract that from 989: 989 - 920 = 69.\n\n23 \u00d7 3 = 69. So 40 +3 =43. Yep, that checks out too.\n\nSo all my methods confirm that the product is 989. I think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. Therefore, the answer should be 989.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 43 and 23 is \\boxed{989}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 43 and 23, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. Multiply 43 by the units digit of 23 (which is 3):\n   \\[\n   43 \\times 3 = 129\n   \\]\n2. Multiply 43 by the tens digit of 23 (which is 2, representing 20):\n   \\[\n   43 \\times 20 = 860\n   \\]\n3. Add the results of the two multiplications:\n   \\[\n   129 + 860 = 989\n   \\]\n\nWe can verify this result using the distributive property:\n\\[\n(40 + 3)(20 + 3) = 40 \\times 20 + 40 \\times 3 + 3 \\times 20 + 3 \\times 3\n\\]\n\\[\n= 800 + 120 + 60 + 9 = 989\n\\]\n\nAdditionally, checking by division confirms the result:\n- Dividing 989 by 43 gives 23.\n- Dividing 989 by 23 gives 43.\n\nThus, the product of 43 and 23 is \\boxed{989}.",
