      "text": ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 24 and 67. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers... I remember that product means multiplication, right? So, 24 times 67. Alright, let me recall how to do this. Maybe I should use the standard multiplication algorithm. Let me write it down step by step.\n\nFirst, I can write 24 on top and 67 below it, aligning the digits by place value. Wait, actually, usually we put the larger number on top, but maybe it doesn't matter here. Let me see. Let me try 24 on the top and 67 on the bottom. So:\n\n```\n   24\nx 67\n-------\n```\n\nNow, I need to multiply each digit of the bottom number by each digit of the top number, right? Starting with the ones place. So first, multiply 24 by 7 (the ones digit of 67), then multiply 24 by 60 (the tens digit, which is 6 in the tens place, so 60), and then add the two results together.\n\nLet me do the first part: 24 times 7. Let's break that down. 20 times 7 is 140, and 4 times 7 is 28. Adding those together: 140 + 28 = 168. So that's 24 x 7 = 168.\n\nNow, the second part is 24 times 60. Since 60 is 6 tens, I can think of this as 24 x 6 x 10. Let me compute 24 x 6 first. 20 x 6 = 120 and 4 x 6 = 24, so 120 +24 = 144. Then multiply by 10 gives 1440. So 24 x 60 = 1440.\n\nNow I need to add the two results: 168 + 1440. Let me add them. 168 + 1440. Hmm, 1440 + 100 is 1540, then +68 is 1608. Wait, let me check again. 1440 plus 168. 1440 + 160 is 1600, then plus 8 is 1608. Yeah, that's correct.\n\nSo, the total product is 1608. Let me just confirm this another way to be sure. Maybe using the distributive property differently. 24 x 67 can be written as (20 +4)(60 +7). Expanding that, it's 20*60 + 20*7 +4*60 +4*7.\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n20*60 = 1200,\n\n20*7 = 140,\n\n4*60 = 240,\n\n4*7 = 28.\n\nAdding them all together: 1200 +140 is 1340, plus 240 is 1580, plus 28 is 1608. Okay, same result. That's reassuring.\n\nAlternatively, maybe do it another way. Let's see, 24 times 67. Maybe breaking down 67 into 70 -3? Hmm, 24*(70 -3) = 24*70 -24*3. Let's see:\n\n24*70: 24*7=168, so times 10 is 1680,\n\n24*3=72,\n\nSo 1680 -72 = 1608. Yep, same answer again. So that's good. \n\nAlternatively, using the standard vertical multiplication:\n\n   24\nx 67\n-------\n  168  (this is 24 x7)\n+1440  (this is 24 x60, shifted one position to the left)\n-------\n 1608\n\nYes, that adds up. So I think the answer is 1608. Let me check with a calculator in my head. Wait, 24*60 is 1440, 24*7 is 168, so 1440+168 is indeed 1608. Okay, I think that's correct.\n\nI don't see any mistakes in my calculations. All the different methods give me the same result, so I'm confident that the product of 24 and 67 is 1608.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 24 and 67 is \\boxed{1608}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 24 and 67, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. Multiply 24 by 7 (the ones digit of 67):\n   \\[\n   24 \\times 7 = 168\n   \\]\n2. Multiply 24 by 60 (the tens digit of 67, which is 6 in the tens place):\n   \\[\n   24 \\times 60 = 1440\n   \\]\n3. Add the results of the two multiplications:\n   \\[\n   168 + 1440 = 1608\n   \\]\n\nWe can verify this using the distributive property:\n\\[\n(20 + 4)(60 + 7) = 20 \\times 60 + 20 \\times 7 + 4 \\times 60 + 4 \\times 7\n\\]\nCalculating each term:\n\\[\n20 \\times 60 = 1200, \\quad 20 \\times 7 = 140, \\quad 4 \\times 60 = 240, \\quad 4 \\times 7 = 28\n\\]\nAdding these together:\n\\[\n1200 + 140 + 240 + 28 = 1608\n\\]\n\nAnother verification using the distributive property:\n\\[\n24 \\times (70 - 3) = 24 \\times 70 - 24 \\times 3\n\\]\nCalculating each term:\n\\[\n24 \\times 70 = 1680, \\quad 24 \\times 3 = 72\n\\]\nSubtracting these:\n\\[\n1680 - 72 = 1608\n\\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 24 and 67 is \\boxed{1608}.",
