ial product (tens digit, which is 2 in 24, so 20):\n\n20 *53:  \n2*3=6, but since it's the tens place, this 6 is actually 60, so write 6 in the tens place. Wait, actually, when writing the partial product, you shift one position left, so:\n\nFirst digit: 2*3 =6, but written in the tens column, so it's the tens place. Then 2*5=10, so writing that in the hundreds place. So 2*5 is 10, so write 0 in the hundreds and carryover 1 to the thousands? Wait, no, perhaps:\n\nWait, when multiplying the 2 (which is in the tens place) by 5 (which is in the tens place of 53?), no, 5 is in the tens place of 53. Wait, 53 is 5 tens and 3 units. \n\nSo 2 (tens) * 3 (units) = 6 tens (i.e., 60), and 2 (tens) * 5 (tens) = 10 hundreds (1000). So total is 1000 + 60 = 1060. \n\nAdding to the first partial product 212 (which is 200 +12) gives 1060 + 200 = 1260, plus 12 is 1272. \n\nYes, that still holds. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using division. If 53 *24 =1272, then 1272 divided by 53 should be 24. Let me try dividing 1272 by 53. \n\n53 goes into 127 two times (since 53*2=106). 127 -106 =21. Bring down the 2, making it 212. \n\n53 goes into 212 exactly 4 times (53*4=212). So total is 24. Perfect, that checks out. \n\nAlternatively, divide 1272 by 24. 24*50=1200. 1272-1200=72. 24*3=72. So 50+3=53. Yep, that works. \n\nAlright, so after multiple methods and checks, I'm confident that the product of 53 and 24 is indeed 1272. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, and all the verification steps confirm the answer. So I think that's the correct result.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 53 and 24 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Break down the numbers using the distributive property:**\n   \\[\n   (50 + 3) \\times (20 + 4) = 50 \\times 20 + 50 \\times 4 + 3 \\times 20 + 3 \\times 4\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 1000 + 200 + 60 + 12 = 1272\n   \\]\n\n2. **Standard multiplication algorithm:**\n   - Multiply 53 by 4:  \n     \\(53 \\times 4 = 212\\)\n   - Multiply 53 by 20 (shift one place left):