final_output:  ['\n\nTo find the product of 23 and 95, we can use the distributive property and the standard multiplication algorithm.\n\nFirst, we break down the multiplication using the distributive property:\n\\[\n23 \\times 95 = 23 \\times (90 + 5) = (23 \\times 90) + (23 \\times 5)\n\\]\n\nCalculating each part separately:\n1. \\(23 \\times 5\\):\n   \\[\n   23 \\times 5 = 115\n   \\]\n\n2. \\(23 \\times 90\\):\n   \\[\n   23 \\times 90 = 23 \\times (9 \\times 10) = (23 \\times 9) \\times 10\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   23 \\times 9 = 207 \\quad \\text{(since } 20 \\times 9 = 180 \\text{ and } 3 \\times 9 = 27; 180 + 27 = 207\\text{)}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   207 \\times 10 = 2070\n   \\]\n\nAdding the two results together:\n\\[\n115 + 2070 = 2185\n\\]\n\nWe can also verify this using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   \\quad 23 \\\\\n\\times 95 \\\\\n\\hline\n   115 \\quad (\\text{23 × 5}) \\\\\n+2070 \\quad (\\text{23 × 90, shifted one position to the left}) \\\\\n\\hline\n   2185 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 23 and 95 is \\(\\boxed{2185}\\).', ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 23 and 95. Let me think about how to do this step by step. Hmm, multiplying two numbers... I remember that multiplication is essentially repeated addition, but doing 23 added 95 times would take forever. There must be a faster way.\n\nMaybe I can use the standard algorithm for multiplication. Let me recall how that works. You write the numbers vertically, aligning the digits by place value, then multiply each digit of the bottom number by the top number, starting from the right, and add the results. Wait, but both numbers here are two-digit numbers, so let me try that.\n\nFirst, I'll write 23 on top and 95 below it, aligning the ones place:\n\n\u2003\xa023\n×\xa095\n------\n\nNow, starting with the ones digit of the bottom number, which is 5. Multiply 5 by each digit of 23, starting from the right. \n\n5 × 3 = 15. I'll write down the 5 and carry over the 1.\n\nThen 5 × 2 = 10, plus the carried over 1 makes 11. So the first partial product is 115.\n\nNext, I need to multiply the tens digit of the bottom number, which is 9. But since it's in the tens place, it's actually 90. So I have to remember to shift this partial product one place to the left. Let me write it as 90 × 23. \n\nStarting again with the ones digit of the top number: 9 × 3 = 27. Write down 7 and carry over 2.\n\nThen 9 × 2 = 18, plus the carried over 2 is 20. So the second partial product is 207, but since we shifted, it's actually 2070. Wait, no, hold on. Wait, actually, when you write it down, the second line should be shifted one place to the left. Let me see:\n\nSo after the first partial product (115), the second multiplication by 9 (the tens digit) would be:\n\n\u2003\xa023\n×\xa095\n------\n\xa0115  (this is 23 × 5)\n+2070 (this is 23 × 90, so shifted left by one)\n------\nThen add them together.\n\nWait, but how exactly is this done? Let me write it step by step.\n\nFirst, multiplying by 5 (the ones place):\n\n5 × 3 = 15. Write 5, carry 1.\n\n5 × 2 = 10 + 1 = 11. So the first line is 115.\n\nThen, moving to the tens place, which is 9. Since it's the tens place, we need to start the next partial product one digit to the left. So we can put a zero placeholder first? Wait, actually, when doing it on paper, you don't write the zero, but you just start multiplying from the left. Let me think again.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can compute 23 × 90 separately and then add the two products.\n\nSo 23 × 95 = 23 × (90 + 5) = 23×90 + 23×5. That's the distributive property. Maybe breaking it down this way will help me avoid mistakes.\n\nLet me compute 23×5 first. 20×5=100, 3×5=15, so 100+15=115. That's the same as before.\n\nThen 23×90. Let's do 23×9×10. 23×9: 20×9=180, 3×9=27, so 180+27=207. Then multiply by 10 gives 2070.\n\nNow add 2070 + 115. Let's see: 2070 + 100 = 2170, then +15 = 2185. Wait, that gives 2185?\n\nWait, hold on. Let me add them again. 2070 plus 115. 2070 + 100 is 2170, then +15 is 2185. Hmm, but let me check with another method to be sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made an error in the multiplication steps. Let me try the standard algorithm again.\n\nWriting it out vertically:\n\n\u2003\u200323\n×\xa095\n-------\nFirst, multiply 23 by 5 (the ones digit):\n\n5×3 = 15, write 5, carry 1.\n\n5×2 =10 plus 1 is 11. So first partial product is 115.\n\nThen multiply by the 9 (the tens digit), so we need to shift one place to the left. So:\n\n9×3 =27, write down 7, carry 2.\n\n9×2 =18 plus 2 is 20. So the second partial product is 207, but shifted left one digit, making it 2070.\n\nAdding 115 and 2070:\n\n115\n\n+2070\n\n------\n\nAdding units digits: 5 + 0 =5\n\nTens place: 1 +7=8\n\nHundreds place: 1 +0=1 (from 115) and 0 from 2070? Wait no, let's line them up properly:\n\nWait, 115 is three digits: 1 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 5 (units).\n\n2070 is four digits: 2 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 0 (units). \n\nSo aligning:\n\n\u2003\xa0115\n\n+2070\n\n------\n\nStart from the right:\n\nUnits: 5 +0 =5\n\nTens: 1 +7=8\n\nHundreds: 1 +0=1\n\nThousands: 0 +2=2\n\nSo total is 2185. Okay, that seems consistent.\n\nWait, but let me check using another method to confirm. Maybe using the difference of squares or another approach?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps 23 × 95 can be thought of as (20 + 3)(90 +5). But that might complicate. Alternatively, 95 is 100 -5. So 23×(100-5)=23×100 -23×5=2300 -115=2185. Oh, that's another way and gives the same result. That confirms it again.\n\nSo 23×100 is 2300, minus 23×5=115, so 2300-115=2185. Yep, that works. So that's another method and same answer. So I think that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do 23×95 as 23×(9×10 +5). But that's similar to before. Anyway, seems like all methods point to 2185. Wait, but let me check with straight multiplication:\n\n23\n\ntimes 95. Let's do 23×5 first: 115. Then 23×90: 23×9=207, so 2070. Adding those gives 2185. Okay, that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, if I use the standard multiplication:\n\n\u2003\u2003\xa023\n\n×\xa095\n\n--------\n\nFirst step: 23×5=115\n\nSecond step: 23×90=2070\n\nAdd them together: 115 +2070=2185.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can use a calculator, but since this is a thought process, I have to do it manually. Let me see if there's any chance I messed up a multiplication step.\n\nWait, 23×9: 20×9=180, 3×9=27. 180+27=207. So that's correct. Then times 10 is 2070. Then adding 115: yes, 2070 +100 is 2170, plus 15 is 2185.\n\nAlternatively, maybe do 95×20 and 95×3, then add. Let's see:\n\n95×20: 95×2=190, so times 10 is 1900.\n\n95×3: 285.\n\nAdding 1900 +285 =2185. Yep, that works too. So that's another way. So that's consistent again.\n\nHmm, so all methods lead to 2185. Therefore, the product of 23 and 95 is 2185. I think that's the correct answer. I don't see any errors in my calculations, and cross-verifying with different methods gives the same result. So I'm confident that's the right answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 23 and 95 is \\boxed{2185}.\n"]
