squares, but maybe another approach.\n\nAlternatively, note that 77 is 7\u00d711 and 31 is a prime number. But I don't think that helps directly. Maybe just multiply straight out. \n\nAlternatively, think of 31 as 30 +1, so 77\u00d730 +77\u00d71. Wait, that's exactly what I did before, so same result.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps 77\u00d731. Let me reverse it, 31\u00d770 is 2170, and 31\u00d77 is 217. Then add them together: 2170 +217. Let me compute that: 2170 +200 is 2370, plus 17 is 2387. Yep, same answer again.\n\nHmm, so all methods are leading me to 2387. I think that's the correct product. Let me just confirm with a calculator in my mind. Wait, 77\u00d730 is 2310, plus 77 gives 2387. Yeah, that seems right. I don't see any errors in the steps. So I think the answer is 2387.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 77 and 31 is calculated as follows:\n\nFirst, break down the numbers using the distributive property:\n\\[\n(70 + 7) \\times (30 + 1) = 70 \\times 30 + 70 \\times 1 + 7 \\times 30 + 7 \\times 1\n\\]\n\nCalculate each term:\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n70 \\times 30 &= 2100, \\\\\n70 \\times 1 &= 70, \\\\\n7 \\times 30 &= 210, \\\\\n7 \\times 1 &= 7.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nAdd all the results together:\n\\[\n2100 + 70 + 210 + 7 = 2387.\n\\]\n\n**Verification using the standard multiplication algorithm:**\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n\\quad \\ 77 \\\\\n\\times 31 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\quad 77 \\quad (\\text{77 \u00d7 1}) \\\\\n+2310 \\quad (\\text{77 \u00d7 30, shifted one position left}) \\\\\n\\hline\n2387 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product is **2387**.\n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{2387}",
