 the area would be the product. Breaking 61 into 60+1 and 40 into 40+0 (but that might not help). Alternatively, breaking 40 into 30+10. Wait, maybe overcomplicating. Let me just stick with the previous methods. \n\nAlternatively, using calculator verification. But since I can't use a calculator, I can do manual multiplication step by step. \n\n61 multiplied by 40. \n\nFirst, ignore the zero in 40 for a second, so multiply 61 by 4. \n\n61 x4: \n\n1x4=4, 6x4=24. So that gives 244. Then since we ignored the zero, we add it back, making it 2440. \n\nYes, that's the same as before. \n\nAlternatively, adding 61 forty times. But that's tedious. But maybe add 61 40 times. Well, 61*10 is 610, so 61*40 is 610*4. 610*2 is 1220, times 2 again is 2440. Yep, same answer. \n\nAlright, so all methods are pointing to 2440. I think that's the correct product. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. Let me just confirm once more:\n\n61 * 40:\n\nMultiply 61 by 40:\n\n61 * 40 = (60 + 1) * (40) = 60*40 + 1*40 = 2400 + 40 = 2440. \n\nYep, that's solid. I think that's the answer.\n"]
