      "text": ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 91 and 41. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers means adding one number to itself as many times as the other number says. But doing that manually for 91 and 41 would take a while. Maybe there's a faster way?\n\nWait, maybe I can use the standard multiplication algorithm. Let me recall how that works. If I write 91 times 41, I can break it down into parts. Let me think, 41 is 40 plus 1, right? So maybe I can use distributive property here. That would be 91 times 40 plus 91 times 1. That might be easier to calculate.\n\nFirst, let me calculate 91 times 40. 91 multiplied by 40. Well, 91 times 4 is 364, so multiplying that by 10 gives me 3640. Wait, no, actually, 40 is 4 times 10, so 91*4 is 364, then 364*10 is 3640. Yeah, that seems right.\n\nThen, 91 times 1 is just 91. So adding those two results together: 3640 plus 91. Let me add those. 3640 plus 90 would be 3730, and then plus 1 more is 3731. So is the answer 3731?\n\nWait, let me check that again. Maybe I made a mistake in the addition. 3640 + 91. Let's break it down: 3640 + 90 is 3730, then add 1 to get 3731. Hmm, that seems correct. But let me try another method to confirm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do the multiplication the traditional way:\n\n\u2003\u200391  \n\u00d7\u00a041  \n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500  \nFirst multiply 91 by 1 (the units place of 41), which is 91.  \nThen multiply 91 by 40 (the tens place of 41, which is 4 in the tens place so 4*10). So 91*40 is 3640.  \nThen add them together: 91 + 3640 = 3731.  \nOkay, same result. Maybe that's right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use another method like the lattice method or some other trick? Let me think. 91 times 41. Wait, 91 is close to 90, and 41 is close to 40. Maybe approximate and adjust? Let's see:\n\n90 times 40 is 3600. Then, the difference would be (91-90)*(41) + (41-40)*(90) + (1*1). Wait, maybe that complicates things. Let me recall the formula (a+b)(c+d) = ac + ad + bc + bd.\n\nSo, (90 +1)(40 +1) = 90*40 + 90*1 + 1*40 + 1*1 = 3600 + 90 + 40 +1 = 3600 + 131 = 3731. Oh, that's another way to get the same answer. So that's consistent.\n\nHmm, so all methods point to 3731. Maybe I was right the first time. Let me just verify with a calculator in my mind. 40 times 90 is 3600, plus 10 times 90 is 900, but wait, no. Wait, perhaps breaking it down differently?\n\nAlternatively, 91 multiplied by 41. Let me write it out vertically again:\n\n\u2003\u2003\u00a091  \n\u2003\u00d741  \n\u2003\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500  \n\u2003\u200391 (this is 91\u00d71)  \n+3640 (this is 91\u00d740, shifted one position to the left)  \n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500  \n\u20033731\n\nYes, that's correct. The addition is 91 + 3640. Let me add the units digits: 0 +1 is 1. Then tens place: 4 +9 is 13, so write down 3 and carry over 1. Hundreds place: 6 + 3 plus the carried 1 is 10, so write 0 and carry 1. Thousands place: 3 + 0 plus carried 1 is 4. Wait, hold on, that doesn't align. Wait, maybe I need to write it out properly.\n\nWait, 3640 is:\n\n3 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 0 (units).\n\nAdding 91 (0 thousands, 0 hundreds, 9 tens, 1 unit).\n\nSo adding:\n\nThousands place: 3 + 0 = 3\n\nHundreds place: 6 + 0 = 6\n\nTens place: 4 + 9 = 13. Write down 3, carry over 1.\n\nUnits place: 0 +1 =1 plus the carryover? Wait no, the units place is just 0 +1. Wait, actually, when adding 3640 and 91:\n\n3640  \n+\u00a0\u00a091  \n=?\n\nStart from the rightmost digit:\n\n0 +1 =1\n\n4 +9 =13, so write 3, carryover 1.\n\n6 +0 =6 plus the carried 1 is 7.\n\n3 +0 =3.\n\nSo the total is 3731. Yes, that's correct. So that's consistent. \n\nAlternatively, if I do 91*41, maybe I can think of 41 as (40 +1), so 91*(40+1)=91*40 +91*1=3640+91=3731. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using some other multiplication trick? Like, for numbers near 100? Wait, 91 is 9 less than 100, and 41 is 59 less than 100? Hmm, maybe that complicates. Let me try.\n\nWait, the method for numbers near a base, like 100:\n\nIf both numbers are near 100, then the formula is (100 -a)(100 -b) = 10000 -100(a +b) +ab. But here, 91 is 100 -9, and 41 is 100 -59. So:\n\n(100 -9)(100 -59) = 100*100 -100*(9+59) + (9*59)\n\n=10000 -100*(68) + 531\n\n=10000 -6800 +531\n\n= (10000 -6800) +531 = 3200 +531 = 3731. \n\nWow, that also gives the same result. So that's another way. So that's consistent. \n\nSo all methods are giving me 3731, so I think that's correct. \n\nWait, just to be thorough, maybe I can do the multiplication step by step again:\n\nMultiplying 91 by 41.\n\nFirst, 1 times 91 is 91.\n\nThen 4 (which is in the tens place, so actually 40) times 91:\n\n4 times 1 is 4, 4 times 9 is 36. So 4*91 is 364, but since it's the tens place, it's 3640.\n\nAdding 91 + 3640:\n\n3640 + 91:\n\n3640 + 90 = 3730, plus 1 is 3731. \n\nYes. So that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, if I use the standard algorithm:\n\n\u2003\u2003\u00a091  \n\u2003\u00d7\u00a041  \n\u2003\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500  \n\u2003\u2003\u00a091 (this is 1\u00d791)  \n\u20033640 (this is 40\u00d791, written shifted one position to the left)  \n\u2003\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500  \n\u20033731\n\nYep, that adds up. \n\nI don't see any mistakes in my calculations. All different methods give the same answer, so I think 3731 is correct. Therefore, the product of 91 and 41 is 3731.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 91 and 41 is \\boxed{3731}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 91 and 41, we can use the distributive property and break down the numbers:\n\n1. Break down 41 into 40 and 1:\n   \\[\n   91 \\times 41 = 91 \\times (40 + 1)\n   \\]\n\n2. Apply the distributive property:\n   \\[\n   91 \\times 40 + 91 \\times 1\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate each part separately:\n   - \\( 91 \\times 40 \\):\n     \\[\n     91 \\times 4 = 364 \\quad \\text{then} \\quad 364 \\times 10 = 3640\n     \\]\n   - \\( 91 \\times 1 = 91 \\)\n\n4. Add the results of the two parts:\n   \\[\n   3640 + 91 = 3731\n   \\]\n\nVerification using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n- Multiply 91 by 1 (units place):\n  \\[\n  91 \\times 1 = 91\n  \\]\n- Multiply 91 by 40 (tens place, which is 4 in the tens place):\n  \\[\n  91 \\times 40 = 3640\n  \\]\n- Add the results:\n  \\[\n  3640 + 91 = 3731\n  \\]\n\nAnother verification using the formula \\((a + b)(c + d)\\):\n- Break down 91 as \\(90 + 1\\) and 41 as \\(40 + 1\\):\n  \\[\n  (90 + 1)(40 + 1) = 90 \\times 40 + 90 \\times 1 + 1 \\times 40 + 1 \\times 1\n  \\]\n- Calculate each term:\n  \\[\n  90 \\times 40 = 3600, \\quad 90 \\times 1 = 90, \\quad 1 \\times 40 = 40, \\quad 1 \\times 1 = 1\n  \\]\n- Sum the terms:\n  \\[\n  3600 + 90 + 40 + 1 = 3731\n  \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 91 and 41 is \\(\\boxed{3731}\\).",
