      "index": 0,
      "text": ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 43 and 36. Hmm, let me think about how to do this step by step. I remember that multiplying two numbers means adding one number to itself as many times as the other number says. But doing that directly here would take too long since 43 times 36 is a big number. Maybe there's a better way?\n\nWait, right, the standard multiplication algorithm. Let me recall how that works. You write the numbers vertically, aligning the digits by place value, then multiply each digit of the bottom number by the top number, starting from the right, and then add up the results. Let me try that.\n\nFirst, I'll write 43 on top and 36 below it, aligning the ones places:\n\n\u2003\u00a043\n\u00d7\u00a036\n------\n\nNow, starting with the ones digit of the bottom number, which is 6. Multiply 6 by 43. Let's do that:\n\n6 \u00d7 3 is 18. I'll write down 8 and carry over 1. Then 6 \u00d7 4 is 24, plus the carried 1 makes 25. So that gives 258. So the first partial product is 258.\n\nNext, I need to multiply the tens digit of the bottom number, which is 3 (but since it's in the tens place, it's actually 30). So I have to multiply 30 by 43. But since it's the tens place, the result should be shifted one position to the left. Let me do that step.\n\n3 \u00d7 3 is 9. Then 3 \u00d7 4 is 12. So that would be 129, but since it's the tens place, it's actually 1290. Wait, let me check again. \n\nWait, actually, when multiplying by the tens digit (3 in the tens place is 30), so 30 \u00d7 43. Let me compute that as 3 \u00d7 43 first, then multiply by 10. 3 \u00d7 43 is 129, so 129 \u00d7 10 is 1290. So that's the second partial product. \n\nNow I need to add the two partial products together: 258 + 1290. Let me add them.\n\n258 + 1290. Adding the ones place: 8 + 0 = 8. Tens place: 5 + 9 = 14. Write down 4 and carry over 1. Hundreds place: 2 + 2 + 1 (carry) = 5. Then the thousands place: 1. So that gives 1548? Wait, let me check that again.\n\nWait, maybe I should write it out:\n\n\u2003\u00a0258\n+1290\n------\nStarting from the right:\n8 + 0 = 8\n5 + 9 = 14 (write 4, carry 1)\n2 + 2 + 1 = 5\n1 (from 1290's thousands place) + nothing = 1\nSo the total is 1548. Hmm, okay.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do it another way to verify. Let me try breaking down the numbers using distributive property. 43 \u00d7 36 can be thought of as (40 + 3)(30 + 6). Let's expand that.\n\nFirst, 40 \u00d7 30 = 1200\nThen 40 \u00d7 6 = 240\nThen 3 \u00d7 30 = 90\nThen 3 \u00d7 6 = 18\n\nAdding all those together: 1200 + 240 is 1440, plus 90 is 1530, plus 18 is 1548. Okay, that matches the previous result. Good, so that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do 43 \u00d7 36 as 43 \u00d7 (30 + 6) which is 43\u00d730 + 43\u00d76. Let me compute each part:\n\n43 \u00d7 30. Well, 43 \u00d7 3 is 129, so times 10 is 1290. Then 43 \u00d7 6: 40\u00d76=240 and 3\u00d76=18, so 240+18=258. Then add 1290 + 258, which again is 1548. Same answer. So that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using another method: 36 is 4\u00d79, so 43 \u00d7 4 \u00d79. Let me see. 43\u00d74 is 172, then 172\u00d79. Hmm, 172\u00d79: 100\u00d79=900, 70\u00d79=630, 2\u00d79=18. Adding those: 900+630=1530, +18=1548. Again the same result. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do 43\u00d736 as (40\u00d736) + (3\u00d736). Let's see: 40\u00d736 is 1440, and 3\u00d736 is 108. Adding those together gives 1440 + 108 = 1548. Yep, same number again.\n\nSo all the different methods I try give me 1548, so I think that's the correct product. But just to be absolutely sure, maybe do the multiplication the standard way again.\n\n43\n\u00d736\n------\nFirst, 6\u00d743: 6\u00d73=18, write 8 carry 1. 6\u00d74=24 +1=25, so 258.\n\nThen 30\u00d743: 3\u00d743=129, so 1290. Adding 258 +1290: \n\n258 + 1290. Let's add 258 + 1000 = 1258, then +290 more (since 1290 is 1000+290). 1258 + 200 = 1458, then +90 = 1548. Yep, same result.\n\nAlright, so I think the answer is 1548. All the different methods confirm it. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. So I'm confident that's correct.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 43 and 36 is \\boxed{1548}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 43 and 36, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n\\quad 43 \\\\\n\\times 36 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\quad 258 \\quad \\text{(This is } 6 \\times 43\\text{)} \\\\\n+1290 \\quad \\text{(This is } 30 \\times 43\\text{, shifted one position to the left)} \\\\\n\\hline\n1548 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this result using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n43 \\times 36 = (40 + 3)(30 + 6)\n\\]\n\nExpanding this, we get:\n\n\\[\n40 \\times 30 + 40 \\times 6 + 3 \\times 30 + 3 \\times 6 = 1200 + 240 + 90 + 18 = 1548\n\\]\n\nAnother verification method involves breaking down the multiplication:\n\n\\[\n43 \\times 36 = 43 \\times (30 + 6) = 43 \\times 30 + 43 \\times 6 = 1290 + 258 = 1548\n\\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 43 and 36 is \\(\\boxed{1548}\\).",
