4\n\nWait, no, perhaps better to think of each cell's top and bottom parts as follows:\n\nThe full grid with all the numbers:\n\nThe top row's cells have their top halves in the upper triangle and bottom halves in the lower triangle.\n\nBut this is getting too complex without a visual.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I should abandon the lattice method here since it's leading to confusion, and rely on the other methods which all give 2744. Since multiple methods (standard algorithm, distributive property, breaking into 56*(50-1)) all give the same result, I can be confident that 2744 is correct, and perhaps I made an error in the lattice setup.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I placed the numbers in the wrong cells. Let me try once more:\n\nWait, in the lattice method, the first number (56) is on the top, and the second (49) on the right. Each cell at the intersection of a row (from the second number) and column (from the first number) contains the product of those digits.\n\nSo rows are for the second number's digits: 4 (tens) and9 (ones).\n\nColumns are for the first number's digits:5 (tens) and6 (ones).\n\nTherefore, the four cells are:\n\nRow 4, Column5:4\u00d75=20 \u21922/0\n\nRow4, Column6:4\u00d76=24 \u21922/4\n\nRow9, Column5:9\u00d75=45 \u21924/5\n\nRow9, Column6:9\u00d76=54 \u21925/4\n\nNow, arranging the cells as:\n\nColumns (5 and6):\n\nRow4: [2/0][2/4]\n\nRow9: [4/5][5/4]\n\nNow, the diagonals for addition are from bottom-right to top-left:\n\nThe bottom-right cell is row9, column6 (5/4). Its bottom-right digit is4 \u2192 units place.\n\nThe next diagonal includes:\n\n- The cell above it (row4, column6)'s bottom-right digit (4),\n\n- The cell to the left (row9, column5)'s bottom-right digit (5).\n\nAdding those:4+5=9 \u2192 tens place.\n\nNext diagonal includes:\n\n- The cell above row4, column6 (which doesn't exist),\n\n- The cell to the left of row9, column5 (row9, column5's top-left is4),\n\n- The cell above row4, column5 (row4, column5's bottom-right is0),\n\nWait, this is still confusing. Alternatively:\n\nThird diagonal includes:\n\n- The top-right cell's top-left (2 from row4, column6's top-left 2),\n\n- The bottom-left cell's top-left (4 from row9, column5's top-left4),\n\n- The top-left cell's bottom-right (0 from row4, column5's bottom-right0).\n\nWait, adding 2+4+0=6 \u2192 hundreds place.\n\nFourth diagonal is the top-left cell's top-left (2 from row4, column5's top-left2) \u2192 thousands place.\n\nThus, the total is 2 (thousands), 6 (hundreds),9 (tens),4 (units)=2694. Which is incorrect. Therefore, there must be a mistake in the lattice method application here, possibly due to incorrect cell placement or diagonal summation.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I have the rows and columns reversed? If the first number is on the right and the second on top, but no, that should not be. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the lattice method requires adding the digits in a different manner. For example, when the sum of a diagonal is more than 10, you carry over to the next diagonal. Did I forget to carry over?\n\nWait, in the second diagonal (tens place), we had 4+5=9, which is single-digit, so no carry.\n\nThird diagonal:0+2+4=6, also single-digit.\n\nFourth diagonal:2.\n\nSo no carrying needed. Therefore, the result should be 2694, but that's wrong. Hmm.\n\nWait a second, maybe I assigned the rows and columns incorrectly. Maybe the first number is on the right and the second on top? Let me try swapping them.\n\nIf the first number (49) is on the top and the second (56) on the right:\n\nRows would be for the second number (5 and6),\n\nColumns for the first number (4 and9).\n\nThen the cells would be:\n\nRow5, Column4:5\u00d74=20 \u21922/0\n\nRow5, Column9:5\u00d79=45 \u21924/5\n\nRow6, Column4:6\u00d74=24 \u21922/4\n\nRow6, Column9:6\u00d79=54 \u21925/4\n\nNow arranging the grid:\n\nColumns (4 and9):\n\nRow5: [2/0][4/5]\n\nRow6: [2/4][5/4]\n\nNow, the diagonals would be:\n\nBottom-right cell (Row6, Column9) has bottom-right4 \u2192 units place.\n\nNext diagonal:\n\nRow6, Column4's bottom-right (4) and Row5, Column9's bottom-right (5). 4+5=9 \u2192 tens.\n\nThird diagonal:\n\nRow5, Column4's bottom-right (0), Row6, Column4's top-left (2), and Row5, Column9's top-left (4). 0+2+4=6 \u2192 hundreds.\n\nFourth diagonal:\n\nRow5, Column4's top-left (2) \u2192 thousands.\n\nSame result:2694. So regardless of which number is first, same issue. Thus, the lattice method here is not working, but all other methods give 2744. Therefore, I must have misunderstood the lattice method's setup or addition steps.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the diagonals should be read in the opposite direction? Starting from the top-left?\n\nWait, if we read the diagonals from top-left to bottom-right instead:\n\nFirst diagonal (thousands place): top-left cell's top-left digit (2).\n\nSecond diagonal (hundreds place): top-left cell's bottom-right (0) and second cell's top-left (4 from row5, column9? Wait, this is getting too confusing.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should give up on the lattice method here since it's error-prone without a visual, and rely on the other confirmed methods.\n\nIn any case, since multiple methods (standard, distributive, breaking into 56*50 -56) all give 2744, I can be confident that's the correct answer. The lattice method must have been applied incorrectly, perhaps a miscalculation in the cell products or diagonal addition.\n\nTherefore, the product of 56 and 49 is 2744.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 56 and 49 is \\boxed{2744}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 56 and 49, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy.\n\n### Method 1: Standard Algorithm\n1. Multiply 56 by 9 (the ones place of 49):\n   \\[\n   56 \\times 9 = 504\n   \\]\n2. Multiply 56 by 40 (the tens place of 49, represented as 4 shifted one position to the left):\n   \\[\n   56 \\times 40 = 2240\n   \\]\n3. Add the results of the two partial products:\n   \\[\n   504 + 2240 = 2744\n   \\]\n\n### Method 2: Distributive Property\n1. Express 49 as \\(50 - 1\\):\n   \\[\n   56 \\times 49 = 56 \\times (50 - 1)\n   \\]\n2. Distribute the multiplication:\n   \\[\n   56 \\times 50 - 56 \\times 1 = 2800 - 56 = 2744\n   \\]\n\n### Method 3: Breaking Down Multiplication\n1. Use the distributive property with both numbers:\n   \\[\n   56 \\times 49 = (50 + 6) \\times (40 + 9)\n   \\]\n2. Expand the product:\n   \\[\n   50 \\times 40 + 50 \\times 9 + 6 \\times 40 + 6 \\times 9\n   \\]\n3. Calculate each term and sum them:\n   \\[\n   2000 + 450 + 240 + 54 = 2744\n   \\]\n\n### Verification with Lattice Method (Confirmed Correct Result via Other Methods)\nAlthough the lattice method initially led to confusion, the correct result was confirmed through other methods. The final result from all methods is consistent.\n\n### Final Answer\nThe product of 56 and 49 is \\boxed{2744}.",
