final_output:  ['\n\nTo find the product of 89 and 75, we can use the distributive property by breaking down 75 into 70 and 5:\n\n\\[\n89 \\times 75 = 89 \\times (70 + 5)\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate \\(89 \\times 70\\):\n\\[\n89 \\times 70 = 89 \\times 7 \\times 10 = 623 \\times 10 = 6230\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate \\(89 \\times 5\\):\n\\[\n89 \\times 5 = (80 \\times 5) + (9 \\times 5) = 400 + 45 = 445\n\\]\n\nAdd the two results together:\n\\[\n6230 + 445 = 6675\n\\]\n\nWe can also verify this by considering \\(89\\) as \\(90 - 1\\):\n\\[\n(90 - 1) \\times 75 = 90 \\times 75 - 1 \\times 75 = 6750 - 75 = 6675\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 89 and 75 is \\(\\boxed{6675}\\).', "Okay, so I need to find the product of 89 and 75. Hmm, let me think. I remember multiplication is just repeated addition, but adding 89 seventy-five times sounds tedious. There must be a better way. Maybe breaking it down into smaller parts? Let me recall the distributive property. \n\nAlright, 89 times 75. Let me see if I can split either number into easier numbers. 75 is 70 plus 5, right? So maybe 89*(70+5). That would be 89*70 plus 89*5. That seems manageable. Let me calculate each part separately.\n\nFirst, 89 multiplied by 70. Hmm, 89*70. Well, 89*7 is 623, right? Because 90*7 is 630, minus 1*7 is 623. So then, 89*70 would be 623 times 10, which is 6230. Okay, that part makes sense.\n\nNow the second part: 89*5. Let me do that. 80*5 is 400, and 9*5 is 45. So adding those together, 400+45 is 445. So 89*5 is 445.\n\nNow, add the two results together: 6230 + 445. Let me add those. 6230 plus 400 is 6630, and then plus 45 more would be 6675. So is the total 6675? Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nWait, let me verify the first part again. 89 times 70: 89*7 is 623, so times 10 is 6230. That seems right. Then 89*5: 445. Adding them gives 6230 + 445. 6230 + 400 is 6630, then 6630 +45 is indeed 6675. Okay, that seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another way to check. Maybe using another method like the standard multiplication algorithm. Let me try that.\n\nMultiplying 89 by 75. Let's write it down vertically:\n\n\u2003\xa089  \n×\xa075  \n------  \nFirst multiply 89 by 5:  \n89*5 = 445 (as before). \n\nThen multiply 89 by 70 (since the 7 is in the tens place). So 89*70 = 6230 (same as before). \n\nAdding those two gives 445 + 6230 = 6675. Yep, same result. So that checks out.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use another method like the difference of squares or something? Hmm, not sure if that applies here. Let me think. 89 and 75... Not sure. Alternatively, maybe approximate to check? Let's see.\n\n89 is close to 90, and 75 is exact. 90*75 would be 6750. But since we used 89 instead of 90, that's 1 less. So 6750 - 75 = 6675. Oh, that works too! Because 89 is 90-1, so (90-1)*75 = 90*75 - 1*75 = 6750 -75 = 6675. That confirms it again. So that's another way to see it.\n\nHmm, so all methods point to 6675. I think that's the right answer. Let me just do a quick calculator check in my mind. Wait, 75 times 90 is 6750, subtract 75 gives 6675. Yep, that's straightforward. So I feel confident that the product of 89 and 75 is 6675.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 89 and 75 is \\boxed{6675}.\n"]
