      "text": ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 57 and 72. Hmm, let me think. I remember multiplication is just repeated addition, but doing 57 added 72 times sounds tedious. Maybe there's a smarter way. Let me recall some multiplication strategies.\n\nFirst, maybe breaking down the numbers into smaller parts. Like, 57 can be 50 + 7, and 72 can be 70 + 2. Then use the distributive property. So (50 + 7)*(70 + 2). That should work. Let me write that out.\n\nSo expanding the brackets: 50*70 + 50*2 + 7*70 + 7*2. Let me compute each part step by step. \n\nFirst, 50 times 70. Well, 50 times 70 is like 5*7 with two zeros added, right? 5*7 is 35, so 3500. Okay, that's straightforward.\n\nNext term: 50*2. That's easy, 100.\n\nThird term: 7*70. Hmm, 7 times 7 is 49, so 7 times 70 is 490.\n\nLast term: 7*2. That's 14.\n\nNow add all those together: 3500 + 100 + 490 + 14. Let's add them step by step.\n\nStarting with 3500 + 100. That gives 3600.\n\nThen 3600 + 490. Hmm, 3600 + 400 is 4000, and then +90 would be 4090.\n\nThen add the last term, 14. 4090 + 14 is 4104.\n\nWait, let me check that again. Because 3500 + 100 is 3600. Then 3600 + 490. Let me do that more carefully: 3600 + 490. 3600 + 400 is 4000, then +90 is 4090. Then 4090 +14 is 4104. Okay that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method to verify. Let me try the standard multiplication algorithm.\n\nMultiplying 57 by 72. Let's write it as:\n\n\u2003\u200357  \n\u00d7\u00a072  \n\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014  \nFirst, multiply 57 by 2 (the units digit of 72). 57*2 is 114. Then multiply 57 by 70 (the tens digit, so 70). 57*70. Let's see, 57*7 is 399, so times 10 is 3990. Then add the two results: 114 + 3990. That gives 4104. Yep, same answer. Good, that matches.\n\nWait another thought: Maybe using some other trick, like 57*72. Since 57 is close to 60, and 72 is close to 70. Maybe adjust from there? Let me see.\n\nIf I take 60*70, that's 4200. But 57 is 3 less than 60, so subtract 3*70=210. So 4200 - 210 = 3990. But then also, 72 is 2 more than 70, so maybe I need to adjust for that? Wait, maybe this approach is getting confusing. Let me see.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps using (60 - 3)*(70 + 2). Expanding that would be 60*70 + 60*2 - 3*70 - 3*2. Let's compute each term:\n\n60*70 is 4200,\n\n60*2 is 120,\n\n-3*70 is -210,\n\n-3*2 is -6.\n\nAdding those up: 4200 + 120 = 4320,\n\n4320 - 210 = 4110,\n\n4110 - 6 = 4104. Hmm, same result again. So that works too. Okay, so that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the difference of squares or something? Let me see. Not sure if that applies here, but let me think. 57 and 72. Hmm, maybe not straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, maybe 57 *72 = (50+7)(70+2). Wait, that's the same as first method. Anyway, since all methods are giving me 4104, I think that's the right answer. Let me just confirm with a calculator in my mind. Wait, 57 times 70 is 3990, plus 57 times 2 is 114, so total is 3990 + 114. 3990 + 100 is 4090, plus 14 is 4104. Yep, that checks out.\n\nI don't see any mistakes in my calculations. So I think the product is 4104.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 57 and 72 is \\boxed{4104}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 57 and 72, we can use the distributive property by breaking down the numbers into smaller parts:\n\n\\[\n(50 + 7)(70 + 2)\n\\]\n\nExpanding this using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n50 \\times 70 + 50 \\times 2 + 7 \\times 70 + 7 \\times 2\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term step by step:\n\n1. \\(50 \\times 70 = 3500\\)\n2. \\(50 \\times 2 = 100\\)\n3. \\(7 \\times 70 = 490\\)\n4. \\(7 \\times 2 = 14\\)\n\nAdding all these results together:\n\n\\[\n3500 + 100 + 490 + 14\n\\]\n\nFirst, add \\(3500 + 100\\):\n\n\\[\n3500 + 100 = 3600\n\\]\n\nNext, add \\(3600 + 490\\):\n\n\\[\n3600 + 490 = 4090\n\\]\n\nFinally, add \\(4090 + 14\\):\n\n\\[\n4090 + 14 = 4104\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   57 \\\\\n\\times 72 \\\\\n\\hline\n  114 \\quad (\\text{57} \\times 2) \\\\\n+3990 \\quad (\\text{57} \\times 70) \\\\\n\\hline\n  4104 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 57 and 72 is \\(\\boxed{4104}\\).",
