eems right. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using a calculator? Wait, since I don't have a calculator here, but let me do another verification. Let's see, 60 times 90 is 5400, and 60 times 2 is 120, so adding them gives 5520. Alternatively, 92 times 60 is the same as 60 times 92, so commutative property holds. \n\nAlternatively, breaking down 60 into 50 +10. So (50 +10)*92 = 50*92 + 10*92. Let's see what that gives. 50*92: 50*90 is 4500, 50*2 is 100, so 4500+100 = 4600. Then 10*92 is 920. Adding those together: 4600 + 920. Let's see, 4600 + 900 is 5500, plus 20 is 5520. Again the same answer. \n\nHmm, so all methods lead to 5520. I think that's solid. So the product of 60 and 92 is 5520. \n\nWait, just to ensure that I didn't make any addition mistakes earlier. Let me check the first method again:\n\n60*90 = 5400. Correct. 60*2 = 120. Correct. 5400 + 120: 5400 + 100 is 5500, plus 20 is 5520. Yep, that's right. \n\nAlternatively, if I do 92*60 step by step:\n\nMultiply 92 by 6, then add a zero. 92*6: 90*6=540, 2*6=12, so 540+12=552. Then add a zero: 5520. \n\nAnother way: 60*92. Let's think in terms of hundreds. 60*90 is 5400, plus 60*2 is 120, totaling 5520. \n\nAlternatively, maybe use the distributive property in another way: 60*(90 +2) = 60*90 +60*2. Which is exactly what I did. \n\nAlternatively, maybe approximate to check. 60*90 is 5400, so 60*92 should be 5400 + 60*2 = 5520. \n\nAlternatively, if I think of 92 as 100 - 8, then 60*(100 -8) = 6000 - 480 = 5520. Let me see: 60*100 is 6000, 60*8 is 480, so subtracting gives 6000 - 480 = 5520. That works too. \n\nSo all different methods are confirming the same answer. I think that's thorough. I can't see any mistake here, so I think the product is indeed 5520.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 60 and 92 is \\boxed{5520}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 60 and 92, we can use the distributive property and break down the numbers for easier calculation:\n\n1. Break down 92 into 90 and 2:\n   \\[\n   60 \\times 92 = 60 \\times (90 + 2)\n   \\]\n2. Apply the distributive property:\n   \\[\n   60 \\times 90 + 60 \\times 2\n   \\]\n3. Calculate each part separately:\n   - \\(60 \\times 90\\):\n     \\[\n     60 \\times 90 = 5400\n     \\]\n   - \\(60 \\times 2\\):\n     \\[\n     60 \\times 2 = 120\n     \\]\n4. Add the results of the two parts:\n   \\[\n   5400 + 120 = 5520\n   \\]\n\nVerification using another method:\n- Multiply 92 by 6 and then by 10:\n  \\[\n  92 \\times 6 = 552 \\quad \\text{then} \\quad 552 \\times 10 = 5520\n  \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 60 and 92 is \\(\\boxed{5520}\\).",
