      "text": ":\nOkay, let's see. The problem is asking for the product of 26 and 20. Hmm, product means multiplication, right? So I need to multiply 26 by 20. Alright, how do I do that?\n\nWell, maybe I can break it down. 26 times 20. I remember that multiplying by 20 is the same as multiplying by 2 and then by 10. So first, 26 times 2. Let me calculate that. 26 times 2 is 52. Then, multiply that by 10. So 52 times 10 is 520. That seems right.\n\nWait, let me check another way to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm. \n\nWriting it out:\n\n\u2003\u00a026  \n\u00d7\u00a020  \n------  \nFirst, multiply 26 by 0, which is 0. Then multiply 26 by 2 (but since it's the tens place, it's actually 20). So 26 times 2 is 52, and then add a zero at the end because of the tens place. That gives 520. Yep, same answer as before. \n\nAlternatively, I can think of 20 as 20 ones. So adding 26 twenty times. But that's tedious. But maybe if I do 26 times 10 is 260, then double it to get 20 times, so 260 times 2 is 520. Yep, that works too.\n\nHmm, all methods are giving me 520. I think that's the correct answer. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. Let me just verify with a calculator in my mind. 26 multiplied by 20. Since 20 is two tens, 26 times two is 52, then add a zero, so 520. Yep, that checks out.\n\nI guess that's it. The product of 26 and 20 is definitely 520.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 26 and 20 is calculated by multiplying the two numbers together:\n\n\\[\n26 \\times 20 = 520\n\\]\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n1. **Break down 20 into 2 \u00d7 10:**\n   \\[\n   26 \\times 20 = 26 \\times (2 \\times 10)\n   \\]\n2. **Multiply 26 by 2:**\n   \\[\n   26 \\times 2 = 52\n   \\]\n3. **Multiply the result by 10:**\n   \\[\n   52 \\times 10 = 520\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer:** \\boxed{520}",
