o that's a good sign. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do 69*86 as (70 -1)*(80 +6) as I did, but another way is to use the standard algorithm again but check my steps again. Let me try writing it vertically again to see if I messed up the addition before.\n\nFirst line: 69 x 6 = 414. \n\nSecond line: 69 x 80 = 5520. \n\nAdding them: \n\n414  \n+5520  \n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500  \n5934. \n\nYes, that's correct. 414 plus 5520: 414 + 5000 is 5414, plus 520 is indeed 5934. So that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method, like the lattice method, just to confirm? Hmm, not sure, but let's see. \n\nWait, perhaps cross-multiplication? Let me think. \n\nAlternatively, breaking down both numbers into tens and units:\n\n69 = 60 +9,\n\n86 =80 +6,\n\nSo the product is (60 +9)(80 +6) = 60*80 +60*6 +9*80 +9*6.\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n60*80 = 4800,\n\n60*6 = 360,\n\n9*80 =720,\n\n9*6=54.\n\nAdding them up: 4800 + 360 = 5160; 5160 +720=5880; 5880 +54=5934. \n\nSame result again. Okay, so that's consistent. \n\nHmm, so all methods point to 5934. Maybe I was just confused earlier when adding, but now it's clear. \n\nWait, but let me do it step by step once more to be thorough. \n\nFirst, 69 times 86. Let me use another approach where I multiply 69 by 86 as 69*(85 +1) = 69*85 +69*1. \n\nBut 69*85 might not be easier, but let's try:\n\nAlternatively, maybe 69*86 = (70 -1)*86 =70*86 -86. \n\n70*86: 70*80=5600, 70*6=420, so total 5600+420=6020. Then subtract 86 gives 6020-86=5934. \n\nSame answer again. \n\nAlright, so I think that's solid. So the product of 69 and 86 is 5934. \n\nBut just to be absolutely sure, maybe I can do the multiplication step by step again:\n\nMultiplying 69 by 86:\n\nFirst, multiply 69 by 6 (units place):\n\n9*6=54, write down 4, carryover 5.\n\n6*6=36, plus 5 is 41. So first partial product is 414.\n\nNext, multiply 69 by 8 (which is actually 80, so we'll shift one position to the left):\n\n9*8=72, write down 2, carryover 7.\