again 30, so 3 and 0.\n\nThen 5*3=15, same as before.\n\nWait, this might be confusing. Let me try to lay it out step by step.\n\nWait, maybe I should recall that in lattice multiplication, each digit of the first number is on the top, and each digit of the second is on the right. So for 55 x63:\n\nTop: 5 (hundreds?), wait no. Let me think again.\n\nActually, the first number is 55, so two digits, so two columns. The second number 63 is two digits, so two rows. The grid is 2x2.\n\nEach cell is split by a diagonal.\n\nStarting with the first digit of the top number (5) and first digit of the right number (6):\n\nMultiply 5*6=30. So put 3 in the upper triangle and 0 in the lower triangle of that cell.\n\nNext cell in the same row (top digit 5 and right digit 3):\n\n5*3=15, so 1 upper, 5 lower.\n\nSecond row (top digit 5 and right digit 6 again? Wait no, second row corresponds to the second digit of the right number. Wait, perhaps I need to clarify.\n\nWait, actually, the lattice is set up with the first number's digits across the top, and the second number's digits down the right side. Each cell at the intersection of a top digit and a right digit is where their product is placed.\n\nSo for 55 (top: 5 and 5) and 63 (right: 6 and 3). So the grid is:\n\nFirst row (top digit 5):\n\n- Column 1 (right digit 6): 5*6=30 \u2192 3 and 0\n\n- Column 2 (right digit 3): 5*3=15 \u21921 and5\n\nSecond row (top digit 5):\n\n- Column 1 (right digit 6):5*6=30 \u21923 and0\n\n- Column 2 (right digit3):5*3=15 \u21921 and5\n\nThen, after filling all cells with the products split into tens and units, you add along the diagonals from right to left.\n\nThe grid would look like this (each cell is upper\\lower):\n\nFirst row (top 5):\n\nFirst cell (5*6): 3\\0\n\nSecond cell (5*3):1\\5\n\nSecond row (top 5):\n\nFirst cell (5*6):3\\0\n\nSecond cell (5*3):1\\5\n\nNow, the diagonals:\n\nStarting from the bottom right, the first diagonal (units place) is the lower part of the second cell in the se