t.\n\n55*60: 55*6=330, so times 10 is 3300.\n\n55*3: 50*3=150 and 5*3=15, so 150+15=165.\n\nAdding them together: 3300 + 165 = 3465. Again, same answer. \n\nHmm, seems like all methods are pointing to 3465. So that must be the correct product. \n\nWait, but just to be thorough, maybe I can do it another way. Let me try the lattice method. Although I might need to visualize it. \n\nThe lattice method involves creating a grid. Since both numbers are two digits, it would be a 2x2 grid. \n\nFirst, write 55 on top and 63 on the right side. \n\nSplit each cell into diagonals. \n\nMultiply 5 (from 55) by 6 (from 63): 30. So write 3 above the diagonal and 0 below.\n\nThen 5 (top right) times 3 (bottom left): 15. Write 1 above and 5 below.\n\nNext row: 5 (second digit of 55) times 6 (first digit of 63): again 30, so 3 and 0.\n\nThen 5*3=15, same as before.\n\nWait, this might be confusing. Let me try to lay it out step by step.\n\nWait, maybe I should recall that in lattice multiplication, each digit of the first number is on the top, and each digit of the second is on the right. So for 55 x63:\n\nTop: 5 (hundreds?), wait no. Let me think again.\n\nActually, the first number is 55, so two digits, so two columns. The second number 63 is two digits, so two rows. The grid is 2x2.\n\nEach cell is split by a diagonal.\n\nStarting with the first digit of the top number (5) and first digit of the right number (6):\n\nMultiply 5*6=30. So put 3 in the upper triangle and 0 in the lower triangle of that cell.\n\nNext cell in the same row (top digit 5 and right digit 3):\n\n5*3=15, so 1 upper, 5 lower.\n\nSecond row (top digit 5 and right digit 6 again? Wait no, second row corresponds to the second digit of the right number. Wait, perhaps I need to clarify.\n\nWait, actually, the lattice is set up with the first number's digits across the top, and the second number's digits down the right side. Each cell at the intersection of a top digit and a right digit is where their product is plac