      "text": ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 97 and 34. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers, right? 97 times 34. I guess the straightforward way is to do it the standard multiplication method. Let me recall how that works.\n\nFirst, I can write it down vertically:\n\n\u2003\u00a097\n\u00d7\u00a034\n------\n\u00a0\n\nStarting with the ones place of the bottom number. Wait, actually, in vertical multiplication, you usually take the top number and multiply by each digit of the bottom number, starting from the right. Since 34 is the second number, I should break it down into 30 and 4. So maybe 97 times 30 plus 97 times 4? That might be easier.\n\nLet me try that approach first. Let's compute 97 \u00d7 30. Well, 97 times 10 is 970, so 30 is three times that. So 970 \u00d7 3. Let me calculate that. 900 \u00d7 3 is 2700, and 70 \u00d7 3 is 210. Adding those together gives 2700 + 210 = 2910. So 97 \u00d7 30 is 2910.\n\nNow, 97 \u00d7 4. Let me do that. 90 \u00d7 4 is 360, and 7 \u00d7 4 is 28. Adding them gives 360 + 28 = 388. So 97 \u00d7 4 is 388.\n\nNow, adding the two results together: 2910 + 388. Let me add 2910 + 300 first, which is 3210, then add the remaining 88. 3210 + 88 = 3298. Wait, let me check that again. 2910 plus 388. Breaking it down: 2910 + 300 is 3210, then 3210 + 88. 3210 + 80 is 3290, plus 8 is 3298. Okay, that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do the multiplication the standard vertical way to confirm:\n\n\u2003\u200397\n\u00d7\u00a034\n------\nFirst multiply 97 by 4: 97\u00d74=388 (as above). Then multiply 97 by 30 (since the 3 is in the tens place, so 3\u00d710). So that's 97\u00d73=291, then add a zero to make it 2910. Then add 2910 and 388, which is 3298. Same result.\n\nWait, just to make sure, maybe another method? Like using distributive property differently. Maybe (100 - 3) \u00d7 34. Since 97 is 100 minus 3. Let's see:\n\n(100 - 3) \u00d7 34 = 100\u00d734 - 3\u00d734 = 3400 - 102. \n\nCalculating 3400 - 102: 3400 - 100 is 3300, then subtract 2 more gives 3298. Oh, that works too. That might be a quicker way actually. \n\nHmm, so all methods are giving me 3298. Let me just check with a calculator in my mind. 97 times 30 is 2910, plus 97 times 4 is 388, totaling 3298. Yeah, that seems correct. Alternatively, 34 times 100 is 3400, minus 34 times 3 is 102, so 3400-102=3298. Yep. \n\nAlternatively, maybe breaking down 34 into 30 + 4 and doing 97\u00d730 + 97\u00d74 as I did before. Either way, all methods lead to the same answer. So I think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. \n\nWait, let me just verify the 97\u00d74 again. 90\u00d74=360, 7\u00d74=28, so 360+28=388. Correct. 97\u00d730: 97\u00d73=291, so times 10 is 2910. Adding 2910 + 388. Let's see, 2910 + 388. 2910 + 300 is 3210, then 3210 +88 is 3298. Correct. \n\nAlternatively, adding 2910 + 388: \n\n2910  \n+\u00a0388  \n------  \nStarting from the right: 0 +8=8, 1+8=9, 9+3=12, 2 +0=2. Wait, actually writing it out:\n\n\u20032910  \n+\u00a0\u00a0388  \n=------  \nStarting from the units digit:\n\n0 + 8 = 8  \n1 (tens place) + 8 (tens) = 9 tens.  \n9 (hundreds place) + 3 (hundreds) = 12 hundreds. So write 2 and carry over 1.  \n2 (thousands place) + the carried over 1 = 3.  \nSo total is 3298. Yep, that works. \n\nAnother way: 97 \u00d734. Let me do 97\u00d734 as (90+7)\u00d7(30+4). Using the distributive property again. \n\nSo expanding that: 90\u00d730 + 90\u00d74 + 7\u00d730 + 7\u00d74.\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n90\u00d730 = 2700  \n90\u00d74 = 360  \n7\u00d730 = 210  \n7\u00d74 = 28  \n\nAdding all these together: 2700 + 360 = 3060; 210 +28 = 238. Then 3060 +238 = 3298. \n\nYep, same result. \n\nI think that's thorough. All different methods confirm that the product is 3298. I can't see any errors in my calculations. So I'm confident that's the right answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 97 and 34 is \\boxed{3298}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 97 and 34, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy:\n\n1. **Breaking down into smaller parts:**\n   - Calculate \\(97 \\times 30\\):\n     \\[\n     97 \\times 30 = 97 \\times 10 \\times 3 = 970 \\times 3 = 2910\n     \\]\n   - Calculate \\(97 \\times 4\\):\n     \\[\n     97 \\times 4 = (90 \\times 4) + (7 \\times 4) = 360 + 28 = 388\n     \\]\n   - Add the results:\n     \\[\n     2910 + 388 = 2910 + 300 + 88 = 3210 + 88 = 3298\n     \\]\n\n2. **Using the distributive property with \\(97 = 100 - 3\\):**\n   - Calculate \\((100 - 3) \\times 34\\):\n     \\[\n     100 \\times 34 - 3 \\times 34 = 3400 - 102 = 3298\n     \\]\n\n3. **Expanding using distributive property:**\n   - Break down \\(97 \\times 34\\) as \\((90 + 7) \\times (30 + 4)\\):\n     \\[\n     90 \\times 30 + 90 \\times 4 + 7 \\times 30 + 7 \\times 4\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     = 2700 + 360 + 210 + 28 = 2700 + 360 = 3060; \\quad 210 + 28 = 238\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     3060 + 238 = 3298\n     \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 97 and 34 is \\(\\boxed{3298}\\).",
