final_output:  ['\n\nThe product of 22 and 36 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Breakdown using distributive property:**\n   - \\(22 \\times 36 = 22 \\times (30 + 6)\\)\n   - \\(= (22 \\times 30) + (22 \\times 6)\\)\n   - \\(= 660 + 132\\)\n   - \\(= 792\\)\n\n2. **Standard multiplication:**\n   ```\n      22\n    × 36\n    -----\n      132  (22 × 6)\n     660   (22 × 30, shifted left)\n    -----\n     792\n   ```\n\n3. **Alternative verification:**\n   - \\(22 \\times 36 = (20 + 2) \\times 36 = 720 + 72 = 792\\)\n   - \\(22 \\times 36 = 11 \\times (2 \\times 36) = 11 \\times 72 = 792\\)\n\nAll methods confirm the result is **792**.\n\n**Answer:** The product of 22 and 36 is \\boxed{792}.', ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 22 and 36. Hmm, let's see. Product means multiplication, right? So I just have to multiply 22 by 36. Alright, how do I do that?\n\nFirst, maybe I should break it down. Multiplying 22 by 36 might be easier if I split 36 into smaller parts. Like, 30 and 6. So, 22 times 30 plus 22 times 6. That should work because of the distributive property. Let me try that.\n\n22 times 30... Well, 22 times 10 is 220, so 22 times 30 would be three times that. 220 times 3. Let me calculate that. 200 times 3 is 600, and 20 times 3 is 60, so 600 + 60 = 660. So 22 times 30 is 660.\n\nNow, 22 times 6. That's easier. 20 times 6 is 120, and 2 times 6 is 12. Adding those together gives 120 + 12 = 132. So 22 times 6 is 132.\n\nNow, add the two results together: 660 + 132. Let me add 660 and 100 first, which is 760, then add the remaining 32. 760 + 32 = 792. So the total is 792.\n\nWait, let me check that again to be sure. Maybe I made a mistake in addition. 660 plus 132. Breaking it down: 600 + 100 = 700, 60 + 32 = 92. So 700 + 92 = 792. Yeah, that's the same. Okay.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do it another way to confirm. Let's try the standard multiplication algorithm.\n\nMultiplying 22 by 36:\n\nFirst, write 22  \n           x36  \n       --------\n           132  (which is 22 x 6)  \n          660   (which is 22 x 30, shifted one position to the left)  \n       --------\n          792  \n\nAdding 132 and 660 gives 792. That matches my previous result. So that seems right.\n\nAnother way: Maybe using the distributive property differently. 22 can be 20 + 2. So (20 + 2) * 36 = 20*36 + 2*36. Let's see:\n\n20*36: 20 times 30 is 600, 20 times 6 is 120, so total is 720.  \n2*36 is 72.  \nAdding them together: 720 +72 = 792. Yep, same answer.\n\nHmm, all methods lead to 792. Maybe that's correct. But let me try another approach just to be thorough.\n\nAlternatively, 36 is 4*9, so 22*4*9. 22*4 is 88, then 88*9. Let's compute 88*9. 80*9=720, 8*9=72, so 720+72=792. Same result again.\n\nAlternatively, 22 is 11*2, so 11*2*36. 2*36 is 72, then 11*72. 11*70 is 770, 11*2 is 22, so 770+22=792. Yep, still the same.\n\nOkay, so all different methods give me 792. I think that's the right answer. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. Maybe I can use a calculator if I have one, but since I'm doing this manually, and all methods cross-check, I'm confident it's 792.\n\nWait, just to make sure, maybe I'll do the multiplication vertically again step by step.\n\n       22  \n     x36  \n     -----  \n       132  (22 x 6)  \n      660   (22 x 30, since the 3 is in the tens place, so it's actually 22 x 30)  \n     -----  \n      792  \n\nYes, adding 132 and 660 gives 792. So that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, if I reverse the numbers: 36 times 22. Let's see if that works the same.\n\n36  \nx22  \n-----  \n 72   (36 x 2)  \n720   (36 x 20, shifted left)  \n-----  \n792  \n\nSame result. Perfect, commutative property holds here. So definitely, the product is 792.\n\nI think that's solid. I can't think of another way to verify, but all the methods I've tried confirm the same answer. So I think that's it. The product of 22 and 36 is 792.\n"] 24%|██▍       | 119/500 [2:15:20<5:40:14, 53.58s/it]
