e down 3 and carry over 1. Then 2 plus 8 plus the carried 1 is 11, so write 1 and carry over 1. Finally, the 2 in the thousands place plus the carried 1 makes 3. So that gives 3132. \n\nBut let me check that again to make sure I didn't make any mistakes. Maybe there's another way to calculate this. For instance, using the distributive property: 36 times 87 is the same as 36*(80 +7) which is 36*80 + 36*7. \n\nCalculating 36*80 first. 36*8 is 288, so 36*80 is 2880. Then 36*7: 30*7 is 210, and 6*7 is 42, so 210 +42 is 252. Adding those together again, 2880 +252 is indeed 3132. Okay, that matches the first method. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do it another way to confirm. Let me try breaking down both numbers into smaller parts. 36 is 30 +6, and 87 is 80 +7. So using the distributive property again:\n\n(30+6)*(80+7) = 30*80 + 30*7 +6*80 +6*7.\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n30*80 = 2400,\n\n30*7 = 210,\n\n6*80 = 480,\n\n6*7 = 42.\n\nAdding all those together: 2400 +210 is 2610, plus 480 is 3090, plus 42 is 3132. Yep, same result. \n\nHmm, so all three methods give me 3132. That seems solid. Maybe I can use a calculator to verify, but since I don't have one here, cross-checking with different methods is the way to go. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the standard algorithm again but more carefully. Let me write it out once more:\n\n\u2003\u2003\xa036  \n×\xa087  \n──────  \n\u2003252 (which is 36×7)  \n+2880 (which is 36×80)  \n──────  \n\u20033132\n\nYes, that's consistent. I think I can be confident that 36 multiplied by 87 is 3132. \n\nWait a second, just to make sure I didn't transpose any numbers. Let me see: 3 times 8 is 24, so the first digits would be in the thousands place. Since 30*80 is 2400, that's 24 hundred. Then adding the rest should get us over 3000. 2400 + 700 (from 30*7 and 6*80?) Hmm, maybe not, but in any case, the total adds up. \n\nAlternatively, maybe approximate to check. 36 is close to 40, and 87 is close to 90. 40*90 is 3600. Since both numbers are a bit less, the actual product should be less than 3600. 3132 is indeed less, so that makes sense. If I had gotten something over 3600, I would know it's wrong. \n\nAlternatively, 36*87: Let me think of 36*90, which would be 3240, then subtract 36*3 (since 87 is 90-3). 36*3 is 108. So 3240 -108 is 3132. That's another method, and it still gives the same answer. \n\nOkay, so using multiple methods: standard algorithm, distributive property breaking down each number, and adjusting from a known multiple (36*90), all lead to 3132. I think that's correct. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, so I'll go with that.\n"] 24%|██▍       | 120/500 [2:17:03<7:11:54, 68.20s/it]
