first partial product is 522.\n\nThen, moving to the 7 (tens place, so it's actually 70). So we need to shift one position to the left:\n\nStart by writing a 0 in the units place.\n\n7 \u00d7 8 = 56. Write 6, carryover 5.\n\n7 \u00d7 5 = 35 + 5 = 40. So the second partial product is 4060.\n\nAdding 522 and 4060:\n\n522  \n+4060  \n------  \n4582\n\nYes, that adds up to 4582. Hmm, so that's the same as before. Maybe that's correct then.\n\nAlternatively, another way to check is using the complement method or another multiplication trick. Let's see, perhaps using the fact that 58 \u00d7 79 = (60 - 2)(80 - 1). Let me try expanding that.\n\n(60 - 2)(80 - 1) = 60\u00d780 - 60\u00d71 - 2\u00d780 + 2\u00d71\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n60\u00d780 = 4800\n\n60\u00d71 = 60\n\n2\u00d780 = 160\n\n2\u00d71 = 2\n\nSo the total is 4800 - 60 - 160 + 2.\n\nCompute step by step:\n\n4800 - 60 = 4740\n\n4740 - 160 = 4580\n\n4580 + 2 = 4582\n\nAgain, same answer. Okay, so that seems to confirm it again. Maybe 4582 is the correct answer. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can do 58 \u00d7 80 first and then subtract 58, since 79 is 80 -1.\n\nSo, 58 \u00d7 80 = (58 \u00d7 8) \u00d7 10\n\n58 \u00d78: 50\u00d78=400, 8\u00d78=64, so 400+64=464. Then \u00d710 is 4640.\n\nThen subtract 58: 4640 -58 = 4582. Yep, that works too.\n\nAlright, so multiple methods are giving me 4582, so I think that must be the correct product. \n\nWait, but let me just do straight multiplication once again step by step to be absolutely sure.\n\nMultiplying 58 by 79:\n\nFirst, 58 \u00d7 9:\n\n9 \u00d7 8 = 72, write 2, carryover 7.\n\n9 \u00d75 =45 +7=52. So first partial product is 522.\n\nSecond, 58 \u00d770:\n\nSince it's the tens place, so 7\u00d710, so we can compute 58\u00d77=406, then add a zero to get 4060.\n\nAdding 522 +4060:\n\n522 + 4060:\n\nAdding the thousands: 4000 + 500 =4500\n\nAdding the hundreds: 0 + 20 =20 (Wait, perhaps better to do digit by digit.)\n\nWait, 4060 + 522:\n\n4060 + 500 is 4560,\n\n4560 +