      "text": ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 36 and 22. Hmm, let me think. Multiplication, right? But maybe I should break it down to make sure I don't make a mistake. Let's see, 36 times 22. \n\nFirst, I remember that multiplying by 22 is the same as multiplying by 20 plus 2. So maybe I can use the distributive property here. That would be 36 times 20 plus 36 times 2. Let me write that out. \n\n36 multiplied by 20... Well, 36 times 2 is 72, so if I add a zero at the end, that would be 720. Yeah, that makes sense because 20 is 2 times 10, so multiplying by 20 is just multiplying by 2 and then by 10. So 36*2=72, then 72*10=720. \n\nThen, 36 times 2 is straightforward. 36*2 is 72. \n\nNow, adding those two results together: 720 plus 72. Let me add those. 700 plus 70 is 770, and 20 plus 2 is 22, so total would be 770 + 22 = 792? Wait, no, that doesn't sound right. Wait, actually, 720 + 72 is like adding 720 + 70 first, which is 790, and then +2 more would be 792. Yeah, that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using another method. Let me try the standard multiplication algorithm. \n\n       36\n     x22\n     -----\nFirst multiply 36 by 2 (the units place of 22). 36*2=72. Then multiply 36 by 20 (the tens place, so it's actually 20). So 36*20=720. Then add them together: 72 + 720 = 792. That matches what I got before. \n\nWait, another way: Maybe using 36*22 as (30+6)*(20+2). Expanding that would be 30*20 + 30*2 + 6*20 + 6*2. Let's calculate each term:\n\n30*20 = 600,\n\n30*2 = 60,\n\n6*20 = 120,\n\n6*2 = 12.\n\nAdding all those up: 600 + 60 is 660, plus 120 is 780, plus 12 is 792. Yep, same result. \n\nHmm, seems like all methods are pointing to 792. I think that's the right answer. Let me just confirm with a calculator in my mind. 36 times 20 is 720, plus another 36 times 2 is 72, so 720+72 is indeed 792. Okay, I feel confident now. \n\nWait, but just to make sure, maybe do it another way. Let's think of 22 as 11*2. So 36*11 is 396, and then multiply by 2 gives 792. That works too. Because 36*11 is 396 (since 36*10 is 360 plus 36 is 396), then times 2 is 792. \n\nAnother angle: Maybe using the fact that 36 is 30 + 6. So 30*22 is 660, and 6*22 is 132. Then 660 + 132 is 792. Yep, that also works. \n\nAlright, so all different methods give me the same answer, so I think it's safe to say the product of 36 and 22 is 792. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. I considered multiple approaches to cross-verify, and they all align. So, yeah, that's the answer.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 36 and 22 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Using the distributive property:**\n   \\[\n   36 \\times 22 = 36 \\times (20 + 2) = (36 \\times 20) + (36 \\times 2)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 720 + 72 = 792\n   \\]\n\n2. **Standard multiplication algorithm:**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     \\quad 36 \\\\\n   \\times 22 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     \\quad 72 \\quad (\\text{36 \u00d7 2}) \\\\\n   + 720 \\quad (\\text{36 \u00d7 20, shifted left by one digit}) \\\\\n   \\hline\n     792 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Breaking into smaller terms:**\n   \\[\n   (30 + 6) \\times (20 + 2) = 30 \\times 20 + 30 \\times 2 + 6 \\times 20 + 6 \\times 2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 600 + 60 + 120 + 12 = 792\n   \\]\n\n4. **Alternative method (via 11\u00d72):**\n   \\[\n   36 \\times 22 = 36 \\times (11 \\times 2) = (36 \\times 11) \\times 2 = 396 \\times 2 = 792\n   \\]\n\nAll methods confirm the result is consistent. \n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{792}",
      "logprobs": null,
      "finish_reason": "stop",
      "stop_reason": null,
