er again. Okay, so all methods are pointing to 3366. I think that's correct. Let me just do a quick estimation to check. 50 times 60 is 3000, and 50 times 66 would be 3300, so 51 is a bit more, so 3366 makes sense. \n\nAlternatively, 66 times 50 is 3300, adding one more 66 gives 3366. Yep, that's straightforward. \n\nI think I'm confident that the product of 51 and 66 is 3366.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 51 and 66 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Breakdown using distributive property**:\n   \\[\n   51 \\times 66 = (50 + 1) \\times 66 = 50 \\times 66 + 1 \\times 66\n   \\]\n   \n2. **Calculate each term**:\n   - \\( 50 \\times 66 = 3300 \\)  \n   - \\( 1 \\times 66 = 66 \\)\n\n3. **Sum the results**:\n   \\[\n   3300 + 66 = 3366\n   \\]\n\n**Verification via standard multiplication**:\n```\n    51\n  \u00d7 66\n  -------\n    306   (51 \u00d7 6)\n  3060    (51 \u00d7 60, shifted one position left)\n  -------\n   3366\n```\n\n**Final Answer**:  \nThe product of 51 and 66 is \\(\\boxed{3366}\\).",
