final_output:  ['\n\nThe product of 99 and 31 can be calculated efficiently by recognizing that 99 is 100 minus 1. Using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n99 \\times 31 = (100 - 1) \\times 31 = 100 \\times 31 - 1 \\times 31\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n- \\(100 \\times 31 = 3100\\)\n- \\(1 \\times 31 = 31\\)\n\nSubtracting these results:\n\\[\n3100 - 31 = 3069\n\\]\n\nVerification using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n- \\(99 \\times 1 = 99\\)\n- \\(99 \\times 30 = 2970\\)\n- Adding the partial products: \\(99 + 2970 = 3069\\)\n\nBoth methods confirm the result. \n\n**Answer:** The product of 99 and 31 is \\(\\boxed{3069}\\).', ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 99 and 31. Hmm, let me think about the best way to do this. Multiplying 99 by 31 directly might be a bit tedious, but maybe there's a trick to make it easier. \n\nFirst, I remember that 99 is just one less than 100. Maybe I can use that to simplify the multiplication. So if I consider 99 as (100 - 1), then the problem becomes 31 multiplied by (100 - 1). That should be straightforward using the distributive property. Let me write that out:\n\n31 × (100 - 1) = 31 × 100 - 31 × 1.\n\nCalculating each part separately, 31 times 100 is easy—it's just 3100. Then, 31 times 1 is 31. Subtracting those gives me 3100 - 31. \n\nNow, subtracting 31 from 3100. Let me do that step by step. 3100 minus 30 would be 3070, and then minus 1 more is 3069. So that should be the result. \n\nWait, let me double-check to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Maybe I can verify by doing the multiplication the standard way. Let's see:\n\nMultiplying 99 by 31 using the standard algorithm:\n\nFirst, write 99  \n×31  \n________\n\nMultiply 99 by 1 (units place): 99  \nThen multiply 99 by 30 (tens place): 99 × 30 = 2970  \nAdd them together: 99 + 2970 = 3069. \n\nYep, that matches the previous result. Okay, so that seems correct. Another way to check could be adding 99 thirty-one times, but that would take too long. Alternatively, maybe breaking down 31 into smaller numbers? Let's see:\n\n31 can be 30 + 1, so 99 × (30 +1) = 99×30 + 99×1. \n\nCalculating 99×30: 99×10 is 990, so times 3 is 2970. Then adding 99 gives 2970 + 99 = 3069. Same answer again. \n\nAlright, so all methods are pointing to 3069. I think that's solid. Just to be thorough, maybe use another approach. Let's see, maybe using the difference of squares? Wait, not sure if that applies here. Alternatively, maybe 99×31 is the same as (100 -1)×31, which we already did. \n\nAlternatively, maybe convert to binary or something, but that's overcomplicating. Alternatively, approximate? 100×30 is 3000, but since it's 99×31, which is a bit less than 100×30, but actually 100×31 would be 3100, so subtracting 31 gives 3069. Yep, that's exactly the first method. \n\nAlternatively, maybe use a calculator? But since I'm doing this manually, I need to trust my arithmetic. Let me check the subtraction again: 3100 -31. 3100 minus 10 is 3090, minus another 21 would be 3090 -21 = 3069. Correct. \n\nAlternatively, adding 99 thirty-one times... Well, if I do 99×10=990, so 99×20 would be 1980, and 99×11 is 1089. Wait, 20+11=31. So 1980 +1089 = 3069. Yep, that works too. \n\nHmm, seems like all methods confirm the same result. I think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. So the product of 99 and 31 is 3069.\n"]
