 see. 51 times 73. Hmm, is there a way to write this as (a + b)(a - b) or something? Not sure. Alternatively, maybe express one of the numbers as a multiple. For example, 73 is 70 +3, so maybe not. Alternatively, 51 is 50 +1. \n\nAlternatively, maybe use the standard algorithm again but more carefully. Let me write it down step by step:\n\n       51\n     x73\n     ----\n       153  (this is 51 x 3)\n      3570  (this is 51 x 70, shifted one position to the left)\n     ----\n      3723\n\nYes, adding 153 and 3570 gives 3723. \n\nAlternatively, maybe check with a calculator? Wait, but since I can't use a calculator, perhaps do the multiplication in another order. 73 times 51. Let's see:\n\n   73\nx 51\n-----\n   73 (times 1)\n365  (times 50, shifted one position)\n-----\n3723\n\nWait, 73*1 is 73, and 73*50 is 3650. Adding them gives 3650 +73 = 3723. Yep, same result. \n\nI think that's consistent across all methods. So I feel confident that the product is 3723. Let me just do a quick estimation check. 50*70 is 3500, and 50*73 would be 3650, so 51*73 should be a bit more than that. 3723 is 73 more than 3650, which makes sense because 51 is one more than 50, so adding 1*73. So yes, that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, another way: 51*70 = 3570, then add 51*3=153, so total is 3570+153=3723. Yep. \n\nAlright, I think that's solid. All different methods cross-check to the same answer. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, so I think the product of 51 and 73 is 3723.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 51 and 73 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Breakdown using the standard multiplication algorithm:**\n   - Multiply 51 by 3:  \n     \\( 51 \\times 3 = 153 \\)\n   - Multiply 51 by 70 (since 7 is in the tens place):  \n     \\( 51 \\times 70 = 3570 \\)\n   - Add the results:  \n     \\( 153 + 3570 = 3723 \\)\n\n2. **Verification using the distributive property:**  \n   Expand \\( (50 + 1)(70 + 3) \\):  \n   \\[\n   50 \\times 70 + 50 \\times 3 + 1 \\times 70 + 1 \\times 3 = 3500 + 150 + 70 + 3 = 3723\n   \\]\n\n3. **Alternative method:**  \n   Rewrite as \\( (50 + 1) \\times 73 \\):  \n   \\[\n   50 \\times 73 + 1 \\times 73 = 3650 + 73 = 3723\n   \\]\n\nAll methods confirm the result is consistent. \n\n**Answer:** The product of 51 and 73 is \\boxed{3723}.",
