quared? Not sure if that\'s helpful here.\n\nAlternatively, maybe approximate to check. 60 times 95 is 5700, and 3 times 95 is 285, so total is 5700 +285=5985. Yep, that\'s the same as before. Okay, so that\'s another way.\n\nAlternatively, using calculator? But since I don\'t have a calculator here, but I can do manual calculation again. Let me try once more step by step.\n\n63 × 95. Let\'s think of 95 as 95.0. So, 63 multiplied by 95. Let me write it vertically again:\n\n       63\n     × 95\n     -----\nFirst step: 63 × 5 = 315\n\nSecond step: 63 × 90. Since 9 is in the tens place, so 63 ×9 = 567, then add a zero to get 5670.\n\nAdding 315 and 5670: 315 + 5670. Let\'s add 5670 + 300 first, which is 5970, then add 15 more, which is 5985. Yep, same result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the commutative property, switching the numbers: 95 × 63. Let\'s try that.\n\n95 × 60 is 5700, and 95 ×3 is 285. Adding them gives 5700 +285 = 5985. Same answer. Alright, so all methods are confirming that the product is 5985.\n\nI think that\'s solid. So the answer should be 5985. Wait, just to make sure I didn\'t make a mistake in any of my calculations, let me do 63 ×95 step by step once more.\n\nStarting with 63 × 95. Let\'s break down 95 into 90 +5. So:\n\n63 × 90 = ?\n\n63 ×9 = 567, so times 10 is 5670.\n\n63 ×5 = 315.\n\nAdding 5670 + 315:\n\n5670 + 300 = 5970,\n\n5970 +15 = 5985. Yep, same as before.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can use another method called the "cross" multiplication for two-digit numbers. Let me see. 63 ×95.\n\nMultiply the units digits first: 3×5=15. Write down 5 and carry over 1.\n\nThen, cross multiply the tens and units: (6×5) + (3×9) = 30 +27=57. Add the carried over 1 to get 58. Write down 8 and carry over 5.\n\nThen multiply the tens digits: 6×9=54. Add the carried over 5 to get 59. So putting it all together, you have 5985. Wait, that\'s interesting. Let me explain that step by step because that might be a quicker method.\n\nWait, actually, that\'s the