et me write it down vertically:\n\n\u2003\u200383  \n\u00d7\u00a058  \n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500  \nFirst, multiply 83 by 8 (the units digit of 58):\n\n83 \u00d7 8. 3\u00d78 is 24, write down 4 and carry over 2. Then 8\u00d78 is 64, plus the carried 2 is 66. So that gives 664.\n\nThen multiply 83 by 50 (the tens digit of 58, which is 5, but since it's the tens place, it's actually 50). So 83 \u00d7 50. \n\nTo do this, first multiply 83 by 5, then add a zero at the end. \n\n83 \u00d7 5: 3\u00d75=15, write 5 and carry 1. 8\u00d75=40 +1=41. So that's 415, then add a zero to get 4150.\n\nNow add the two results together: 664 + 4150. Let's see, 664 + 4000 is 4664, then +150 is 4814. Yep, same result as before. Okay, that checks out.\n\nAlternatively, maybe another way: using the difference of squares or some other trick? Let me think. 83 and 58, not sure if they are close to the same number or anything. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe 83 \u00d7 58 can be thought of as (80 + 3)(60 - 2). Wait, 58 is 60 minus 2. Maybe expanding that?\n\nLet me try that. (80 + 3)(60 - 2) = 80\u00d760 + 80\u00d7(-2) + 3\u00d760 + 3\u00d7(-2). \n\nCalculating each term:\n\n80\u00d760 = 4800,\n\n80\u00d7(-2) = -160,\n\n3\u00d760 = 180,\n\n3\u00d7(-2) = -6.\n\nAdding them up: 4800 - 160 + 180 -6.\n\nFirst, 4800 - 160 is 4640,\n\n4640 + 180 is 4820,\n\n4820 -6 is 4814. \n\nSame answer again. Okay, so that method also works. So that confirms it again.\n\nHmm, seems like all methods are pointing towards 4814. Maybe I can check with a calculator if I have one, but since this is a problem-solving exercise, I have to rely on manual calculations. Since all three methods give me the same result, I think it's safe to say that the product is 4814.\n\nWait, just one more check. Let me do the multiplication in another way. Maybe breaking down 58 into 50 + 8 again but in a different order. Let's see:\n\n83 \u00d7 58 = 83 \u00d7 (50 + 8) = 83\u00d750 + 83\u00d78. \n\nCalculating each term:\n\n83\u00d7