  "choices": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "text": ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 99 and 20. Hmm, product means multiplication, right? So I just have to multiply 99 by 20. Let me think about how to do this step by step.\n\nFirst, I know that multiplying by 20 is the same as multiplying by 2 and then by 10. Because 20 is 2 times 10. Maybe that can make it easier. Let me try that. So if I take 99 times 2 first, what's 99 times 2? Well, 100 times 2 is 200, so 99 is one less than 100. So 200 minus 2 would be 198. So 99 times 2 is 198.\n\nNow I need to multiply that result by 10. Because we broke down 20 into 2 and 10. So 198 times 10. That should be straightforward. Multiplying by 10 just adds a zero at the end of the number. So 198 times 10 is 1980. Therefore, 99 multiplied by 20 equals 1980.\n\nWait, let me check another way to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's write it out vertically:\n\n\u2003\u00a099\n\u00d7\u00a020\n------\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\n\nFirst, multiply 99 by 0 (the units place of 20), which gives 0. Then multiply 99 by 2 (the tens place of 20, which is actually 20). So 99 times 2 is 198, but since the 2 is in the tens place, it's actually 1980. Adding the two results together (0 + 1980) gives 1980. That matches my previous answer.\n\nAlternatively, I could think of 99 as (100 - 1). So 99 \u00d7 20 = (100 - 1) \u00d7 20. Using distributive property: 100\u00d720 - 1\u00d720 = 2000 - 20 = 1980. Yep, same result. Okay, so all methods are giving me 1980. That seems right.\n\nI guess another way to verify is by estimation. If 100 times 20 is 2000, then 99 times 20 should be 20 less, which is 1980. That checks out. So I think the answer is definitely 1980. No mistakes here.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 99 and 20 is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\n99 \\times 20 = (100 - 1) \\times 20 = 100 \\times 20 - 1 \\times 20 = 2000 - 20 = 1980\n\\]\n\n**Answer:** 1980",
