final_output:  ['\n\nThe product of 74 and 100 is calculated by multiplying the two numbers together. \n\n**Step-by-Step Calculation:**\n1. **Multiplication by 100 Rule:** Multiplying any number by 100 appends two zeros to the end of that number.\n2. Apply this rule to 74:\n   \\[\n   74 \\times 100 = 74\\,00\n   \\]\n3. **Result:**  \n   \\[\n   \\boxed{7400}\n   \\]\n\n**Verification Methods:**\n- **Breakdown:**  \n  \\(74 \\times 100 = 74 \\times (10 \\times 10) = (74 \\times 10) \\times 10 = 740 \\times 10 = 7400\\).\n- **Distributive Property:**  \n  \\(74 \\times 100 = (70 + 4) \\times 100 = 70 \\times 100 + 4 \\times 100 = 7000 + 400 = 7400\\).\n- **Long Multiplication:**  \n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{r}\n    \\quad 74 \\\\\n  \\times 100 \\\\\n  \\hline\n    \\quad 0 \\quad (\\text{74 × 0}) \\\\\n    \\quad 0 \\quad (\\text{74 × 0, shifted 1 place}) \\\\\n    7400 \\quad (\\text{74 × 1, shifted 2 places}) \\\\\n  \\hline\n    7400 \\\\\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\nAll methods confirm the final answer is **7400**. \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{7400}\n\\]', ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 74 and 100. Let me think. Hmm, product means multiplication, right? So I just have to multiply 74 by 100. Alright, multiplying by 100... I remember that when you multiply a number by 100, you can just add two zeros to the end of that number. Let me check if that works here. \n\nSo 74 times 100. If I add two zeros to 74, that would make it 7400. Wait, is that right? Let me verify another way. Maybe break it down. 74 times 100 is the same as 74 times 10 times 10. Because 10 times 10 is 100. So first, 74 times 10 is 740. Then, 740 times 10 is 7400. Yeah, that matches. \n\nAlternatively, I can think of it as 74 * 100. In mathematical terms, multiplying by 100 shifts the decimal point two places to the right. Since 74 is a whole number, the decimal is after the 4, so moving it two places would add two zeros, resulting in 7400.0, which is 7400. \n\nHmm, another way to check could be using distributive property. Let's see, 70 + 4 = 74. So (70 + 4) * 100 = 70*100 + 4*100 = 7000 + 400 = 7400. Yep, that still works. \n\nI guess all the different methods are pointing to the same answer. I don't see any mistakes here. Maybe I can use a calculator to confirm? Wait, since this is a simple multiplication, I probably don't need a calculator. But just to be thorough, let me write it out the long multiplication way.\n\n       74\n   x 100\n   -------\n       000  (74 x 0)\n      000   (74 x 0, shifted one position)\n    74      (74 x 1, shifted two positions)\n   -------\n    7400\n\nAdding those up gives 7400. Okay, that checks out. So I think the answer is definitely 7400. No doubts here.\n"]
