00. So 1400 + 100 is indeed 1500. Okay, so 20 times 75 is 1500. \n\nThen, subtract 1 times 75, which is 75. So 1500 minus 75. Let me do that subtraction. 1500 minus 75... Well, 1500 minus 100 would be 1400, but since it's only 75, I need to add back 25. So 1400 + 25 = 1425? Wait, that doesn't seem right. Wait, hold on. Let me do it step by step. 1500 minus 75. Break down 75 into 70 +5. So, 1500 - 70 is 1430, then subtract 5 more, which is 1425. Yes, that's correct. So 1500 -75 is 1425. \n\nTherefore, the product of 19 and 75 is 1425. Let me check this another way to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Maybe by doing the multiplication directly. \n\nMultiplying 19 by 75 the standard way. Let's write it out:\n\n   19\nx 75\n------\nFirst, multiply 19 by 5. 9*5 is 45, carry over the 4. 1*5 is 5 plus the 4 is 9. So that gives 95.\n\nThen, multiply 19 by 70 (since the 7 is in the tens place, so it's 70). So 19*70. Well, 19*7 is 133, so 19*70 is 1330. \n\nNow add the two results: 95 + 1330. Let's see, 95 + 1330. 1330 + 95: 1330 + 70 is 1400, then +25 is 1425. Yep, same answer. So that checks out. \n\nAlternatively, maybe another method. Let me think if there's another way. Maybe using the fact that 75 is 3/4 of 100? Hmm, not sure if that helps here. Alternatively, 75 is 25*3, so 19*25*3. Let's see, 19*25. 25 is a quarter of 100, so 19*25 is 475 (since 20*25=500, minus 1*25=25, so 500-25=475). Then 475*3. Let's compute that: 400*3=1200, 75*3=225. 1200+225=1425. Again the same result. \n\nSo, all methods are pointing to 1425. I think that's solid. Maybe another check: using the calculator method. If I had a calculator, I would just type 19*75, but since I can't do that here, but let me do the multiplication step by step again. \n\n19 multiplied by 75:\n\nBreaking it down as (20 -1)*75 = 20*75 - 75 = 1500 -75 =1425. Yep. \n\nAlternatively, using the standard algorithm again. Let me write it vertically again:\n\n       19\n     x75\n     -----\n       95  (19 x 5)\n     1330  (19 x 70, shifted one position to the left)\n     -----\n      1425\n\nAdding 95 and 1330 gives 1425. \n\nAnother way: Maybe convert both numbers into factors. Let's see, 19 is a prime number, so that's 19. 75 is 25*3, which is 5^2*3. So 19*(5^2*3). Multiplying them all together: 19*3=57, 57*25. Wait, that's the same as before. 57*25: 50*25=1250, 7*25=175, so 1250+175=1425. \n\nHmm, all methods are confirming the same answer. I don't think I made a mistake here. Let me see if there's any chance I miscalculated in one of the steps. \n\nWait, when I did 20 times 75, I got 1500. Let me verify that. 20 times 70 is 1400, plus 20 times 5 is 100, so 1400+100=1500. Correct. Then subtract 75, so 1500-75 is 1425. \n\nAlternatively, 19 times 75. Let me think of 75 as 75. So 19*75. Let me do 10*75=750, 9*75=675, then 750+675. 750+600 is 1350, plus 75 is 1425. Yeah, that works too. \n\nBreaking into 10+9:\n\n(10 +9)*75 = 10*75 +9*75 = 750 + 675 = 1425. \n\nYep, same answer. \n\nI think this is solid. All different methods of breaking down the multiplication give the same result. So I can be confident that the product of 19 and 75 is indeed 1425. \n\nJust to recap, the key was either using the distributive property by making 19 into 20-1, which simplifies the calculation, or breaking down the multiplication into parts and adding them up. Either way, all paths lead to 1425, so I think that's the correct answer.\n"]
