      "text": ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 90 and 44. Hmm, let's see. Multiplying two numbers together... right, product means multiplication. Alright, 90 times 44. I should probably do this step by step. Let me think of the easiest way to calculate this without a calculator.\n\nFirst, maybe break down the numbers into smaller parts to make it easier. 90 is 9 times 10, and 44 is 4 times 11. Wait, or maybe 44 can also be thought of as 40 plus 4. That might be helpful. Let me try the distributive property here. So, 90 times (40 + 4). That would be 90*40 plus 90*4. Let me calculate each part separately.\n\nStarting with 90 multiplied by 40. 90 times 40... Well, 90 times 4 is 360, so if I add a zero at the end because it's 40 instead of 4, that would be 3600. Okay, that part seems straightforward. Now, 90 times 4. That's 360. So adding those two results together: 3600 + 360. Let me add those. 3600 plus 300 is 3900, and then plus 60 more would be 3960. So is the total 3960?\n\nWait, let me check another way to confirm. Maybe using another method to ensure I didn't make a mistake. Let's try multiplying 44 by 90 directly. \n\nMultiplying 44 by 90. Since 90 is 9 times 10, I can first multiply 44 by 9 and then multiply the result by 10. 44 times 9... Let's see, 40 times 9 is 360, and 4 times 9 is 36. Adding those together gives 360 + 36 = 396. Then multiply by 10, so 396 times 10 is 3960. Okay, same result. That's a good sign.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the standard algorithm for multiplication. Let me write it out vertically:\n\n\u2003\u00a044\n\u00d7\u00a090\n------\nFirst, multiply 44 by 0 (the units place of 90), which is 0. Then multiply 44 by 9 (the tens place of 90, so actually 90), which would be 396, but shifted one position to the left. So adding a zero in the units place and then 396, so 3960. Yep, that matches.\n\nHmm, seems like all methods are pointing towards 3960. Let me just verify once more with another approach. Maybe using the fact that 90 is 100 - 10. Wait, but 44 times (100 - 10) would be 44*100 minus 44*10. That would be 4400 - 440 = 3960. Oh, that works too. So that's another confirmation.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps breaking down 44 into 11*4. Then 90*11 is 990, and then times 4 would be 990*4. Let's see, 900*4 is 3600 and 90*4 is 360, so 3600 + 360 = 3960. Yep, same answer again.\n\nI think that's solid. All different methods give me 3960, so I'm pretty confident that's the correct product of 90 and 44. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, so I think that's the right answer.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 90 and 44 is calculated as follows:\n\nFirst, break down the multiplication using the distributive property:\n\\[\n90 \\times 44 = 90 \\times (40 + 4) = (90 \\times 40) + (90 \\times 4)\n\\]\nCalculate each part:\n\\[\n90 \\times 40 = 3,\\!600 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 90 \\times 4 = 360\n\\]\nAdd the results:\n\\[\n3,\\!600 + 360 = 3,\\!960\n\\]\n\n**Verification using alternative methods:**\n- **Direct multiplication:**\n  \\[\n  44 \\times 90 = (44 \\times 9) \\times 10 = 396 \\times 10 = 3,\\!960\n  \\]\n- **Standard algorithm:**\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{r}\n    \\quad 44 \\\\\n  \\times 90 \\\\\n  \\hline\n    0\\, (44 \\times 0) \\\\\n  + 3960\\, (44 \\times 90) \\\\\n  \\hline\n    3,\\!960 \\\\\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n- **Alternative breakdown:**\n  \\[\n  90 \\times 44 = 90 \\times (100 - 10) = 9,\\!000 - 900 = 8,\\!100 \\quad \\text{(Wait, this is incorrect. Correct method:)} \\\\\n  44 \\times (100 - 10) = 4,\\!400 - 440 = 3,\\!960\n  \\]\n\nAll methods confirm the result is consistent.\n\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{3960}\\)",
