0 is 3900, and then plus 60 more would be 3960. So is the total 3960?\n\nWait, let me check another way to confirm. Maybe using another method to ensure I didn't make a mistake. Let's try multiplying 44 by 90 directly. \n\nMultiplying 44 by 90. Since 90 is 9 times 10, I can first multiply 44 by 9 and then multiply the result by 10. 44 times 9... Let's see, 40 times 9 is 360, and 4 times 9 is 36. Adding those together gives 360 + 36 = 396. Then multiply by 10, so 396 times 10 is 3960. Okay, same result. That's a good sign.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the standard algorithm for multiplication. Let me write it out vertically:\n\n\u2003\xa044\n×\xa090\n------\nFirst, multiply 44 by 0 (the units place of 90), which is 0. Then multiply 44 by 9 (the tens place of 90, so actually 90), which would be 396, but shifted one position to the left. So adding a zero in the units place and then 396, so 3960. Yep, that matches.\n\nHmm, seems like all methods are pointing towards 3960. Let me just verify once more with another approach. Maybe using the fact that 90 is 100 - 10. Wait, but 44 times (100 - 10) would be 44*100 minus 44*10. That would be 4400 - 440 = 3960. Oh, that works too. So that's another confirmation.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps breaking down 44 into 11*4. Then 90*11 is 990, and then times 4 would be 990*4. Let's see, 900*4 is 3600 and 90*4 is 360, so 3600 + 360 = 3960. Yep, same answer again.\n\nI think that's solid. All different methods give me 3960, so I'm pretty confident that's the correct product of 90 and 44. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, so I think that's the right answer.\n"]
