nal line. The top number goes to the left of the diagonal, the bottom to the right.\n\nLet me try again. For each cell:\n\nTop-left cell (4\u00d71=4): write 0 in the top triangle and 4 in the bottom.\n\nTop-right cell (6\u00d71=6): 0 top, 6 bottom.\n\nBottom-left cell (4\u00d72=8): 0 top, 8 bottom.\n\nBottom-right cell (6\u00d72=12): 1 top and 2 bottom.\n\nNow, the diagonals: The rightmost diagonal is the bottom of the top-right cell (6) and the bottom of the bottom-right cell (2). Wait, maybe the diagonals are vertical lines from top right to bottom left.\n\nActually, the diagonals are the diagonal lines in each cell. The addition is done along the diagonals from top-right to bottom-left.\n\nSo starting from the rightmost diagonal (the ones place):\n\nThe first diagonal (rightmost) is just the bottom part of the bottom-right cell, which is 2.\n\nNext diagonal to the left includes the bottom of the top-right cell (6) and the top of the bottom-right cell (1). So 6 +1 =7.\n\nNext diagonal to the left includes the bottom of the top-left cell (4), the top of the bottom-left cell (0), and the top of the top-right cell (0). Wait, maybe I'm getting confused.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's better to look up the exact steps, but since I might not remember perfectly, let me try again.\n\nWait, perhaps I should arrange the numbers properly. Let me draw it mentally:\n\nThe lattice grid for 46 \u00d712 would look like this:\n\n\u20034\u20036\n\n1\n\n\u20032\n\nEach cell is divided by a diagonal. The intersections are:\n\nTop row first cell (4\u00d71):\n\nTop-left cell: 4\u00d71=4 \u2192 written as 0/4 (top/bottom of the diagonal)\n\nTop row second cell (6\u00d71=6 \u2192 0/6\n\nSecond row first cell (4\u00d72=8 \u2192 0/8\n\nSecond row second cell (6\u00d72=12 \u21921/2\n\nThen, the diagonals are vertical lines from top-right to bottom-left. The rightmost diagonal is the bottom part of the second cell in the top row (6) and the bottom part of the second cell in the second row (2).