      "text": ":\nOkay, let's see. I need to find the product of 70 and 94. Hmm, multiplying two numbers. Alright, so 70 times 94. Let me think about the best way to do this. Maybe I can use the standard multiplication method, or perhaps there's a quicker way by breaking it down.\n\nFirst, I know that 70 is 7 times 10, so maybe I can rewrite the problem as 7 * 10 * 94. Then, since multiplication is associative, I can rearrange that as 7 * (10 * 94). Let me compute 10 times 94 first. That's straightforward because multiplying by 10 just adds a zero at the end. So 10 times 94 is 940. Then I have 7 times 940. \n\nHmm, 7 times 900 is 6300, and 7 times 40 is 280. Adding those together would give 6300 + 280 = 6580. Wait, is that right? Let me check again. 940 times 7. Breaking it down: 900*7 is 6300, 40*7 is 280, so adding them gives 6300 + 280. 6300 plus 200 is 6500, then plus 80 is 6580. Okay, that seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do it the standard way. Let me write it out vertically:\n\n   70\nx 94\n------\nFirst, multiply 70 by 4: 70*4=280. Then multiply 70 by 90 (since the 9 is in the tens place, so it's 90). 70*90. Let's see, 7*9 is 63, and then add two zeros, so 6300. Then add the two results: 280 + 6300 = 6580. Yep, same answer. \n\nWait, just to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Let me try another method. Maybe using distributive property: (70)*(94) = 70*(90 + 4) = 70*90 + 70*4. Which is exactly what I did before, so that gives the same result. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using some other trick. For example, 70*94 is the same as 70*(100 - 6). Let's see, that would be 70*100 minus 70*6. 70*100 is 7000, and 70*6 is 420. So 7000 - 420 = 6580. Hmm, same answer again. \n\nOkay, so all methods are giving me 6580. I think that's the correct product. Let me just confirm with a calculator in my mind. 70 times 90 is 6300, plus 70 times 4 is 280, so total is 6300 + 280. 6300 plus 200 is 6500, plus 80 is 6580. Yep, that's consistent. \n\nI don't see any errors in my calculations here. All different approaches lead to the same result, so I'm pretty confident the answer is 6580.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 70 and 94 is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\n70 \\times 94 = 70 \\times (90 + 4) = (70 \\times 90) + (70 \\times 4)\n\\]\n\nFirst, compute \\(70 \\times 90\\):\n\\[\n70 \\times 90 = 6,\\!300\n\\]\n\nThen compute \\(70 \\times 4\\):\n\\[\n70 \\times 4 = 280\n\\]\n\nAdd the two results together:\n\\[\n6,\\!300 + 280 = 6,\\!580\n\\]\n\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{6580}\\)",
