      "text": ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 40 and 46. Hmm, let's see. Multiplying two numbers... I remember that product means multiplication. So, 40 times 46. Let me think about the best way to do this.\n\nFirst, maybe I can just do it the standard way. Let's write it down like a multiplication problem:\n\n   40\nx 46\n------\n\nStarting with the 6 in the ones place of 46. Multiply 6 by 40. Wait, but actually, when multiplying, you have to consider the place values. So 46 is 40 + 6, right? So maybe breaking it down using distributive property would help. Let me try that.\n\n40 times 46 is the same as 40*(40 + 6). That would be 40*40 plus 40*6. Let me compute each part.\n\n40*40 is 1600. Okay, that's straightforward. Then 40*6 is 240. Adding those together: 1600 + 240. Hmm, 1600 + 200 is 1800, and then +40 more is 1840. So, is the answer 1840?\n\nWait, but let me check another way to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Maybe using the standard algorithm. Let me line them up:\n\n     40\n   x46\n   -----\nFirst multiply 40 by 6. 40*6 is 240. Then multiply 40 by 40 (the 4 in 46 is in the tens place, so it's actually 40*40). But wait, when doing the standard algorithm, you have to shift one position for the tens place. So actually, the second line would be 40*40, but written as 40*4 (the digit) times 10, so 40*4 is 160, and then times 10 is 1600. Then adding 240 + 1600 gives the same 1840. Okay, that matches.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another method. I remember something about multiplying numbers around 40 or 50? Let me think. Like, if you have two numbers that are close to 40 or 50, maybe there's a trick. Wait, 40 and 46 are both close to 40. Let me see. Alternatively, maybe using the idea of (a + b)(a - b) but that might not apply here.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps 40 times 46 is the same as 40*(45 +1). So 40*45 plus 40*1. 40*45 is... Well, 40*45 is half of 40*90, which would be 3600, so half of that is 1800. Wait, no, 45 is half of 90, so 40*45 is 40*(90/2) = (40*90)/2 = 3600/2 = 1800. Then adding 40*1 = 40 gives 1800 +40=1840 again. Same answer. So that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using 46 as 50 -4. So 40*(50 -4) = 40*50 -40*4 = 2000 - 160 = 1840. Yep, that works too. Hmm, all methods are leading me to 1840. So that's probably the right answer.\n\nWait, let me just do straight multiplication again to confirm. 40 times 46. Since 40 is a multiple of 10, maybe it's easier. 4 times 46 is 184, and then add a zero at the end to get 1840. Oh, that's a simpler way. 4*46 is 184, so 40*46 is 1840. Yep, that's quick.\n\nSo all the different methods give me the same result, so I think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. So the product of 40 and 46 is 1840.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 40 and 46 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Using the distributive property**:\n   \\[\n   40 \\times 46 = 40 \\times (40 + 6) = (40 \\times 40) + (40 \\times 6) = 1600 + 240 = 1840\n   \\]\n\n2. **Standard multiplication algorithm**:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     \\quad 40 \\\\\n   \\times 46 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     240 \\quad (\\text{40 \u00d7 6}) \\\\\n   +1600 \\quad (\\text{40 \u00d7 40, shifted one position left}) \\\\\n   \\hline\n     1840 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Simpler method**:\n   \\[\n   40 \\times 46 = (4 \\times 46) \\times 10 = 184 \\times 10 = 1840\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer**: \\boxed{1840}",
      "logprobs": null,
