      "text": ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 36 and 49. Hmm, let's see. Multiplying these two numbers. I remember that product means multiplication, right? So 36 times 49. I should probably just do the multiplication step by step.\n\nFirst, maybe break it down into smaller parts. Let me think, 36 is 30 plus 6, and 49 is 40 plus 9. Wait, maybe using the distributive property would help here. So, (30 + 6) multiplied by (40 + 9). That would be 30*40 + 30*9 + 6*40 + 6*9. Let me calculate each part separately.\n\n30 times 40 is... 30*40. Well, 3*4 is 12, and then there are two zeros, so 1200. Okay, that's straightforward. Then 30 times 9. That's 270. Then 6 times 40. 6*4 is 24, so with a zero, that's 240. And finally, 6 times 9 is 54. Now add all those together: 1200 + 270 + 240 + 54.\n\nLet me add them step by step. Starting with 1200 + 270. That should be 1470. Then 1470 + 240. Hmm, 1470 plus 200 is 1670, then plus 40 more is 1710. Then adding the last 54. 1710 + 54. 1710 + 50 is 1760, plus 4 is 1764. So the total is 1764?\n\nWait, let me check that again. Maybe I made an addition mistake. Let's verify each step. \n\nFirst part: 30*40 = 1200. Correct. 30*9 is indeed 270. 6*40 is 240, and 6*9 is 54. Adding them all: 1200 + 270 is 1470. Then 1470 + 240. Let me do that again: 1470 + 200 is 1670, plus 40 is 1710. Then 1710 +54. 1710 +50 is 1760, plus 4 is 1764. Hmm, that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another way to compute 36*49. Let me see. Maybe using some other method to confirm. For example, 36 times 50 would be easier, right? 36*50 is 1800. But since it's 49, that's one less than 50. So 36*49 is 36*(50-1) = 36*50 - 36*1 = 1800 - 36 = 1764. Oh, that's the same answer. So that confirms it. \n\nSo both methods give me 1764. I think that's correct. Let me just do straight multiplication as well to be thorough. \n\nMultiplying 36 by 49 the standard way:\n\n   36\nx49\n------\nFirst multiply 36 by 9: 36*9=324\n\nThen multiply 36 by 40 (since the 4 is in the tens place, so 4*10). 36*40 = 1440\n\nNow add those two results: 324 + 1440. \n\n324 + 1440. Let's see, 300 + 1400 is 1700, 24 + 40 is 64, so total is 1764. Yep, same answer again. \n\nOkay, so all three methods give me 1764. I think that's the right product. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. So the answer should be 1764.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 36 and 49 is calculated as follows:\n\n**Method 1: Using the Distributive Property**\n\\[\n(30 + 6)(40 + 9) = 30 \\times 40 + 30 \\times 9 + 6 \\times 40 + 6 \\times 9\n\\]\n\\[\n= 1200 + 270 + 240 + 54 = 1764\n\\]\n\n**Method 2: Adjusting for Simplicity**\n\\[\n36 \\times 49 = 36 \\times (50 - 1) = 36 \\times 50 - 36 \\times 1\n\\]\n\\[\n= 1800 - 36 = 1764\n\\]\n\n**Method 3: Standard Multiplication**\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   \\quad 36 \\\\\n\\times 49 \\\\\n\\hline\n   324 \\quad (\\text{36 \u00d7 9}) \\\\\n+1440 \\quad (\\text{36 \u00d7 40, shifted one position left}) \\\\\n\\hline\n  1764 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n**Final Answer:**  \nThe product of 36 and 49 is \\boxed{1764}.",
