      "text": ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 81 and 61. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers... I remember that the product is just the result when you multiply them together. But wait, maybe there's a trick to do this faster instead of just straight multiplying?\n\nLet me recall some multiplication strategies. Maybe using the distributive property? Like breaking down the numbers into smaller parts. 81 is 80 + 1, and 61 is 60 + 1. Maybe I can use the formula (a + b)(c + d) = ac + ad + bc + bd. Let's try that.\n\nSo, (80 + 1)(60 + 1) = 80*60 + 80*1 + 1*60 + 1*1. Calculating each term:\n\n80 times 60... 8 times 6 is 48, and then there are two zeros, so 4800.\n\nThen 80 times 1 is 80.\n\n1 times 60 is 60.\n\nAnd 1 times 1 is 1.\n\nNow add all those together: 4800 + 80 is 4880, plus 60 is 4940, plus 1 is 4941. So that gives me 4941.\n\nWait, but maybe I should check by doing the standard multiplication to be sure. Let me try that.\n\nMultiplying 81 by 61 the traditional way:\n\nFirst, write 81  \n           x61  \n       --------\n           81 (which is 81 x 1)  \n          4860 (which is 81 x 60, shifted one position to the left)  \n       --------\n          4941\n\nAdding 81 and 4860: 81 + 4860. Let's see, 4860 + 80 is 4940, plus 1 is 4941. Yep, same result. Okay, so that checks out.\n\nAlternatively, is there another way? Maybe using the difference of squares? Let me see. Wait, 81 is 9 squared, and 61 is... not sure. Maybe not applicable here. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, 81 times 60 would be 81*60, which is 4860, and then add another 81 to get 81*61. That's actually the same as the standard multiplication. 4860 + 81 = 4941. Yep, same answer.\n\nHmm, seems consistent. I think that's solid. Let me just confirm with a calculator in my mind. Wait, 80 times 60 is 4800, plus 80 times 1 is 80, plus 1 times 60 is 60, plus 1. So 4800+80 is 4880, plus 60 is 4940, plus 1 is 4941. Definitely that's right. Okay, so I think the product is 4941.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 81 and 61 is calculated as follows:\n\nFirst, break down the numbers using the distributive property:\n\\[\n(80 + 1)(60 + 1) = 80 \\times 60 + 80 \\times 1 + 1 \\times 60 + 1 \\times 1\n\\]\n\nCalculate each term:\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n80 \\times 60 &= 4800, \\\\\n80 \\times 1 &= 80, \\\\\n1 \\times 60 &= 60, \\\\\n1 \\times 1 &= 1.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nAdd the results together:\n\\[\n4800 + 80 + 60 + 1 = 4880 + 60 + 1 = 4940 + 1 = 4941.\n\\]\n\n**Verification via standard multiplication:**\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   \\quad 81 \\\\\n\\times 61 \\\\\n\\hline\n   \\quad 81 \\quad (\\text{(81 \u00d7 1)}) \\\\\n+ 4860 \\quad (\\text{(81 \u00d7 60, shifted left by 1)}) \\\\\n\\hline\n   4941 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n**Final Answer:**  \nThe product of 81 and 61 is \\boxed{4941}.",
