those gives 260 +39=299. Yep. So all methods point to 299. \n\nI think that's solid. So the product of 23 and 13 is 299.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 23 and 13 is calculated by multiplying the two numbers together:\n\n\\[\n23 \\times 13 = 299\n\\]\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n1. **Break down using distributive property:**  \n   \\(23 \\times 13 = 23 \\times (10 + 3)\\)  \n   \\(= (23 \\times 10) + (23 \\times 3)\\)  \n   \\(= 230 + 69\\)  \n   \\(= 299\\).\n\n2. **Verification via standard multiplication:**  \n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     \\quad 23 \\\\\n   \\times 13 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     \\quad 69 \\quad (\\text{23 \u00d7 3}) \\\\\n   + 230 \\quad (\\text{23 \u00d7 10, shifted left}) \\\\\n   \\hline\n     299 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the result is **299**.\n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{299}",
