3 times 30. Since 23 is close to 25, maybe I can use that. 25 times 30 is 750, right? But 23 is 2 less than 25. So each of those 30 would subtract 2*30 = 60. So 750 minus 60 is 690. That works too. Hmm, that's another method and still 690.\n\nAlternatively, using the distributive property: (20 + 3) * 30 = 20*30 + 3*30. 20*30 is 600, and 3*30 is 90. 600 + 90 is 690. Yep, that's the same as before.\n\nI guess all these different methods are leading me to the same answer, 690. I think that's correct. Let me just do a quick check with division. If 23 times 30 is 690, then dividing 690 by 23 should give me 30. Let me see: 23 times 30 is 690. 690 divided by 23. Let me do that division. 23 times 30 is 690, so yes, that works. Alternatively, 690 divided by 30 is 23. Let me check that: 30 times 20 is 600, and 30 times 3 is 90, so 600 +90 is 690. So that's correct.\n\nI think I'm confident that the product is 690. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, and all the different methods cross-check to the same result. So yeah, the answer should be 690.\n"] 46%|████▌     | 231/500 [4:05:58<3:04:14, 41.09s/it] 46%|████▋     | 232/500 [4:06:41<3:06:31, 41.76s/it]
