\u00d710). So 58\u00d76 is 348, and since it's times 10, it becomes 3480.\n\nNow, add the two results: 290 + 3480. 290 + 3480 is 3770. Okay, same result. So that checks out.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another approach, like the difference of squares or something, but I don't know if that applies here. Let me see. 58 and 65... Hmm, not sure. Maybe if I can express them as numbers around a common base?\n\nAlternatively, maybe 58 \u00d7 65 can be thought of as (60 - 2) \u00d7 (60 + 5). Wait, but that might complicate more. Let me try expanding that. (60 - 2)(60 + 5) = 60\u00d760 + 60\u00d75 - 2\u00d760 - 2\u00d75. That would be 3600 + 300 - 120 -10. Let's compute that: 3600 + 300 is 3900. Then 3900 -120 is 3780, then minus 10 is 3770. Same answer again. Okay, so that's consistent.\n\nHmm, so all methods are giving me 3770. Wait, but just to be thorough, maybe I can do it another way. Let me use the distributive property step by step again.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can break down 65 into 60 + 5, so 58\u00d760 + 58\u00d75. Let me compute each part:\n\n58\u00d760: 58\u00d76 is 348, so times 10 is 3480.\n\n58\u00d75: 50\u00d75=250 and 8\u00d75=40, so 250+40=290.\n\nAdding 3480 + 290: 3480 + 200 is 3680, then +90 is 3770. Yep, same result.\n\nAlright, so all methods are giving me 3770. I think that's solid. Let me just confirm once more with a calculator approach, but since I don't have a calculator, maybe do another check.\n\nWait, if I reverse the numbers, 65\u00d758, it should be the same. Let me try that way. 65\u00d750 is 3250, and 65\u00d78 is 520. Adding them gives 3250 + 520 = 3770. Yep, same answer. So that's another confirmation.\n\nAlternatively, maybe add 58 sixty-five times? That would be tedious, but perhaps do a small portion. Let's see:\n\nIf 58\u00d710 is 580, so 58\u00d760 would be 580\u00d76=3480. Then 58\u00d75 is 290. 3480 + 290 = 3770. Yep, same as before.\n\nHmm, so all these different methods lead me to 3770. I think that's the correct answer. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, and cross-verifying with different methods gives the same result. Therefore, I'm confident that the product of 58 and 65 is 3770.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 58 and 65 is calculated as follows:\n\nFirst, break down the numbers using the distributive property:\n\\[\n(50 + 8) \\times (60 + 5) = 50 \\times 60 + 50 \\times 5 + 8 \\times 60 + 8 \\times 5\n\\]\n\nCalculate each term:\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n50 \\times 60 &= 3000, \\\\\n50 \\times 5 &= 250, \\\\\n8 \\times 60 &= 480, \\\\\n8 \\times 5 &= 40.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nAdd the results together:\n\\[\n3000 + 250 + 480 + 40 = 3770.\n\\]\n\n**Verification using standard multiplication:**\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   \\quad 58 \\\\\n\\times 65 \\\\\n\\hline\n  290 \\quad (\\text{58 \u00d7 5}) \\\\\n+3480 \\quad (\\text{58 \u00d7 60}) \\\\\n\\hline\n  3770 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the result is **3770**.\n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{3770}",
