final_output:  ['\n\nTo find the product of 42 and 71, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. Write the numbers vertically:\n   ```\n    42\n  × 71\n  ------\n  ```\n\n2. Multiply 42 by the units digit of 71 (which is 1):\n   \\[\n   42 \\times 1 = 42\n   \\]\n\n3. Multiply 42 by the tens digit of 71 (which is 7, representing 70). First, calculate \\(42 \\times 7\\):\n   \\[\n   42 \\times 7 = 294\n   \\]\n   Then, shift the result one position to the left (since 7 is in the tens place):\n   \\[\n   294 \\times 10 = 2940\n   \\]\n\n4. Add the two results together:\n   \\[\n   42 + 2940 = 2982\n   \\]\n\nTo verify, we can use the distributive property:\n\\[\n(40 + 2)(70 + 1) = 40 \\times 70 + 40 \\times 1 + 2 \\times 70 + 2 \\times 1\n\\]\n\\[\n= 2800 + 40 + 140 + 2 = 2800 + 40 = 2840; \\quad 2840 + 140 = 2980; \\quad 2980 + 2 = 2982\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 42 and 71 is \\(\\boxed{2982}\\).', ":\nOkay, let's see. I need to find the product of 42 and 71. Hmm, multiplication. Alright, so 42 times 71. Let me think about the best way to do this. Maybe I can use the standard multiplication algorithm? Let me recall how that works.\n\nFirst, write down the numbers vertically, aligning the digits by place value. So, 42 on top of 71. Wait, actually, does it matter which one is on top? Not really, but maybe it's easier if I put the larger number first? Wait, 71 is actually larger than 42, so perhaps I should write 71 on top and 42 below? No, actually, in multiplication, the order doesn't matter because multiplication is commutative. So 42 times 71 is the same as 71 times 42. So maybe it's easier to do 42 times 70 plus 42 times 1? Wait, that's breaking it down into distributive property. Alternatively, using the standard algorithm step by step.\n\nLet me try the standard way. Let me write 42 multiplied by 71. Let me set it up:\n\n\u2003\xa042\n×\xa071\n------\n\nStarting with the rightmost digit of the bottom number. Wait, actually, in the standard algorithm, you usually take the bottom number's digits from right to left. But here, since both are two-digit numbers, let me see. Let me consider 42 as the top number and 71 as the bottom number. So:\n\n\u2003\xa042\n×\xa071\n------\nFirst, multiply 42 by 1 (the units place of 71). Then multiply 42 by 70 (the tens place of 71, which is 7 in the tens place, so 70), and then add the two results.\n\nSo, step by step:\n\nFirst step: 42 × 1 = 42.\n\nSecond step: 42 × 70. Since 70 is 7×10, so 42×7×10. Let me compute 42×7 first. 42×7: 40×7=280, and 2×7=14, so 280+14=294. Then multiply by 10 gives 2940.\n\nThen add the two results: 42 + 2940 = 2982.\n\nWait, that seems straightforward. Let me check if that's correct. Alternatively, maybe I can do it another way to verify.\n\nAlternatively, using the distributive property: (40 + 2)(70 + 1). Let's expand that.\n\nMultiply each term:\n\n40×70 + 40×1 + 2×70 + 2×1.\n\nCalculating each part:\n\n40×70 = 2800,\n\n40×1 = 40,\n\n2×70 = 140,\n\n2×1 = 2.\n\nNow add them all together: 2800 + 40 = 2840; 2840 + 140 = 2980; 2980 + 2 = 2982. So same result. Okay, that's good.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use another method, like the lattice method? Let me try that quickly.\n\nBut maybe that's overcomplicating. Alternatively, just do straight multiplication:\n\n42\n\n×71\n\nMultiply 1 (units) by 42: 42.\n\nMultiply 7 (tens place, so actually 70) by 42: 42×7=294, then shift one position to the left (because it's the tens place), so 2940. Then add 42 + 2940 = 2982. Yep, same as before.\n\nAlternatively, maybe check with a calculator? Wait, but since I don't have a calculator here, I need to verify manually.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps breaking down the numbers differently. Let's see, 42 ×71.\n\nNotice that 42 ×70 is 2940, plus 42 gives 2982. That's the same as before. So that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe 71×42. Let's see if that helps. 70×42 +1×42. 70×42 is 2940, plus 42 is 2982. Same result.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps check via another method: 42 ×71.\n\nLet me think of 42×71 as (40 + 2)(70 +1). Wait, that's the same as the distributive method above. So that gives me confidence that it's 2982.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can use the difference of squares or some other trick? Hmm, maybe not straightforward here. Let me see.\n\nAlternatively, 42 ×71. Let me see, 42 is 6×7, and 71 is a prime number, I think. Not sure if that helps.\n\nAlternatively, approximate to check. 42×70 is 2940, so 42×71 is 2940 +42=2982. So that's the exact value.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check by dividing the result by one of the numbers to see if I get the other. For example, 2982 divided by 42. Let me do that division.\n\n2982 ÷42. Let's see. 42×70 is 2940. 2982 -2940 is 42. So 70 +1=71. So yes, 42×71=2982. That checks out.\n\nAlternatively, 2982 divided by71. Let's see how that goes. 71×40=2840. Subtract that from 2982: 2982-2840=142. 71×2=142. So total is 40+2=42. So that works. So that confirms it.\n\nHmm, so all the methods are pointing to 2982. I think that's solid. Let me just recap the steps once more to be thorough.\n\nFirst method: standard multiplication:\n\n42\n\n×71\n\n--------\n\nMultiply 1×42: 42.\n\nMultiply 7 (tens place, so actually 70) ×42: 7×42=294, shifted one decimal to the left becomes 2940.\n\nAdd 42 +2940:\n\n2940\n\n+\xa0 42\n\n------\n\n2982.\n\nSecond method: expanding (40+2)(70+1):\n\n40×70 =2800,\n\n40×1=40,\n\n2×70=140,\n\n2×1=2.\n\nAdding all: 2800+40=2840; 2840+140=2980; 2980+2=2982.\n\nThird method: division check. 2982 ÷42=71, which is correct, and 2982 ÷71=42, which is also correct.\n\nTherefore, I'm confident the answer is 2982.\n\nWait, but let me just do the multiplication again step by step, in case I made a mistake in the initial calculation.\n\nFirst, writing 42 ×71.\n\nStarting with the units digit of the lower number (assuming 71 is the lower number). Wait, actually, in the standard algorithm, it's arbitrary which is on top, but the process is same.\n\nSuppose:\n\n\u2003\xa042\n\n×\xa071\n\n--------\n\nFirst, multiply 42 by 1 (units digit of 71):\n\n1×2=2, 1×4=4, so 42.\n\nThen, multiply 42 by 7 (which is in the tens place, so actually 70). So 7×2=14, write down 4 and carry over 1. Then 7×4=28, plus the carried 1 is 29. So that gives 294. But since the 7 is in the tens place, we shift one position to the left, so it's 2940.\n\nAdding 42 and 2940:\n\n42\n\n+2940\n\n------\n\n2982. Yep, same result.\n\nAlternatively, if I had written 71 on top and 42 below, it would be the same:\n\n\u2003\xa071\n\n×\xa042\n\n--------\n\nFirst, 71×2=142.\n\nSecond, 71×40=2840. Adding 142 +2840=2982. Same answer.\n\nAlright, so all methods lead to 2982, so I think that's the correct product.\n\nI don't see any errors in my calculations. The checks via division also confirm it. Therefore, I can be confident the answer is 2982.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 42 and 71 is \\boxed{2982}.\n"]
