    {
      "index": 0,
      "text": ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 66 and 73. Hmm, let me think about the best way to do this. I remember that multiplying two numbers can sometimes be done more easily by breaking them down into smaller parts. Maybe using the distributive property? Let me try that.\n\nFirst, I can write 66 as 60 + 6, and 73 as 70 + 3. So, the problem becomes (60 + 6) multiplied by (70 + 3). To use the distributive property, I have to multiply each part of the first number by each part of the second number and then add them all together. Let me write that out step by step.\n\nStarting with 60 times 70. That should be straightforward. 60 times 70 is... Well, 6 times 7 is 42, and then I have to add the two zeros from 60 and 70, so that makes 4200. Okay, got that part.\n\nNext, 60 times 3. That's easier. 60 times 3 is 180. Alright, moving on to the next term in the first parentheses. That's 6 times 70. 6 times 70 is 420. And finally, the last part is 6 times 3, which is 18. \n\nNow I need to add all those results together. Let me list them again to make sure I don't miss any:\n\n- 60 * 70 = 4200\n- 60 * 3 = 180\n- 6 * 70 = 420\n- 6 * 3 = 18\n\nAdding them up step by step. Let's start with 4200 + 180. That's 4380. Then, adding 420 to that gives me 4380 + 420. Hmm, 4380 + 400 would be 4780, and then +20 more is 4800. Wait, that seems off. Let me check again. 4380 + 420. 4380 + 400 is 4780, plus 20 more is 4800. Yeah, that's right. Then add the last term, which is 18. So 4800 + 18 = 4818. \n\nWait, but let me make sure I did each step correctly. Maybe there's another way to check. Alternatively, I can use another method like the standard multiplication algorithm. Let me try that to confirm.\n\nMultiplying 66 by 73. Let's write it as:\n\n\u2003\u200366  \n\u00d7\u00a073  \n\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014  \nFirst, multiply 66 by 3. 6 times 3 is 18, so write down 8 and carry over 1. Then 6 times 3 is 18 plus the carried 1 is 19. So that gives 198. \n\nThen, multiply 66 by 70. Since it's the tens place, we'll shift one position to the left. 66 times 7 is 462, so adding a zero at the end makes it 4620. \n\nNow add 198 and 4620 together. 198 + 4620. Let's see, 4620 + 200 would be 4820, but subtract the 2 that's extra (since 198 is 200 - 2), so 4820 - 2 = 4818. \n\nOkay, that matches the first method. So both ways give me 4818. Hmm, that seems right. But just to be thorough, maybe I can use another approach like the difference of squares or something else? Wait, 66 and 73 aren't symmetric around a central number, so maybe that's not the best. Alternatively, maybe breaking down 73 into 70 + 3 and multiplying by 66 directly?\n\nWait, that's actually similar to the first method. Let me try that again. 66 times 70 is (60*70) + (6*70) = 4200 + 420 = 4620, and 66 times 3 is 198. Then adding them gives 4620 + 198. Let me add them step by step. 4620 + 100 is 4720, then +98 more is 4720 + 98 = 4818. Yep, same result. \n\nAlternatively, maybe use a calculator? But since I don't have one here, I can do another check. Let me divide the result by one of the numbers and see if I get the other. Let's try 4818 divided by 66. \n\nDividing 4818 by 66. Let's see how many times 66 goes into 4818. \n\nFirst, 66 x 70 is 4620, as before. Subtract that from 4818: 4818 - 4620 = 198. Then, 66 goes into 198 exactly 3 times (since 66 x 3 = 198). So total is 70 + 3 = 73. Perfect, that's the other number. So that confirms that 66 multiplied by 73 is indeed 4818. \n\nAnother way, maybe using some mental math tricks. For instance, 66 is 6x11, and 73 is a prime number, I think. Not sure if that helps. Alternatively, maybe approximate? 66 times 70 is 4620, and then 66 more three times would be 198, so total 4620 + 198 = 4818. Yep, same as before. \n\nI think all methods are converging to the same answer, so I must be correct. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. So the product of 66 and 73 is 4818.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 66 and 73 is \\boxed{4818}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 66 and 73, we can use the distributive property by breaking down the numbers into smaller parts:\n\n\\[\n66 \\times 73 = (60 + 6) \\times (70 + 3)\n\\]\n\nExpanding this using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n(60 + 6) \\times (70 + 3) = 60 \\times 70 + 60 \\times 3 + 6 \\times 70 + 6 \\times 3\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n\n\\[\n60 \\times 70 = 4200\n\\]\n\\[\n60 \\times 3 = 180\n\\]\n\\[\n6 \\times 70 = 420\n\\]\n\\[\n6 \\times 3 = 18\n\\]\n\nAdding all these results together:\n\n\\[\n4200 + 180 + 420 + 18 = 4200 + 180 = 4380\n\\]\n\\[\n4380 + 420 = 4800\n\\]\n\\[\n4800 + 18 = 4818\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   66 \\\\\n\\times 73 \\\\\n\\hline\n  198 \\quad (\\text{66} \\times 3) \\\\\n+4620 \\quad (\\text{66} \\times 70) \\\\\n\\hline\n  4818 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 66 and 73 is \\(\\boxed{4818}\\).",
