final_output:  ['\n\nThe product of 74 and 42 is calculated as follows:\n\nUsing the distributive property:\n\\[\n74 \\times 42 = (70 + 4) \\times (40 + 2)\n\\]\n\\[\n= 70 \\times 40 + 70 \\times 2 + 4 \\times 40 + 4 \\times 2\n\\]\n\\[\n= 2800 + 140 + 160 + 8\n\\]\n\\[\n= 2800 + 140 = 2940\n\\]\n\\[\n2940 + 160 = 3100\n\\]\n\\[\n3100 + 8 = 3108\n\\]\n\nVerification using the standard algorithm:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   \\hphantom{0}74 \\\\\n\\times 42 \\\\\n\\hline\n   148 \\quad (\\text{74 × 2}) \\\\\n+2960 \\quad (\\text{74 × 40, shifted one position left}) \\\\\n\\hline\n   3108 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n**Answer:** The product of 74 and 42 is \\boxed{3108}.', ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 74 and 42. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two-digit numbers can sometimes be a bit tricky, but I remember there are different methods to do it. Maybe I can use the standard algorithm, or perhaps break it down into smaller parts. Let me try both ways to make sure I get the right answer.\n\nFirst, the standard algorithm. I'll write 74 on top and 42 below it, aligning the digits by place value. So:\n\n   74\nx 42\n------\n\nStarting with the ones place of the bottom number, which is 2. Multiply 2 by 4 (the ones digit of 74) to get 8. Then 2 times 7 (the tens digit) is 14. So that gives me 148. Then, moving to the tens place of the bottom number, which is 4. But since it's the tens place, this 4 actually represents 40. So I need to multiply 74 by 40. To do this, I can multiply 74 by 4 and then add a zero at the end. 74 times 4 is... let's see, 70*4=280 and 4*4=16, so 280+16=296. Adding the zero gives 2960. Now, add the two results together: 148 + 2960. Let me add those. 148 + 2960... 148 + 2000 is 2148, then +960? Wait, no, that's not right. Wait, 2960 is 2000 + 900 + 60. Adding 148 to 2960: 2960 + 100 is 3060, then +48 is 3108. So total is 3108? Hmm, let me check that again because sometimes I might make a mistake in the steps.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the distributive property. 74 times 42 can be broken down as (70 + 4) * (40 + 2). Then expanding that, it's 70*40 + 70*2 + 4*40 + 4*2. Let's compute each part:\n\n70*40 is 2800.\n\n70*2 is 140.\n\n4*40 is 160.\n\n4*2 is 8.\n\nNow add all those together: 2800 + 140 = 2940; 2940 + 160 = 3100; 3100 + 8 = 3108. Okay, that matches the first method. So that seems correct.\n\nWait, but just to be thorough, maybe I can try another way. Maybe 74*42 is the same as 74*(40 + 2) which is 74*40 + 74*2. Let's compute those.\n\n74*40: 74*4=296, so times 10 is 2960.\n\n74*2=148.\n\nAdding those gives 2960 + 148 = 3108. Yep, same result. Okay, so that's three different ways and they all give 3108, so I think that's the right answer. Maybe I can check with a calculator if I have one, but since I'm doing this manually, I'll just confirm once more.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can reverse the multiplication: 42*74. Let's see if that gives the same result. 42*70 is 2940 and 42*4 is 168. Adding those together: 2940 + 168 = 3108. Yep, same answer. Alright, that seems solid. So the product of 74 and 42 is 3108.\n"]
