lus 280. Let's see, 860 + 200 is 1060, and then +80 more would be 1140. Wait, no, that's not right. Wait, 860 plus 280. Let me do it another way. 800 + 200 is 1000, and 60 + 80 is 140, so total is 1140. Yeah, that's correct. Then add the last number, which is 21. So 1140 +21 is 1161. \n\nHmm, so the total is 1161? Let me check if that makes sense. Alternatively, maybe I can do the multiplication the standard way, vertically, to confirm.\n\nLet me write 27  \ntimes 43  \n________\n\nStarting with the standard algorithm. Multiply 27 by 3 first. 7*3 is 21, write down 1 and carry over 2. Then 2*3 is 6 plus the carried 2 is 8. So that gives 81. Then, since the next digit is 4 (in the tens place), we need to shift one position to the left. So multiply 27 by 40. \n\nWait, actually, when doing the standard multiplication, you do 27 times 3 (units place) which is 81, then 27 times 4 (tens place, so it's actually 40), so 27*4 is 108, but shifted one place to the left, so 1080. Then add 81 + 1080. That would be 1161. Yeah, that's the same as before. \n\nAlternatively, maybe another way to check. Let's see, 27*43. Maybe break it into 27*(40 +3) which is 27*40 +27*3. 27*40: 27*4 is 108, so 108*10 is 1080. 27*3 is 81. Then 1080 +81 is 1161. Yep, same answer. \n\nHmm, seems like all methods are pointing towards 1161. Maybe another way is to use the difference of squares or something, but not sure if that applies here. Let me think. Alternatively, 27*43 can be thought of as (30 -3)*(40 +3). Wait, maybe that complicates things more, but let me try. \n\nExpanding (30 -3)(40 +3) = 30*40 +30*3 -3*40 -3*3. Which is 1200 +90 -120 -9. Calculating that: 1200 +90 is 1290, minus 120 is 1170, minus 9 is 1161. Oh, same result again. So that works too. \n\nAlright, so multiple methods give me 1161. I think that's the correct answer. Let me just confirm with a calculator in my mind. 27*40 is 1080, plus 27*3 is 81. 1080+81 is indeed 1161. Yep. So I think that's right. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, so I'm confident the product is 1161.\n"]
