Calculating 15×10 is straightforward, that's 150. Then 15×7 is 105, as before. Adding them gives 150 + 105 = 255. Okay, same result. \n\nHmm, maybe that's correct. Let me think if there's another way to verify. \n\nAlternatively, I can use the formula (a + b)(a - b) = a² - b², but wait, that's for difference of squares. Not sure if that applies here. Let me see, 15 and 17 are two numbers that are 1 apart from 16. Wait, 15 is 16-1 and 17 is 16+1. Oh, actually, that is a difference of squares scenario!\n\nWait, hold on. (16 -1)(16 +1) = 16² -1² = 256 -1 = 255. Oh! That's another way to see it. So 15×17 is indeed 255. \n\nSo all methods are giving me 255. I guess that's the right answer then. \n\nBut just to be thorough, maybe I can do the multiplication step by step again. Let me write it out vertically again:\n\n\u202f\xa015  \n×17  \n------  \nFirst, multiply 7 (from 17) by 15:\n\n7 × 5 = 35, write down 5 and carry over 3.  \n7 × 1 = 7, plus the carried 3 is 10. So the first partial product is 105.\n\nThen, multiply the 1 (from the tens place in 17, which is actually 10) by 15. Since it's the tens place, we can write a zero first in the ones place:\n\n1 × 5 = 5, but shifted one position to the left, so it's 50.  \n1 × 1 = 1, so 10 × 10 is 100? Wait, maybe I should think of it as 10 × 15. \n\nYes, 10 × 15 is 150. So writing that as the second partial product, shifted one place to the left. \n\nAdding 105 and 150 gives 255. Yep, that checks out. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using another method like the lattice method? Hmm, not sure, but let me try. \n\nIn lattice multiplication, you create a grid. Since both numbers are two digits, the grid would be 2x2. \n\nWrite 15 on top and 17 on the right side. \n\nMultiply each digit:\n\nTop left cell: 1 (from 15) × 1 (from 17) = 1. Split the cell with a diagonal, write 1 and 0? Wait, actually, maybe I need to do 1×1=1 and 1×7=7, but I'm getting confused. Maybe this isn't the best method here. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps breaking down the numbers differently. Let's see, 15 × 17. Since 15 is 10 +5, and 17 is 10 +7. So expanding (10+5)(10+7). \n\nMultiply term by term:\n\n10×10 = 100  \n10×7 = 70  \n5×10 =50  \n5×7 =35  \n\nAdding all those: 100 +70 is 170, plus 50 is 220, plus 35 is 255. Yep, same result again. \n\nAlright, so after multiple methods, I keep getting 255. I think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, so I'm confident the product of 15 and 17 is 255. \n\nJust to be 100% sure, maybe I can use a calculator? Wait, since I'm doing this mentally, let me check with another approach. \n\nAdding 15 seventeen times: 15+15=30 (2), 30+15=45 (3), 45+15=60 (4), 60+15=75 (5), 75+15=90 (6), 90+15=105 (7), 105+15=120 (8), 120+15=135 (9), 135+15=150 (10), 150+15=165 (11), 165+15=180 (12), 180+15=195 (13), 195+15=210 (14), 210+15=225 (15), 225+15=240 (16), 240+15=255 (17). \n\nOh wow, that worked too. Counting up 15, seventeen times gives 255. So that's another confirmation. \n\nAlright, I think that's more than enough verification. The answer is definitely 255.\n"]
