['Please solve the math problem.\nQuestion: What is the Product of 67 and 11?']
response:  {
  "id": "cmpl-e1f5cbb2bbd9454c894ac830c32f9064",
  "object": "text_completion",
  "created": 1750187611,
  "model": "QwQ-32B",
  "choices": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "text": ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 67 and 11. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying by 11 can sometimes have a trick, right? Like for two-digit numbers, you add the digits and put them in the middle. Wait, does that work here?\n\nLet me recall the trick. If you have a two-digit number, say AB, then multiplying by 11 is A followed by (A+B) followed by B. But what if A+B is more than 9? Oh right, then you carry over the extra. So let me try that with 67.\n\nFirst, the number is 67. So A is 6 and B is 7. Adding them gives 6+7=13. Hmm, that's more than 9, so I can't just put 13 in the middle directly. So how does the carrying work here? Let me see.\n\nThe standard method would be to do 67*10 + 67*1, which is 670 + 67 = 737. But let's see if the trick still works. If I do 6 (6+7) 7, that would be 6 13 7. Since 13 is two digits, I need to carry over the 1 to the previous digit. So the middle digit becomes 3 and add 1 to the hundreds place. Wait, the original hundreds place is 6, so adding 1 would make it 7. So then it becomes 737. Oh, that works! So the trick still applies even when the sum is more than 9, as long as you carry over. \n\nAlternatively, just to make sure, I can do the multiplication step by step. Let me write it out:\n\n   67\nx 11\n-----\n   67  (67 x 1)\n+670  (67 x 10)\n-----\n  737\n\nYep, adding 67 and 670 gives 737. Both methods give the same result, so that must be correct. I think that's the answer. Let me check with another method. Maybe breaking down 11 into 10 + 1 again, but that's the same as above. Alternatively, 67*11 is 67* (10 +1) so 670 +67 is indeed 737. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using the distributive property another way. Let's see. 67*11 = (60 +7)*11 = 60*11 + 7*11. 60*11 is 660, and 7*11 is 77. Adding those together: 660 +77 = 737. Yep, that also works. So three different methods all leading to the same answer. I think that's solid. \n\nI don't see any mistakes in my calculations. Let me just confirm once more with straight multiplication:\n\nMultiply 67 by 11. Starting from the right, 7*1=7, then 6*1=6, so the first partial product is 67. Then, moving to the tens place, 7*1 (but shifted one position) is 70, and 6*1 is 60, so adding those gives 670. Then adding 67 and 670 gives 737. \n\nAlternatively, in the standard algorithm:\n\n       67\n     x11\n     -----\n       67   (67 x 1)\n      67    (67 x 1, shifted one position to the left)\n     -----\n      737\n\nAdding those two lines: 67 + 670 = 737. Yep, that's correct. \n\nAnother way: using the area model. 60*10=600, 60*1=60, 7*10=70, 7*1=7. Adding all those: 600+60=660, 70+7=77, then 660+77=737. \n\nI think I'm convinced that 67 multiplied by 11 is 737. All the different methods cross-check to the same result. So the product is 737.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 67 and 11 is calculated as follows:\n\nUsing the distributive property:  \n67 \u00d7 11 = 67 \u00d7 (10 + 1)  \n= (67 \u00d7 10) + (67 \u00d7 1)  \n= 670 + 67  \n= **737**\n\nAlternatively, applying the multiplication algorithm:  \n```\n   67\n\u00d7 11\n-----\n   67   (67 \u00d7 1)\n+670   (67 \u00d7 10)\n-----\n  737\n```\n\n**Answer:** 737",
      "logprobs": null,
      "finish_reason": "stop",
      "stop_reason": null,
      "prompt_logprobs": null
