y 1 (the tens place): 6*1=6, but since it's the tens place, it's actually 60. Wait, no, actually when you multiply by the tens digit, you have to shift one place. So 1 (from 13) times 6 is 6, but in the tens place, so it's 60. Then 1 times 8 is 8, but in the hundreds place, so 800. So 860. Then adding 258 + 860.\n\nWait, perhaps I should write it out as:\n\nFirst partial product:\n86  \nx\xa03  \n---  \n258  \n\nSecond partial product (since the 1 is in the tens place, it's 10):\n\xa0\xa086  \nx10  \n----  \n\xa0860  \n\nThen adding them:\n\n\xa0258  \n+860  \n-----  \n1118  \n\nYes, that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, if I do the multiplication in a different order, like 13 times 86. Let's see if that changes anything. \n\n13  \nx86  \n------  \n13*6 = 78  \n13*80 = 1040  \nAdding them: 78 + 1040 = 1118. Yep, same answer. \n\nAlternatively, breaking down 86 into 80 +6 and 13 into 10 +3, then using the distributive property as I did before. \n\nI think I've checked this multiple ways, so I'm confident that the product of 86 and 13 is 1118. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, and all methods cross-validate the result. So I think that's the correct answer.\n"]
