confirms it. So the product is 6633. \n\nI think that's correct. Let me just do a quick estimation to check. 100 times 67 is 6700, so 99 times 67 should be 67 less, which is exactly what we did. So that makes sense. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using another method. Let's see, breaking down 67 into 60 + 7, but that's similar to the first method. Wait, actually, that's exactly what the standard algorithm does. \n\nAlternatively, using the distributive property: 99*67 = (90 + 9)*(60 +7). But that might be more complicated, but let's try it for verification. \n\nExpanding that: 90*60 + 90*7 + 9*60 + 9*7. \n\nCalculating each term:\n\n90*60 = 5400,\n\n90*7 = 630,\n\n9*60 = 540,\n\n9*7 = 63.\n\nAdding all those together: 5400 + 630 = 6030; 540 + 63 = 603. Then 6030 + 603 = 6633. \n\nSame result again. Okay, so that's three different methods all leading to 6633. I feel confident that's the right answer. \n\nJust to be thorough, maybe do the multiplication straight up:\n\n   99\nx 67\n------\nFirst multiply 99 by 7: 693\n\nThen multiply 99 by 60 (shift one position to the left):\n\n  5940\n\nAdding them:\n\n  693\n+5940\n------\n 6633\n\nYep, same as before. Alright, so I think the answer is 6633. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, and all methods cross-check to the same result.\n"]
