final_output:  ['\n\nThe product of 90 and 64 is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\n90 \\times 64 = (90 \\times 60) + (90 \\times 4)\n\\]\n\nFirst, compute \\(90 \\times 60\\):\n\\[\n90 \\times 60 = 5400\n\\]\n\nNext, compute \\(90 \\times 4\\):\n\\[\n90 \\times 4 = 360\n\\]\n\nAdd the two results together:\n\\[\n5400 + 360 = 5760\n\\]\n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{5760}', ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 90 and 64. Hmm, let me think about how to approach this. Multiplying two numbers... right, that's straightforward, but maybe I should break it down to make sure I don't make a mistake. \n\nFirst, I know that 90 times 64 is the same as 90 multiplied by 64. Maybe I can use the standard multiplication algorithm here. Let me write it out step by step. \n\nStarting with the numbers:\n\n\u2003\u200390  \n×\xa064  \n------  \n\nI remember that when multiplying by hand, you start with the digit in the ones place of the second number. So 4 times 90. Let me calculate that first. \n\n4 times 90: Well, 4 times 9 is 36, and then since there's a zero at the end of 90, I add another zero, making it 360. So that part is 360. \n\nNext, I need to multiply the 6 in the tens place of 64 by 90. But wait, since the 6 is in the tens place, it's actually 60. So I have to remember to shift one position to the left when writing the result. Let me do that. \n\n6 times 90: 6 times 9 is 54, and then adding a zero gives 540. But since this is the tens place, I need to add a zero at the end, making it 5400? Wait, no, hold on. Maybe I confused the steps here. Let me clarify.\n\nActually, when you multiply by the tens digit (6 in this case), you should first multiply 6 by 90, which is 540, and then because it's the tens place, you shift the result one place to the left, which is equivalent to multiplying by 10. So 540 times 10 is 5400. Hmm, but that seems a bit off. Wait, maybe I need to think of it as 60 times 90 instead of 6 times 90. Let me recast the problem.\n\nAlternatively, maybe breaking down the numbers into smaller parts would help. Let's see, 90 times 64 can be thought of as (90 × 60) + (90 × 4). That's the distributive property. That might be easier to compute.\n\nCalculating 90 × 60 first: 90 times 60. 90 times 6 is 540, so adding another zero gives 5400. Then 90 times 4 is 360. Adding those two results together: 5400 + 360. Let's see, 5400 plus 300 is 5700, then plus 60 more is 5760. So that would give 5760. \n\nWait a second, that seems different from the first approach. Wait, in my first approach, I had 4×90=360, then 6×90=540 shifted by one, so 5400, then adding 360 + 5400 would also be 5760. Okay, so both methods give the same result. That's reassuring. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method. Let's try the standard multiplication again step by step to confirm.\n\nMultiplying 90 by 4 (the ones place of 64):\n\n90  \n×\xa0\xa04  \n------  \n360  \n\nThen multiplying 90 by 60 (since the 6 is in the tens place, so it's 60):\n\n90  \n×60  \n------  \n5400  \n\nNow add those two results together: 360 + 5400 = 5760. Yep, same answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use some other multiplication tricks. For instance, 90 is 9 times 10, and 64 is 64. So 9×64=576, then multiply by 10 to get 5760. That's another way. Let me check that:\n\n9 × 64: 9×60=540, 9×4=36, so 540+36=576. Then 576×10=5760. That works too. \n\nHmm, all these methods lead me to 5760. I think that's the correct answer. Let me just verify with a calculator approach in my mind. \n\nAlternatively, if I think of 64 × 90, since multiplication is commutative, it's the same as 64×90. Let me compute that way. 64×90. Again, 64×9 is 576, then add a zero to get 5760. Yep, same result. \n\nWait, maybe I can also use the fact that 90 is 100 - 10. So 64 × (100 -10) = 64×100 -64×10 = 6400 - 640 = 5760. That's another method. \n\nBreaking it down that way also gives me 5760. Okay, so all these different methods are confirming the same answer. I think that's solid. \n\nI guess the key here is to make sure that when multiplying by the tens place, you remember to shift the result appropriately. In the initial step-by-step multiplication, that's crucial. Let me just go through that again once more to be thorough.\n\nFirst line: 90 × 4. \n\nStarting from the rightmost digit of 64, which is 4. Multiply 4 × 0 = 0, write that down. Then 4 ×9 = 36. So that gives 360.\n\nSecond line: 90 ×6, but since the 6 is in the tens place, this is actually 60 ×90. So when writing this, you need to put a zero in the ones place first, then multiply 6 ×0 =0, and 6×9=54. So that gives 5400. Then adding 360 +5400 gives 5760. \n\nAlternatively, if I do it in a vertical format:\n\n\u2003\u2003\xa090  \n×\xa064  \n------  \n\u2003360  (this is 90 ×4)  \n+5400 (this is 90 ×60)  \n------  \n\xa05760  \n\nYes, that's exactly how it works. So the total is 5760. \n\nAnother way to check could be to estimate first. 90 is close to 90, 64 is close to 60. 90×60=5400, and since 64 is a bit more than 60, the actual result should be a bit higher. 5760 is 360 more than 5400, which makes sense because 64 is 4 more than 60, so 90×4=360. That checks out. \n\nAlternatively, if I think of 90 as 9×10 and 64 as 64×1, then multiplying all together: 9×64×10. 9×64 is 576, then times 10 is 5760. \n\nI think I'm confident that the answer is 5760. I don't see any miscalculations in my steps, and multiple methods lead to the same result. So I think that's it.\n"]
