      "text": "\nOkay, let me try to figure out the product of 44 and 39. Hmm, I need to multiply these two numbers. Let me recall how multiplication works. So, 44 times 39. Maybe I can break it down into smaller parts to make it easier. \n\nFirst, I know that 44 is 40 plus 4, and 39 is 30 plus 9. Maybe using the distributive property here would help. Let me try that. So, (40 + 4) multiplied by (30 + 9). That would be 40*30 plus 40*9 plus 4*30 plus 4*9. Let me calculate each part step by step.\n\nStarting with 40 times 30. 40 times 30 is like 4 times 3 times 10 times 10, which is 12 times 100, so 1200. Okay, that part is straightforward. \n\nNext, 40 multiplied by 9. 40 times 9, well 4 times 9 is 36, so adding a zero gives 360. Got that. \n\nThen, 4 times 30. That's similar to the previous step. 4 times 3 is 12, so times 10 gives 120. \n\nFinally, 4 times 9. That's easy, 36. \n\nNow, adding all those together: 1200 + 360 + 120 + 36. Let me add them step by step. \n\nFirst, 1200 plus 360 is 1560. Then, adding 120 to that gives 1560 + 120 = 1680. Then, adding the last 36: 1680 + 36 = 1716. \n\nWait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Let's see:\n\n40*30 = 1200,\n\n40*9 = 360,\n\n4*30 = 120,\n\n4*9 = 36.\n\nAdding them up: 1200 + 360 is 1560, then 1560 + 120 is 1680, and 1680 + 36 is indeed 1716. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another way to check. Maybe I can do 44 times 40, which is 1760, and then subtract 44 from that because 44 times 39 is 44 times (40 -1). So, 44*40 = 1760, minus 44 gives 1760 - 44 = 1716. Yep, same result. That seems to confirm it. \n\nAnother way to verify could be using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n   44\nx 39\n------\nFirst multiply 44 by 9: 44*9. Let's see, 40*9=360 and 4*9=36, so 360+36=396.\n\nThen multiply 44 by 30. 44*30 is 44*3*10. 44*3 is 132, so times 10 is 1320.\n\nAdding those two results: 396 + 1320. Let's add them. 396 + 1320. 1320 + 300 is 1620, plus 96 more is 1716. \n\nSame answer again. Okay, so all methods are pointing to 1716. I think that's the correct product. \n\nJust to be thorough, maybe I can use a calculator if I have one, but since I'm doing this manually, the multiple methods all leading to 1716 gives me confidence. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. So, I think the answer is 1716.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 44 and 39 can be calculated using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n44 \\times 39 = (40 + 4) \\times (30 + 9)\n\\]\n\nExpanding this:\n\n\\[\n= 40 \\times 30 + 40 \\times 9 + 4 \\times 30 + 4 \\times 9\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term:\n- \\(40 \\times 30 = 1200\\)\n- \\(40 \\times 9 = 360\\)\n- \\(4 \\times 30 = 120\\)\n- \\(4 \\times 9 = 36\\)\n\nAdding them together:\n\\[\n1200 + 360 + 120 + 36 = 1716\n\\]\n\n**Verification**:\n- Alternative method: \\(44 \\times 39 = 44 \\times (40 - 1) = 44 \\times 40 - 44 = 1760 - 44 = 1716\\)\n- Standard multiplication:\n  - \\(44 \\times 9 = 396\\)\n  - \\(44 \\times 30 = 1320\\)\n  - Sum: \\(396 + 1320 = 1716\\)\n\n**Final Answer**: \\(\\boxed{1716}\\)",
