      "text": ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 98 and 55. Hmm, let's see. Multiplication, right? But 98 is close to 100, maybe there's a trick to make this easier instead of multiplying straight up. Let me think... Oh, right! The distributive property could help here. If I break down 98 into 100 minus 2, then multiply each part by 55 and add the results. That might be simpler.\n\nSo, 98 times 55 is the same as (100 - 2) times 55. Applying the distributive property: 100*55 minus 2*55. Let me calculate each part. \n\nFirst, 100 multiplied by 55. Well, multiplying by 100 is straightforward; you just add two zeros. So 55*100 is 5500. Then, 2 times 55 is 110. Now subtract the second result from the first: 5500 - 110. \n\nWait, 5500 minus 100 would be 5400, but since it's 110, that's 10 more. So 5400 minus 10 is 5390. Let me check that again to be sure. 5500 - 110. Breaking it down, 5500 - 100 is 5400, then subtract 10 more gives 5390. Yep, that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do it the standard way to confirm. Let's try multiplying 98 by 55 the traditional multiplication method. \n\nFirst, write 98\ntimes 55\n________\n\nMultiply 98 by 5 (the units digit of 55): 98*5. 90*5 is 450, and 8*5 is 40, so 450+40=490. \n\nThen multiply 98 by 50 (the tens digit, so actually 50). 98*50. Well, 98*5 is 490, so times 10 more is 4900. \n\nNow add those two results together: 490 + 4900. That's 5390. Okay, same answer. Good, that matches the first method. \n\nAnother way, maybe using some other trick? Let's see. 55 is half of 110, but not sure if that helps. Alternatively, 55 is 50 +5, so maybe breaking it that way. Wait, but I already did that in the first method. Either way, both methods give me 5390. \n\nLet me just do a quick estimation to check if 5390 is reasonable. 100 times 55 is 5500, so 98 is 2 less, so 2*55=110 less. 5500-110=5390. That makes sense. So I think that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another approach. Let's see, 98 * 55. If I switch the numbers, 55*98, same result. Maybe 55*100 is 5500, minus 55*2=110, so again 5500-110=5390. Yep, same thing. \n\nI think I've verified it enough ways. The answer should be 5390.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 98 and 55 is calculated as follows:\n\nFirst, use the distributive property by breaking down 98 into (100 - 2):\n\n\\[\n98 \\times 55 = (100 - 2) \\times 55\n\\]\n\nApply the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n= 100 \\times 55 - 2 \\times 55\n\\]\n\nCalculate each term:\n\n\\[\n100 \\times 55 = 5500 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 2 \\times 55 = 110\n\\]\n\nSubtract the second result from the first:\n\n\\[\n5500 - 110 = 5390\n\\]\n\n**Verification using standard multiplication:**\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   \\hphantom{0}98 \\\\\n\\times 55 \\\\\n\\hline\n   490 \\quad (\\text{98 \u00d7 5}) \\\\\n+4900 \\quad (\\text{98 \u00d7 50, shifted one position left}) \\\\\n\\hline\n   5390 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the result is consistent.\n\n**Final Answer:**  \nThe product of 98 and 55 is \\boxed{5390}.",
