 540+72=612. So 68*90 is 6120. Then divide by 2 gives 3060. Oh, that works too. So that's another method.\n\nHmm, so all these different methods are leading me to the same answer, 3060, so I feel confident that's the right product. Let me just do a quick estimation to check if it's in the ballpark. 68 is close to 70, and 45 is close to 50. 70*50 is 3500. Since both numbers were rounded up, the actual product should be less than that, which 3060 is. So that makes sense. Alternatively, 60*40 is 2400, and adding the extra parts, yeah, 3060 is reasonable.\n\nWait, another thought: if I do 68 multiplied by 45, maybe I can think of it as 68 times 45 divided by 1, but that might not help. Alternatively, perhaps use the commutative property, switching the numbers. 45*68 is the same as 68*45. Let me try that way just for kicks. 45*68. Using the standard algorithm again:\n\n45\nx68\n______\n\nStarting with the ones digit: 8*45. 8*5=40, write 0, carryover 4. 8*4=32 plus 4 is 36. So 360.\n\nThen the tens digit: 6 (which is 60) times 45. So write a zero first. 6*5=30, write 0, carryover 3. 6*4=24 plus 3 is 27. So 2700.\n\nAdding 360 + 2700 = 3060. Yep, same result.\n\nAlright, so after trying multiple methods—distributive property, standard algorithm, breaking numbers into different components, estimation—it all checks out. The product of 68 and 45 is definitely 3060. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, and all the different approaches cross-validate each other. I think that's solid.\n"]
