final_output:  ['\n\nTo find the product of 78 and 68, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n(70 + 8)(60 + 8)\n\\]\n\nExpanding this using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n70 \\times 60 + 70 \\times 8 + 8 \\times 60 + 8 \\times 8\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n\n\\[\n70 \\times 60 = 4200\n\\]\n\\[\n70 \\times 8 = 560\n\\]\n\\[\n8 \\times 60 = 480\n\\]\n\\[\n8 \\times 8 = 64\n\\]\n\nAdding all these terms together:\n\n\\[\n4200 + 560 + 480 + 64\n\\]\n\nFirst, add \\(4200 + 560\\):\n\n\\[\n4200 + 560 = 4760\n\\]\n\nNext, add \\(4760 + 480\\):\n\n\\[\n4760 + 480 = 5240\n\\]\n\nThen, add \\(5240 + 64\\):\n\n\\[\n5240 + 64 = 5304\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can use the standard multiplication method:\n\n\\[\n78 \\times 68\n\\]\n\nFirst, multiply 78 by 8:\n\n\\[\n78 \\times 8 = 624\n\\]\n\nThen, multiply 78 by 60 (since 68 is 60 + 8):\n\n\\[\n78 \\times 60 = 4680\n\\]\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n624 + 4680 = 5304\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 78 and 68 is \\(\\boxed{5304}\\).', ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 78 and 68. Hmm, let me think about how to do this. I remember that multiplying two numbers can sometimes be done by breaking them down into smaller parts. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. Let me try that.\n\nFirst, I'll write 78 as 70 + 8 and 68 as 60 + 8. Wait, actually, maybe breaking them into tens and units would work. Alternatively, maybe there's a trick for numbers close to 70 or something. Wait, maybe the standard multiplication method is the way to go here. Let me try that.\n\nAlternatively, I could use the formula (a + b)(c + d) = ac + ad + bc + bd. Let me see. Let me try that approach. Let me set a = 70, b = 8, c = 60, d = 8. Wait, no, 68 is 60 + 8, so yes. So (70 + 8)(60 + 8) = 70*60 + 70*8 + 8*60 + 8*8. Let me compute each term step by step.\n\nFirst term: 70 times 60. 70*60 is... well, 7*6 is 42, so 70*60 is 4200. Okay.\n\nSecond term: 70*8. That's 560.\n\nThird term: 8*60. That's