8. \n\nBut let me verify that another way to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Maybe using the distributive property: (30 + 8) \u00d7 (60 + 1). Expanding that would be 30\u00d760 + 30\u00d71 + 8\u00d760 + 8\u00d71. Calculating each term:\n\n30\u00d760 = 1800,\n\n30\u00d71 = 30,\n\n8\u00d760 = 480,\n\n8\u00d71 = 8.\n\nAdding them all up: 1800 + 30 = 1830; 1830 + 480 = 2310; 2310 +8 = 2318. Okay, that matches the previous result. So that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method, like the lattice method or some shortcut? Let me see. Alternatively, maybe breaking down the numbers differently. For example, 38 \u00d7 61. Hmm, 38 is close to 40, and 61 is close to 60. Maybe using the idea of (40 - 2)(60 +1). Let me try that.\n\nExpanding (40 - 2)(60 +1) = 40\u00d760 + 40\u00d71 - 2\u00d760 -2\u00d71.\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n40\u00d760 = 2400,\n\n40\u00d71 = 40,\n\n-2\u00d760 = -120,\n\n-2\u00d71 = -2.\n\nAdding those together: 2400 +40 = 2440; 2440 -120 = 2320; 2320 -2 = 2318. Yep, same answer again. So that's consistent. \n\nAnother way: Maybe 38 \u00d760 is 2280, then add 38 more, since 61 is 60 +1. So 2280 +38 = 2318. That's the same as before. So seems like 2318 is the right answer.\n\nWait, but just to be absolutely sure, maybe I can do straight multiplication again.\n\n38\n\n\u00d761\n\nMultiply 38 by 1: 38\n\nMultiply 38 by 60: 38\u00d760=2280\n\nAdd them: 38+2280=2318. Yep.\n\nAlternatively, if I do the multiplication step by step:\n\nStarting with the units digit of the lower number (assuming 61 is the lower number). Wait, actually, in vertical multiplication, it's usually the second number written below. So:\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a038\n\n\u00d7\u00a061\n\n--------\n\nFirst, multiply 38 by 1 (units place): 38\n\nThen multiply 38 by 6 (tens place, so it's actually 60). So 38\u00d76=228. But since it's the tens place, you write 228 shifted one to the left, so it's 2280.\n\nAdd 38 +2280 = 2318. Correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using calculator verification? But since I can't use a calculator, but I can do step-by-step again.\n\nAlternatively, maybe check with another method: 38\u00d761.\n\nLet me think of 38\u00d761 as 38\u00d7(60 +1) = 38\u00d760 +38\u00d71 = 2280 +38 = 2318.\n\nAlternatively, 61\u00d738, same thing. Let me reverse the numbers to see if I get the same result.\n\n61\u00d738:\n\n61\u00d730 = 1830,\n\n61\u00d78 = 488,\n\nAdding 1830 +488 = 2318. Yep, same answer. So that's good.\n\nHmm, so all methods lead to 2318, so I think that's the correct product.\n\nWait, just to be thorough, maybe I can do the multiplication digit by digit.\n\nLet me write it out:\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a038\n\n\u00d7\u00a061\n\n--------\n\nFirst line: 1\u00d738 = 38\n\nSecond line: 60\u00d738 = 2280 (since 6 is in the tens place, so 6\u00d710, so 6\u00d738\u00d710)\n\nAdding the two lines:\n\n38\n\n+2280\n\n------\n\n2318\n\nYes, that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, writing it as:\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a038\n\n\u00d7\u00a061\n\n--------\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a038 (this is 38\u00d71)\n\n+2280 (this is 38\u00d760, shifted one position to the left)\n\n--------\n\n2318\n\nPerfect. \n\nI think I've verified it multiple ways. So I'm confident the answer is 2318.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 38 and 61 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Break down the multiplication** using the standard algorithm:\n   - Multiply 38 by 1 (units place of 61):  \n     \\( 38 \\times 1 = 38 \\)\n   - Multiply 38 by 60 (tens place of 61, represented by the digit 6):  \n     \\( 38 \\times 60 = 2,\\!280 \\)\n\n2. **Add the results** of the two partial products:  \n   \\( 38 + 2,\\!280 = 2,\\!318 \\)\n\n**Verification using alternative methods** (distributive property, reverse multiplication, and decomposition) confirms the result remains consistent.\n\n**Answer:** The product of 38 and 61 is \\(\\boxed{2318}\\).",
