t, write down 31.\nThen, multiply by 100. Since 100 has two zeros, adding two zeros to 31 gives 3100. \n\nAlternatively, in the standard algorithm:\n\n   31\nx100\n-----\n   0 (31 x 0)\n  0  (31 x 0, shifted one position to the left)\n31  (31 x 1, shifted two positions to the left)\n-----\n3100\n\nWait, actually, when multiplying by the hundreds place, the 1 in 100 is in the hundreds place, so it's 31 x 1 x 100, which is 31 x 100. But that's the same as shifting two zeros. \n\nEither way, all the methods confirm that 31 multiplied by 100 is 3100. I think that's solid. \n\nLet me check with a calculator. Hmm, if I don't have a calculator, maybe do a smaller version. Like 3 times 100 is 300, so 30 times 100 is 3000, plus 1 times 100 is 100, so 3000 + 100 is 3100. Yep, that's consistent. \n\nAnother angle: 100 is 10^2, so 31 * 10^2 = 31 followed by two zeros. Exactly 3100. \n\nI can't see any mistake here. Maybe if I reverse it: 100 times 31. Same thing. 100 + 100... 31 times. But that's 31 hundreds, which is 3100. \n\nAlright, I think I've verified it multiple ways. The answer should be 3100.\n"]
