final_output:  ['\n\nThe product of 87 and 10 is calculated by multiplying the two numbers together. \n\n**Step-by-Step Calculation:**\n1. **Multiply 87 by 10:**  \n   \\( 87 \\times 10 = 870 \\).\n\n**Answer:**  \n\\boxed{870}', ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 87 and 10. Let me think. The product means multiplication, right? So, 87 multiplied by 10. Hmm, multiplying by 10 is usually straightforward. I remember that when you multiply a number by 10, you just add a zero at the end of that number. Let me test that.\n\nIf I take 87 and add a zero, it becomes 870. Let me check if that's correct. Alternatively, I can break it down. 87 times 10 is the same as 87 plus itself ten times. But that's tedious. Maybe using place value helps. 87 is 8 tens and 7 ones. Multiplying by 10 would shift the digits one place to the left, so 8 tens become 8 hundreds, and 7 ones become 7 tens. So that would be 800 + 70 = 870. Yeah, that makes sense. \n\nAlternatively, using the standard algorithm: \n\n   87\nx 10\n-----\n   00  (87 x 0)\n+870   (87 x 10, since the 1 is in the tens place)\n-----\n  870\n\nYep, that adds up. So the product should be 870. I don't think I made any mistakes here. Multiplying by 10 is pretty straightforward, but it's good I double-checked with different methods. All methods give the same result, so I'm confident the answer is 870.\n"]
