11 is definitely 143. I feel confident about this.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 13 and 11 is \\boxed{143}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 13 and 11, we can use multiple methods to verify the result:\n\n1. **Using the 11-multiplication trick**:\n   - For a two-digit number \\(ab\\), multiplying by 11 gives \\(a(a+b)b\\). \n   - Here, \\(13 \\times 11\\): the digits are 1 and 3. Adding them gives \\(1 + 3 = 4\\). Placing 4 between 1 and 3 results in 143.\n\n2. **Standard multiplication**:\n   - \n       13  \n     x 11  \n     -----  \n       13   (13 x 1)  \n      130   (13 x 10, shifted left)  \n     -----  \n      143  \n\n3. **Distributive property**:\n   - \\(13 \\times 11 = 13 \\times (10 + 1) = 13 \\times 10 + 13 \\times 1 = 130 + 13 = 143\\).\n\n4. **Expanding the numbers**:\n   - \\(13 = 10 + 3\\) and \\(11 = 10 + 1\\).\n   - \\((10 + 3)(10 + 1) = 10 \\times 10 + 10 \\times 1 + 3 \\times 10 + 3 \\times 1 = 100 + 10 + 30 + 3 = 143\\).\n\nAll methods confirm the result is the same. \n\n### Final Answer\nThe product of 13 and 11 is \\boxed{143}.",
