 130 = 143. Okay, that matches the trick's result. So that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method, like the distributive property: 13 * (10 + 1) = 13*10 + 13*1 = 130 + 13 = 143. Yep, same answer.\n\nWait, but just to be thorough, what if I do it step by step the long multiplication way:\n\n       13\n     x11\n     -----\n       13   (this is 13 x 1)\n      13    (this is 13 x 10, shifted one position to the left)\n     -----\n      143\n\nAdding those two lines gives 143. Okay, so all methods point to 143. Therefore, the product should be 143.\n\nBut let me just confirm once more with another approach. Maybe breaking down the numbers:\n\n13 is 10 + 3, and 11 is 10 +1. So multiplying them:\n\n(10 +3)(10 +1) = 10*10 + 10*1 + 3*10 + 3*1 = 100 +10 +30 +3 = 143. Yep, same result.\n\nAlright, I think that's solid. All different methods give the same answer. I don't see any mistakes in my reasoning here. So the product of 13 and 11 is definitely 143. I feel confident about this.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 13 and 11 is \\boxed{143}.\n"]
