      "text": ":\nOkay, let me try to figure out the product of 19 and 83. Hmm, I remember that multiplying two numbers means adding one number to itself the other number of times. But doing 19 times 83 directly might be a bit tedious. Maybe there's a smarter way to break it down?\n\nWait, maybe I can use the distributive property here. Let's see, 19 is close to 20, which is easier to work with. So, if I think of 19 as (20 - 1), then 19 times 83 would be the same as (20 - 1) * 83. That should make the calculation simpler.\n\nLet me write that out: (20 - 1) * 83. Using the distributive property, this becomes 20*83 minus 1*83. Okay, so first I need to calculate 20 multiplied by 83. \n\n20 times 80 is 1600, right? And then 20 times 3 is 60. So adding those together, 1600 + 60 = 1660. So 20*83 is 1660. \n\nNow subtract 1 times 83, which is just 83. So 1660 minus 83. Hmm, let me do that subtraction step by step. \n\nStarting with 1660 - 80 would be 1580, and then subtract 3 more, so 1580 - 3 = 1577. Wait, is that right? Let me check again. 1660 minus 83. Alternatively, maybe I can think of 83 as 80 +3. So 1660 - 80 is 1580, then subtract 3 gives 1577. Yeah, that seems correct.\n\nBut let me verify the multiplication another way to be sure. Maybe using the standard algorithm. Let's multiply 19 by 83.\n\nFirst, write 19  \n       x83  \n       ----  \n\nMultiply 19 by 3: 19*3 = 57.  \nThen multiply 19 by 80: 19*80 = 1520.  \nAdd those two results together: 57 + 1520 = 1577.  \n\nOkay, that matches the previous result. So that's good. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method, like the lattice method or breaking both numbers into smaller parts. Let me try breaking 83 into 80 +3 and 19 into 10 +9. Then use the FOIL method?\n\nSo (10 + 9)*(80 +3)  \nFirst: 10*80 = 800  \nOuter: 10*3 = 30  \nInner: 9*80 = 720  \nLast: 9*3 =27  \nAdd them all up: 800 + 30 = 830; 720 +27 =747. Then 830 +747 = 1577. \n\nSame answer again. Hmm, that gives me more confidence. \n\nWait another thought: 19*83 is the same as 83*19, so maybe if I do 83*20 and subtract 83 once. That's actually the same as the first method I used. 83*20 is 1660, subtract 83 gives 1577. Yep, same result.\n\nI think that's consistent across different methods. Let me just do a quick check with approximate values. 20 times 80 is 1600, so 19 is a bit less than 20, and 83 is a bit more than 80. The exact value should be a little less than 1600? Wait, no, actually 19*83 versus 20*80. Hmm, maybe my approximation isn't the best here. Alternatively, 20*83 is 1660, so subtracting 83 gives 1577. Since 1577 is 83 less than 1660, that makes sense. \n\nAlternatively, maybe use a calculator if I had one, but since I'm doing this manually, all the methods cross-check to 1577. I think that's the right answer. \n\nWait, just to be thorough, let me add 19 eighty-three times. But that would take too long. Alternatively, maybe split 83 into 80 +3 as before. 19*80 is 1520, and 19*3 is 57, so adding them gives 1577. Yep, that's the same as before. \n\nI can't see any mistakes in these calculations. All the different methods lead to the same result. So I think the product of 19 and 83 is 1577.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 19 and 83 is calculated as follows:\n\n**Step 1:** Use the distributive property by expressing 19 as (20 - 1):  \n\\[\n(20 - 1) \\times 83 = 20 \\times 83 - 1 \\times 83\n\\]\n\n**Step 2:** Compute \\(20 \\times 83\\):  \n\\[\n20 \\times 80 = 1,\\!600 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 20 \\times 3 = 60  \n\\]  \nAdding these gives:  \n\\[\n1,\\!600 + 60 = 1,\\!660\n\\]\n\n**Step 3:** Subtract \\(1 \\times 83\\) from the result:  \n\\[\n1,\\!660 - 83 = 1,\\!577\n\\]\n\n**Verification via standard multiplication:**  \n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   \\quad 19 \\\\\n\\times 83 \\\\\n\\hline\n   \\quad 57 \\quad (\\text{19 \u00d7 3}) \\\\\n+ 1,\\!520 \\quad (\\text{19 \u00d7 80, shifted left}) \\\\\n\\hline\n   1,\\!577 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n**Final Answer:**  \nThe product of 19 and 83 is \\(\\boxed{1577}\\).",
