final_output:  ['\n\nTo find the product of 67 and 21, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n67 \\times 21 = 67 \\times (20 + 1) = (67 \\times 20) + (67 \\times 1)\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate \\(67 \\times 20\\):\n\n\\[\n67 \\times 20 = 67 \\times 2 \\times 10 = 134 \\times 10 = 1340\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate \\(67 \\times 1\\):\n\n\\[\n67 \\times 1 = 67\n\\]\n\nAdd the two results together:\n\n\\[\n1340 + 67 = 1407\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can use another method, such as breaking down the numbers:\n\n\\[\n67 \\times 21 = (60 + 7) \\times (20 + 1)\n\\]\n\nExpanding this using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n(60 \\times 20) + (60 \\times 1) + (7 \\times 20) + (7 \\times 1)\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n\\[\n60 \\times 20 = 1200, \\quad 60 \\times 1 = 60, \\quad 7 \\times 20 = 140, \\quad 7 \\times 1 = 7\n\\]\n\nAdding these together:\n\n\\[\n1200 + 60 + 140 + 7 = 1407\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 67 and 21 is \\(\\boxed{1407}\\).', ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 67 and 21. Hmm, let's see. Multiplication, right? Alright, let me think about how to approach this. I remember that multiplying two numbers means adding one number to itself as many times as the other number says. But since these are both two-digit numbers, maybe there's a better way than just adding 67 twenty-one times. That would take forever!\n\nWait, maybe I can break it down using the distributive property. Yeah, that's a thing. So, 21 is the same as 20 plus 1. So if I do 67 times 20 and then add 67 times 1, that should give me the same result. Let me write that out:\n\n67 × 21 = 67 × (20 + 1) = (67 × 20) + (67 × 1)\n\nAlright, let's calculate each part separately. Starting with 67 times 20. Hmm, 67 times 2 is 134, right? So if I multiply that by 10, since 20 is 2 times 10, that would be 134 × 10 = 1340. Okay, so 67 × 20 is 1340.\n\nNow, the second part is 67 × 1, which is straightforward. That's just 67.\n\nNow, adding those two results together: 1340 + 67. Let me 