d to each digit. Let me structure it properly.\n\nThe top number is 67, so the top digits are 6 and 7. The right number is 21, so the side digits are 2 and 1 (but actually, in lattice, the side is usually on the left, so perhaps 2 and 1 are on the left, and the top is 6 and 7).\n\nWait, maybe I'm getting confused. Let me recall the lattice method steps.\n\nActually, here's how it works: you write the first number across the top, each digit in its own box, and the second number down the right side, each digit in its own box. Then each cell is split by a diagonal, and you multiply the digits, putting the tens in the upper part and the units in the lower part. Then you add along the diagonals from bottom to top, right to left.\n\nWait, perhaps an example would help, but since I can't draw, I'll try mentally.\n\nLet me set it up:\n\nTop: 6 and 7 (the digits of 67)\nRight: 2 and 1 (the digits of 21)\n\nSo the grid has two rows and two columns. Each cell is at the intersection of a top digit and a right digit.\n\nSo the four cells are:\n\nTop left cell: 6 (top) and 2 (right)\nTop right cell: 7 (top) and 2 (right)\nBottom left cell: 6 (top) and 1 (right)\nBottom right cell: 7 (top) and 1 (right)\n\nNow, multiply each pair:\n\nTop left: 6×2=12 → 1 (tens) and 2 (units)\nTop right: 7×2=14 → 1 and 4\nBottom left: 6×1=6 → 0 and 6\nBottom right:7×1=7 → 0 and7\n\nNow, arrange these in the cells:\n\nTop left cell: 1 (upper) / 2 (lower)\nTop right cell: 1 /4\nBottom left cell:0/6\nBottom right cell:0/7\n\nNow, the diagonals to add:\n\nStarting from the bottom right, the first diagonal is just the lower part of the bottom right cell:7.\n\nNext diagonal is the lower part of bottom left (6) plus the upper part of bottom right (0) → 6+0=6. Wait, actually, maybe the diagonals are formed by the diagonals of the cells. Let me think again.\n\nIn lattice multiplication, the diagonals are drawn from top-right to bottom-left, splitting each cell. The sum is done along these diagonals, starting from the bottom-rightmost diagonal.\n\nSo, each diagonal line (from bottom-right to top-left) will have numbers to add.\n\nLet me see the cells arranged as:\n\nRow 1 (top row): [ (1/2) , (1/4) ]\n\nRow 2 (bottom row): [ (0/6) , (0/7) ]\n\nNow, the diagonals:\n\nFirst diagonal (the far right bottom):\n\nThe cell at bottom right has lower part 7. That's the first diagonal.\n\nSecond diagonal: moving left, it includes the lower part of the top right cell (4) and the upper part of the bottom left cell (0). Wait, no, perhaps it's better to visualize the diagonals as follows:\n\nImagine the grid:\n\n[ (1/2) (1/4) ]\n\n[ (0/6) (0/7) ]\n\nThe diagonals start from the bottom right corner. The first diagonal is just the 7 (from the lower right cell's lower part). The next diagonal goes up and left: it includes the lower part of the bottom left cell (6) and the upper part of the top right cell (1). Wait, maybe not. Let me think step by step.\n\nActually, when adding diagonally, each diagonal corresponds to a place value. The rightmost diagonal is the units place, then the next is tens, then hundreds, etc.\n\nStarting from the bottom right:\n\n- The very first diagonal (units place) is the lower-right cell's lower triangle: 7.\n\n- The next diagonal (tens place) includes the lower triangle of the top-right cell (4) and the lower triangle of the bottom-left cell (6). Wait, no, actually, in lattice multiplication, each diagonal is formed by the upper right to lower left diagonals of the grid cells.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the diagonals are:\n\nFirst diagonal (units): the cell at position (2,2) lower right.\n\nSecond diagonal (tens): cells (1,2) lower and (2,1) upper.\n\nThird diagonal (hundreds): cells (1,1) lower and (2,1) upper? Hmm, this is getting confusing without a diagram.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the sum is done as follows:\n\nAfter filling the cells with the products split into tens and units, you draw the diagonal lines from top-right to bottom-left, and then add the numbers along each diagonal (starting from the bottom):\n\nLet me try again.\n\nThe grid cells with their split parts:\n\nTop row cells:\n\nFirst cell (top-left): upper 1, lower 2\n\nSecond cell (top-right): upper 1, lower 4\n\nBottom row cells:\n\nFirst cell (bottom-left): upper 0, lower 6\n\nSecond cell (bottom-right): upper 0, lower 7\n\nNow, drawing the diagonals:\n\nThe first diagonal (units place) is the lower-right part of the bottom-right cell: 7.\n\nThe next diagonal (tens place) includes the lower part of the top-right cell (4) and the lower part of the bottom-left cell (6). Wait, but the bottom-left cell's lower part is 6, and the top-right cell's lower part is 4. So adding 4 +6 = 10. So we write down 0 and carry over 1?\n\nWait, maybe the diagonals are arranged such that the numbers are in the same diagonal. Let me think of each diagonal as a column in the grid when viewed diagonally.\n\nAlternatively, here's a step-by-step way:\n\nStarting from the bottom right corner, the diagonals go up and to the left.\n\nFirst diagonal (rightmost): only the 7 (from bottom-right cell's lower half).\n\nSecond diagonal: the lower half of the top-right cell (4) and the upper half of the bottom-left cell (0). Wait, no. Maybe the upper half of the bottom-left cell is 0, and the lower half of the top-right cell is 4. So adding those: 0 + 4? But that might not be correct.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the diagonals are as follows:\n\nThe first diagonal (units place):\n\n- The cell at bottom-right has lower part 7 → units place:7\n\nSecond diagonal (tens place):\n\n- The cell above it (top-right's lower part:4) and the cell to the left (bottom-left's upper part:0) → 4 +0=4 → tens place 4?\n\nWait, but that would give 4 in the tens place, but that doesn't align with the previous result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the upper half of the top-left cell (1) is part of the hundreds place?\n\nThis is getting a bit tangled. Let me think of another way. Maybe I should just accept that lattice might not be the best method here, and since the standard algorithm and distributive property both gave me 1407, which seems consistent.\n\nAlternatively, let me use another method: 67 multiplied by 21 can be thought of as (70 - 3) times 21. Maybe that's easier?\n\nSo, 70 ×21 minus 3×21.\n\nCalculating 70×21: 70×20 is 1400, plus 70×1=70, so 1400 +70=1470.\n\nThen 3×21=63.\n\nSo 1470 -63 = 1407. Yep, that works too. So that's another way to confirm.\n\nAlternatively, breaking down both numbers into tens and units:\n\n67 is 60 +7, and 21 is 20 +1.\n\nSo (60+7)(20+1) = 60×20 +60×1 +7×20 +7×1.\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n60×20 = 1200,\n\n60×1 =60,\n\n7×20=140,\n\n7×1=7.\n\nAdding all these together: 1200 +60=1260; 1260 +140=1400; 1400 +7=1407. Yep, same answer.\n\nHmm, so all methods are pointing to 1407. I think that's solid. I guess I was just overcomplicating with the lattice method because I forgot the exact steps, but since the other methods check out, I can be confident.\n\nWait, just to be thorough, maybe I can use a calculator to confirm, but since I don't have one, I can do 67 ×20 is 1340, plus 67 is 1407. Yeah, that's straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, adding 67 twenty-one times. Well, 67×10 is 670, so 67×20 is double that, which is 1340, plus 67 is 1407. Yep.\n\nAlright, I think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. All different methods give the same result, so I'm pretty sure the product of 67 and 21 is 1407.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 67 and 21 is \\boxed{1407}.\n"] 68%|██████▊   | 339/500 [5:55:45<2:41:37, 60.23s/it]
