nsider that the first number is the rows and the second the columns, but that would be unconventional.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the lattice method requires multiplying the other way around.\n\nWait, let me try reversing the numbers: 73 x74 instead of 74x73, but multiplication is commutative, so the result should be the same.\n\nUsing lattice for 73 x74:\n\nFirst number (columns):7 and3,\n\nSecond number (rows):7 and4.\n\nWait, no, that would be different.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps my mistake is in the multiplication of the cells:\n\nWait, in the initial problem, the first number is 74 (so columns are 7 and4),\n\nSecond number is73 (rows are7 and3).\n\nThus, cell (Row1, Col1)=7x7=49,\n\ncell (Row1, Col2)=7x4=28,\n\ncell (Row2, Col1)=3x7=21,\n\ncell (Row2, Col2)=3x4=12.\n\nThese are correct.\n\nSo the grid is correct, but the summation is giving an incorrect result.\n\nWait, perhaps the hundreds place should be 13 + 1 (carryover from thousands?) No.\n\nWait, maybe I'm adding the diagonals in the wrong direction.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should write the numbers in the grid and then add the diagonals from top to bottom.\n\nWait, let me write the grid's numbers in a numerical layout:\n\nThe grid's cells have:\n\nRow1:\n\n4 (TL) 9 (BR) | 2 (TL) 8 (BR)\n\nRow2:\n\n2 (TL) 1 (BR) | 1 (TL) 2 (BR)\n\nIf we remove the divisions, the grid looks like:\n\nRow1: 4 9 | 2 8\n\nRow2: 2 1 | 1 2\n\nNow, the diagonals to add are from bottom-right to top-left:\n\nFirst diagonal (units):2 (from the last cell's BR)\n\nSecond diagonal (tens):1 (from Row2, second cell's BR) and 8 (from Row1, second cell's BR). Total 9.\n\nThird diagonal (hundreds):2 (Row2, first cell's BR), 2 (Row1, second cell's TL), and 9 (Row1, first cell's BR). Wait, this is confusing.\n\nAlternatively, arrange the numbers in a grid without the splits:\n\nThe grid with TL and BR combined:\n\nRow1: 49 | 28\n\nRow2: 21 | 12\n\nNow, to perform lattice addition, you add along the diagonals from bottom-right to top-left:\n\nStarting at the bottom-right of the entire grid (the 2 in 12's units place):\n\n- 2 (units)\n\nMove to the diagonal above-left:\n\n- The 1 (from 21's units) and the 8 (from 28's units):1+8=9 (tens)\n\nNext diagonal:\n\n- The 2 (from 21's tens), the 2 (from 28's tens), and the 9 (from 49's units):2+2+9=13 (hundreds place, 3 with carryover 1)\n\nNext diagonal:\n\n- The 4 (from 49's tens) plus the carryover 1:5 (thousands)\n\nThus, the total is 5392, which is still incorrect.\n\nBut this must mean I'm making a fundamental mistake in understanding lattice multiplication, because all other methods give 5402.\n\nWait a minute, let's try multiplying 74 by73 using the standard algorithm again:\n\n   74\n\nx 73\n\n--------\n\nFirst, 74x3=222.\n\nSecond, 74x70=5180.\n\nAdding:222+5180=5402. Correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the lattice method was applied to 73x74 instead of 74x73? Let me try that.\n\nIf we do 73x74:\n\nFirst number (columns):7 and3,\n\nSecond number (rows):7 and4.\n\nThen cells:\n\nRow1 (7):\n\n7x7=49,\n\n7x3=21.\n\nRow2 (4):\n\n4x7=28,\n\n4x3=12.\n\nThus the grid would be:\n\nRow1:\n\n4/9 |2/1\n\nRow2:\n\n2/8 |1/2\n\nNow, adding diagonals:\n\nUnits: cell (2,2) BR:2.\n\nTens: cell (2,1) BR (8) and cell (1,2) BR (1). 8+1=9.\n\nHundreds: cell (2,1) TL (2), cell (1,2) TL (2), and cell (1,1) BR (9). 2+2+9=13 \u21923 with carryover1.\n\nThousands: cell (1,1) TL (4) +1 \u21925.\n\nResult:5392 again.\n\nSame result. But 73x74 is the same as74x73, so it should give the same answer. Thus, the lattice method must be wrong here, which is impossible.\n\nWait, maybe I need to consider that the TL and BR parts are for two-digit products, so when the product is a single digit, the TL is 0?\n\nAh! Here's the mistake. When multiplying digits that result in a single-digit number, the tens place (TL) should be 0.\n\nIn the previous calculations, for example, cell (Row2, Col2) in the original problem (3x4=12) is correct (1 and2). But cell (Row2, Col1) is 3x7=21, so split into 2/1 (correct). Cell (Row1, Col2) is 7x4=28 \u21922/8 (correct). Cell (Row1, Col1) is 7x7=49 \u21924/9 (correct).\n\nHowever, in the second example where I considered 73x74, cell (Row2, Col2) would be 4x3=12 (1/2), and cell (Row1, Col2)=7x3=21 (2/1), which is correct.\n\nWait, but in all cases, the splits are correct. So why discrepancy?\n\nWait, let me try an example with known result to see.\n\nLet me try 12 x 34 using lattice method:\n\nFirst number (columns):1 and2,\n\nSecond number (rows):3 and4.\n\nCells:\n\nRow1 (3):\n\n3x1=03 \u21920/3,\n\n3x2=06 \u21920/6\n\nRow2 (4):\n\n4x1=04 \u21920/4,\n\n4x2=08 \u21920/8\n\nGrid:\n\nRow1:\n\n0/3 |0/6\n\nRow2:\n\n0/4 |0/8\n\nAdding diagonals:\n\nUnits: cell (2,2) BR=8,\n\nTens: cell (2,1) BR=4 and cell (1,2) BR=6 \u21924+6=10,\n\nHundreds: cell (2,1) TL=0, cell (1,2) TL=0, and cell (1,1) BR=3 \u21920+0+3=3 plus carryover 1 from tens \u21924.\n\nThousands: cell (1,1) TL=0 plus any carryover? \n\nWait, the total would be:\n\nUnits:8,\n\nTens:0 (from 10, write 0, carryover1),\n\nHundreds:3 +1=4,\n\nThousands:0.\n\nThus total is 408. But 12x34=408. Correct.\n\nSo the method works for this case.\n\nAnother test: 11x11.\n\nColumns:1 and1,\n\nRows:1 and1.\n\nCells:\n\nRow1 (1):\n\n1x1=01 \u21920/1,\n\n1x1=01 \u21920/1\n\nRow2 (1):\n\n1x1=01 \u21920/1,\n\n1x1=01 \u21920/1\n\nGrid:\n\nRow1:\n\n0/1 |0/1\n\nRow2:\n\n0/1 |0/1\n\nDiagonals:\n\nUnits: cell (2,2) BR=1,\n\nTens: cell (2,1) BR=1 + cell (1,2) BR=1 \u21922,\n\nHundreds: cell (2,1) TL=0 + cell (1,2) TL=0 + cell (1,1) BR=1 \u21921,\n\nThousands: cell (1,1) TL=0.\n\nTotal: 121, which is correct.\n\nSo the lattice method works.\n\nTherefore, my mistake must be in the current problem's lattice setup.\n\nWait, let's re-express the original problem with correct splits and see:\n\nProblem:74 x73.\n\nColumns (first number 74):7 and4,\n\nRows (second number73):7 and3.\n\nCells:\n\nRow1 (7):\n\n7x7=49 \u21924/9,\n\n7x4=28 \u21922/8,\n\nRow2 (3):\n\n3x7=21 \u21922/1,\n\n3x4=12 \u21921/2.