ibuting to 10^1 (tens place). But in the calculation, it contributed to hundreds when combined with carryover.\n\nWait, confusion arises because carryover affects the next higher place.\n\nPerhaps it's better to consider that each diagonal's sum is added to the corresponding place, and carryover propagates to the next higher place.\n\nIn the 74x73 example:\n\n- Units:2 \u2192 units place.\n\n- Tens:9 \u2192 tens place.\n\n- Hundreds:13 \u2192 hundreds place with carryover 1.\n\n- Thousands:4 +1 \u21925.\n\nTotal:5 thousands, 3 hundreds, 9 tens, 2 units \u21925392.\n\nBut actual product is 5402. The difference is 10. So where is the missing 10?\n\nWait, perhaps I made an error in the multiplication step.\n\nWait, let me recompute 74x73 using another method:\n\n74x73 = (70+4)(70+3) = 70\u00b2 +70\u00d73 +4\u00d770 +4\u00d73 = 4900 +210 +280 +12.\n\nAdding these:\n\n4900 +210 =5110,\n\n5110 +280 =5390,\n\n5390 +12 =5402.\n\nYes, that's correct.\n\nSo the lattice method is giving 5392 instead of 5402. There must be an error in the lattice setup.\n\nWait a minute, in the grid for 74x73:\n\nRow1 (7):\n\ncell (1,1)=7x7=49 \u21924/9,\n\ncell (1,2)=7x4=28 \u21922/8,\n\nRow2 (3):\n\ncell (2,1)=3x7=21 \u21922/1,\n\ncell (2,2)=3x4=12 \u21921/2.\n\nThe grid is correct.\n\nNow, the diagonals:\n\nThe hundreds place should include the cell (1,1)'s BR (9),",
