      "text": ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 36 and 49. Hmm, let me think about how to do this. Multiplication, right? But 36 times 49 might not be something I can just recall off the top of my head. Maybe I can break it down into smaller parts to make it easier. \n\nFirst, I remember that multiplying numbers can sometimes be simplified by breaking them into factors. Let me see, 36 is 6 squared, which is 6 times 6. And 49 is 7 squared, so 7 times 7. But I'm not sure if that helps directly here. Alternatively, maybe I can use the distributive property. \n\nWait, 49 is close to 50. Maybe I can think of 49 as 50 minus 1. That might make the multiplication easier. Let me try that. So, 36 multiplied by (50 - 1). Using distributive property, that would be 36*50 minus 36*1. Let's calculate each part.\n\n36 times 50. Hmm, 36 times 5 is 180, so times 10 would be 1800? Wait, no, 50 is 5 times 10, so 36*5 is 180, then times 10 is 1800. Yeah, that's right. Then 36 times 1 is just 36. So subtracting those, 1800 minus 36. Let me do that subtraction. \n\n1800 minus 30 would be 1770, and then minus 6 more is 1764. So that gives me 1764. Let me check if that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can multiply 36 by 49 the standard way. Let me write it out vertically:\n\n\u2003\u00a036  \n\u00d7\u00a049  \n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500  \nFirst multiply 36 by 9: 36*9. 30*9=270, 6*9=54, so 270+54=324. \n\nThen multiply 36 by 40 (since the 4 in 49 is in the tens place). 36*40. Well, 36*4=144, so times 10 is 1440. \n\nNow add those two results together: 324 + 1440. Let's see, 324 + 1400 is 1724, then plus 40 more is 1764. Yep, same answer. \n\nHmm, so both methods give me 1764. That seems right. Let me confirm once more just to be sure. Maybe using another method. \n\nAnother way could be to recognize that 36*49 is the same as (30 + 6)*(40 + 9). Using the FOIL method here. \n\nFirst: 30*40 = 1200  \nOuter: 30*9 = 270  \nInner: 6*40 = 240  \nLast: 6*9 = 54  \n\nAdding those up: 1200 + 270 is 1470, plus 240 is 1710, plus 54 is 1764. Yep, same result again. \n\nOkay, so all three methods give me 1764. I think that's solid. Just to be absolutely thorough, maybe do a quick check with approximate values. \n\n36 is 36, 49 is almost 50. 36*50 is 1800, so subtracting 36 gives 1764, which is exactly what I got. Since 49 is 1 less than 50, subtracting that 36 makes sense. \n\nAlternatively, if I use calculator verification... Wait, I don't have a calculator here, but I can do another cross-check. Let me divide 1764 by 36 and see if I get 49. \n\n1764 divided by 36. Let's see, 36*40 is 1440. Subtract that from 1764: 1764-1440=324. Then 324 divided by 36 is 9. So total is 40 +9 =49. Perfect, that checks out. \n\nAlternatively, divide 1764 by 49 and see if I get 36. 49*30=1470. 1764-1470=294. 294 divided by 49 is 6. So total is 30+6=36. Yep, that works too. \n\nAlright, I think that's more than enough verification. The answer must be 1764.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 36 and 49 is calculated as follows:\n\n**Method 1: Using the distributive property**  \n36 \u00d7 49 = 36 \u00d7 (50 \u2212 1)  \n= (36 \u00d7 50) \u2212 (36 \u00d7 1)  \n= 1800 \u2212 36  \n= **1764**\n\n**Method 2: Standard multiplication**  \n```\n   36  \n\u00d7 49  \n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500  \n  324  (36 \u00d7 9)  \n1440   (36 \u00d7 40, shifted one position to the left)  \n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500  \n1764  \n```\n\n**Verification:**  \n- Dividing the result by either factor confirms the answer:  \n  1764 \u00f7 36 = 49  \n  1764 \u00f7 49 = 36  \n\n**Final Answer:**  \nThe product of 36 and 49 is **1764**.  \n\n\\boxed{1764}",
