ong the diagonals. Starting from the rightmost diagonal, which is just the bottom right cell's bottom number, which is 5.\n\nNext diagonal: the top right cell's bottom (8) plus bottom left cell's bottom (0) plus the bottom right cell's top (3). Wait, maybe I need to visualize this better.\n\nWait, lattice method diagonals are from top-right to bottom-left. Let me recall properly. The diagonals separate the digits. Let me try again.\n\nThe grid would look like this:\n\nTop row cells: first cell (4 and 8): 32 split into 3/2. Second cell (4 and7): 28 split 2/8.\n\nSecond row cells: first cell (5 and8):40 split 4/0. Second cell (5 and7):35 split 3/5.\n\nNow, the diagonals: the rightmost diagonal is the bottom right cell's bottom half (5). The next diagonal is the sum of the top right cell's bottom (8) and the bottom left cell's bottom (0), and the bottom right cell's top (3). Wait, actually, maybe each diagonal is the sum of the numbers along that diagonal.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to draw it mentally:\n\nThe grid with diagonals:\n\nThe four cells:\n\nFirst row: [3/2 | 2/8]\n\nSecond row: [4/0 | 3/5]\n\nThe diagonals would be:\n\nFirst diagonal (rightmost): the 5 from the bottom right cell's lower half.\n\nSecond diagonal: 8 (from top right cell's lower half) + 3 (from bottom right cell's upper half). Wait, maybe I'm mixing up.\n\nAlternatively, each diagonal is the diagonal from top-right to bottom-left. So starting from the top right cell's upper triangle (2) and going down-left?\n\nHmm, perhaps I'm overcomplicating. Maybe I should proceed step by step.\n\nActually, maybe it's better to look up the lattice method steps again, but since I can't, let me try again.\n\nAfter splitting each cell into halves:\n\nThe top row first cell has 3 (top) and 2 (bottom). The second cell has 2 (top) and 8 (bottom).\n\nSecond row first cell has 4 (top) and 0 (bottom). The second cell has 3 (top) and 5 (bottom).\n\nNow, the diagonals are vertical lines from top-right to bottom-left