final_output:  ['\n\nThe product of 43 and 72 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Break down the multiplication using the distributive property:**\n   \\[\n   43 \\times 72 = 43 \\times (70 + 2) = (43 \\times 70) + (43 \\times 2)\n   \\]\n   \n2. **Calculate each part:**\n   - \\( 43 \\times 70 = 3010 \\)\n   - \\( 43 \\times 2 = 86 \\)\n\n3. **Add the results:**\n   \\[\n   3010 + 86 = 3096\n   \\]\n\n**Verification using another method:**\n- Reverse the multiplication (\\(72 \\times 43\\)):\n  - \\( 72 \\times 40 = 2880 \\)\n  - \\( 72 \\times 3 = 216 \\)\n  - \\( 2880 + 216 = 3096 \\)\n\nBoth methods confirm the result is consistent.\n\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{3096}\\)', ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 43 and 72. Hmm, let me think. Multiplication, right? But I should probably break it down step by step to make sure I don't make a mistake. \n\nFirst, I remember that multiplying two numbers can be done by breaking them into smaller parts. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. Let me try that. So 43 times 72. Let me write it out as 43 × 72. \n\nAlternatively, I could do the standard multiplication algorithm. Let me try that method. \n\nStarting with the ones place: 43 multiplied by 2 (the ones digit of 72). 3 times 2 is 6, and 4 times 2 is 8. So that gives 86. Wait, no, actually, hold on. Wait, when multiplying by the ones digit first, it's 43 × 2. Let me do that again. \n\n43 × 2: 3×2=6, 4×2=8, so that's 86. Then, moving to the tens place, which is 7 in 72, but since it's the tens place, it's actually 70. So I need to multiply 43 by 70. \n\nMultiplying 43 by 70. Let me do 43 × 7 first. 3×7=21, 4×7=28, so 28 plus the 2 from the 21? Wait, no. Wait, actually, 43 × 7 is calculated as (40 +3) ×7 = 40×7 +3×7 = 280 +21= 301. So then, 43×70 would be 3010, since it's 7 times 10. \n\nThen, adding the two results together: 86 (from the 43×2) plus 3010 (from 43×70). Let me add those. 3010 +80 is 3090, then plus 6 more is 3096. So the total is 3096? \n\nWait, let me check that again. Because sometimes when I do multiplication step by step, I might have messed up a step. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method to verify. Let's try the distributive property another way. 43 ×72 can be written as (40 +3)(70 +2). Then expanding that: 40×70 +40×2 +3×70 +3×2. \n\nCalculating each term:\n\n40×70: 40×70 is 2800.\n\n40×2 is 80.\n\n3×70 is 210.\n\n3×2 is 6.\n\nNow, adding them all up: 2800 +80 is 2880. Then 2880 +210 is 3090. Then 3090 +6 is 3096. So same result. That's a good sign.\n\nAlternatively, maybe do it vertically:\n\n   43  \n×72  \n------  \nFirst multiply 43 by 2: 86.\n\nThen multiply 43 by 70 (so shift one position to the left):\n\n43  \n×70  \n-----  \n210 (but since it's 70, actually, 43×7=210? Wait, no. Wait 43×7 is 301, so 43×70 is 3010. So when written vertically, it would be:\n\n     43  \n   ×72  \n   ------  \n     86   (43×2)  \n   3010   (43×70, shifted one position to the left)  \n   ------  \n   3096  \n\nYes, adding 86 and 3010 gives 3096. \n\nAlternatively, maybe use another method, like the lattice method? Hmm, but that might take longer. Alternatively, check using a calculator? But since I can't use a calculator, maybe do another verification. \n\nLet me try adding 43 seventy-two times. Well, that's impractical, but perhaps break it down. \n\nAlternatively, 72 is 70 +2, so 43×70 +43×2, which is exactly what I did before. \n\nAlternatively, reverse the numbers: 72×43. Maybe compute that way. \n\n72 ×40 = 2880, and 72 ×3=216. Then 2880 +216 = 3096. Yep, same answer. \n\nSo that seems consistent. \n\nWait, just to make sure, let me do 43×72 step by step again. \n\nFirst, write 72 on top and 43 below:\n\n   72  \n×43  \n------  \nMultiply 72 by 3: 216. \n\nMultiply 72 by 40: 72×40. 72×4=288, so 2880. \n\nAdd 216 +2880: 216+2800 is 3016, plus 80 more is 3096. \n\nSame result. \n\nOkay, so all methods are leading me to 3096, so I think that's the correct product. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, so I'm confident that's the right answer.\n"] 74%|███████▍  | 371/500 [6:32:36<2:08:42, 59.86s/it]
