      "text": ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 43 and 72. Hmm, let me think about how to do this step by step. I remember that multiplying two numbers means adding one number to itself as many times as the other number says. But since these are both two-digit numbers, maybe there's a better way than just adding 43 seventy-two times. \n\nWait, right, the standard multiplication algorithm! Let me recall how that works. You line up the numbers vertically, right? Let me write them down:\n\n\u200343\n\u00d772\n-----\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit of the bottom number, which is 2, and multiply it by each digit of the top number, moving left. So first, 2 times 3 is 6. Then 2 times 4 is 8. So that gives me 86. But wait, actually, since the 4 is in the tens place, it's 40, so 2 times 40 is 80, so 80 + 6 is 86. That's the first partial product.\n\nThen, I need to multiply the next digit in the bottom number, which is 7. But since it's the tens place, this 7 actually represents 70. So when multiplying by the 7, I have to remember to shift one place to the left, right? So, starting again, 7 times 3 is 21, and 7 times 4 is 28. But since this is the tens place, the 4 is actually 40, so 7 times 40 is 280, and 7 times 3 is 21, so total is 280 +21 = 301? Wait, but when writing it out, we usually write it shifted one position. Let me see:\n\nSo after the first partial product is 86, the second partial product would be 7 times 43, but shifted one place to the left. Let me compute 7 times 43 first. 7 times 40 is 280, and 7 times 3 is 21. Adding those gives 280 +21 = 301. But since we shifted one place, does that mean it's 3010? Wait, no. Wait, actually, when you write it out in the multiplication:\n\n\u2003\u00a043\n\u00d7\u00a072\n-----\n\u200386\u00a0 (this is 43 \u00d7 2)\n301\u00a0 (this is 43 \u00d7 70, so shifted one to the left)\n-----\nThen add them together.\n\nWait, but actually, when you do 43 \u00d7 70, you can write it as 3010? Let me check that. Because 43 \u00d7 7 is 301, so 43 \u00d7 70 is 301 \u00d710, which is 3010. So then adding 86 and 3010.\n\nSo 3010 + 86. Let me compute that. 3010 + 80 is 3090, then +6 is 3096. So the total is 3096?\n\nWait a second, but let me confirm this with another method to be sure. Maybe breaking down the numbers using distributive property.\n\n43 \u00d772 can be written as (40 + 3)(70 + 2). Then expanding that:\n\n40\u00d770 + 40\u00d72 + 3\u00d770 + 3\u00d72.\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n40\u00d770 = 2800,\n\n40\u00d72 = 80,\n\n3\u00d770 = 210,\n\n3\u00d72 = 6.\n\nAdding all those together: 2800 + 80 = 2880, then 2880 +210 = 3090, then +6 is 3096. Okay, same result. So that's good.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method, like the lattice method or something else, but since I got the same answer with two different methods, I think that's solid. Let me try another way just to be thorough.\n\nAlternatively, 43 \u00d772. Let's see, maybe multiply 43 by 70 first, which is 3010 as before, then add 43\u00d72 which is 86, so 3010+86=3096. Yep.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps using the fact that 72 is 8\u00d79, so 43\u00d78\u00d79. Let's see:\n\n43\u00d78 = 344, then 344\u00d79. 344\u00d710 is 3440, minus 344 gives 3440-344=3096. Same answer again.\n\nAlternatively, 72\u00d740 = 2880, and 72\u00d73=216. Adding those: 2880+216=3096. Yep.\n\nHmm, so all methods are pointing to 3096. Wait, but let me just do the standard algorithm again step by step to make sure I didn't make a mistake in that initially.\n\nFirst, writing 43 \u00d772:\n\n\u2003\u00a043\n\u00d7\u00a072\n-----\n\u2003\u00a0?\u00a0?\n\nMultiplying 2 (units place) by 43:\n\n2\u00d73=6, 2\u00d74=8. So 86.\n\nThen multiplying 7 (tens place, so actually 70) by 43:\n\n7\u00d73=21, write down 1 and carryover 2.\n\n7\u00d74=28, plus the carried over 2 is 30. So that gives 301. But since it's the tens place, we shift one to the left, so it's 3010? Wait, but when writing the partial products, it's 301 shifted left by one digit, which is equivalent to multiplying by 10, so yes, 3010. Then adding 86 + 3010 gives 3096.\n\nYes, that all checks out. I think that's correct. Let me just do a quick estimation to see if 3096 makes sense. 40 times 70 is 2800, and 40\u00d772 would be 2880, so 43 is 3 more than 40, so 3\u00d772 is 216, so 2880+216=3096. Yep, that's the same as before. So the answer must be 3096.\n\nI don't see any errors in my calculations here. All different methods give the same result, so I'm confident that's the correct product.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 43 and 72 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Break down the multiplication using the distributive property**:\n   \\[\n   43 \\times 72 = 43 \\times (70 + 2) = (43 \\times 70) + (43 \\times 2)\n   \\]\n   - Calculate \\(43 \\times 70\\):\n     \\[\n     43 \\times 70 = 43 \\times 7 \\times 10 = 301 \\times 10 = 3010\n     \\]\n   - Calculate \\(43 \\times 2\\):\n     \\[\n     43 \\times 2 = 86\n     \\]\n   - Add the results:\n     \\[\n     3010 + 86 = 3096\n     \\]\n\n2. **Verification using the standard algorithm**:\n   ```\n     43\n   \u00d7 72\n   -----\n     86  (43 \u00d7 2)\n   +3010 (43 \u00d7 70, shifted left)\n   -----\n     3096\n   ```\n\n3. **Alternative method (breaking into smaller parts)**:\n   \\[\n   (40 + 3)(70 + 2) = 40 \\times 70 + 40 \\times 2 + 3 \\times 70 + 3 \\times 2\n   \\]\n   - \\(40 \\times 70 = 2800\\)\n   - \\(40 \\times 2 = 80\\)\n   - \\(3 \\times 70 = 210\\)\n   - \\(3 \\times 2 = 6\\)\n   - Sum all parts:\n     \\[\n     2800 + 80 + 210 + 6 = 3096\n     \\]\n\n**Final Answer**: The product of 43 and 72 is \\boxed{3096}.",
