since it's only 194, I need to add back 6. So 1740 +6 = 1746. Again, same result. \n\nAlright, so multiple methods are giving me the same answer, so I think that's solid. \n\nWait, just to check once more, maybe using exponents or another trick. Let me think... Hmm, 18 times 97. 18 is 2*9, and 97 is a prime number, I believe. Not sure if that helps. Alternatively, perhaps using some algebraic identity?\n\nAlternatively, think of 18*97 as (20 - 2)(100 - 3). Wait, that's combining both breakdowns. Let me try that. \n\nExpanding (20 - 2)(100 - 3) using the distributive property (FOIL method):\n\nFirst: 20*100 = 2000  \nOuter: 20*(-3) = -60  \nInner: (-2)*100 = -200  \nLast: (-2)*(-3) = +6  \n\nAdding all those together: 2000 -60 -200 +6. Let's compute step by step:\n\n2000 -60 = 1940  \n1940 -200 = 1740  \n1740 +6 = 1746. \n\nSame result again. That's four different methods all leading to 1746. I think that's definitely the correct product. \n\nJust to recap all the methods I used:\n\n1. Break down 97 into 100-3 and used distributive property.\n2. Standard multiplication algorithm (writing it out the traditional way).\n3. Breaking down 18 into 20-2 and using distributive property again.\n4. Combining both breakdowns and using the FOIL method for the resulting binomials.\n\nAll four methods led me to the same answer, so unless I made the same mistake in all of them (which is possible but unlikely), I can be confident the answer is correct. Let me check once more with another perspective. \n\nSuppose I approximate the numbers first to see if 1746 is a reasonable answer. 18 is approximately 20, and 97 is approximately 100. 20*100 is 2000. Since both approximations are overestimates, the actual product should be less than 2000. 1746 is indeed less, so that makes sense. \n\nAlternatively, 18*100 would be 1800, so subtracting 18*3 (which is 54) gives 1800 -54 = 1746. That also checks out. \n\nAlternatively, if I think of 18*97 as 9*2*97, then maybe compute 9*97 first. Let's 