t maybe overcomplicating. Alternatively, check with calculator? But since I can't use a calculator, let me try another way.\n\nLet me compute 60*28 first, which is easier. 60*28=1680. Then subtract 1*28, which is 28. So 1680 -28 = 1652. Yep, that's the same answer. So that confirms it. \n\nTherefore, the product of 59 and 28 is 1652.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 59 and 28 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Break down using distributive property:**\n   \\[\n   59 \\times 28 = 59 \\times (20 + 8) = (59 \\times 20) + (59 \\times 8)\n   \\]\n   \n2. **Compute each part:**\n   - \\( 59 \\times 20 = 1,\\!180 \\)\n   - \\( 59 \\times 8 = 472 \\)\n   \n3. **Add the results:**\n   \\[\n   1,\\!180 + 472 = 1,\\!652\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer:**  \nThe product of 59 and 28 is \\(\\boxed{1652}\\).",
