234, so times 10 is 2340.\n\nThen 39*1 is 39. Adding those together: 2340 + 39 = 2379. Yep, same result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe do the multiplication the traditional way:\n\n   39\nx 61\n-------\nFirst multiply 39 by 1: 39\nThen multiply 39 by 60: 2340 (since 39*6=234, then add a zero)\nAdd them: 39 + 2340 = 2379. Same answer again.\n\nOkay, so both methods give me 2379. That seems right. I think that's the correct product. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations here. Let me just confirm once more with another approach.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using another difference of squares approach? Let's see. Suppose I pick a different a and b? Hmm, not sure if that would help, but let's see. Alternatively, maybe (40 -1)*(60 +1). Wait, but that might complicate things. Let me try expanding that:\n\n(40 - 1)(60 + 1) = 40*60 + 40*1 -1*60 -1*1 = 2400 + 40 -60 -1 = 2400 -20 -1 = 2379. Oh, that works too! So that's another way to break it down. So that also gives the same answer. Nice.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the standard algorithm step by step:\n\nMultiply 39 by 61:\n\nFirst, write them down:\n\n      39\n    x61\n    ----\n    \nMultiply 39 by 1 (units place): 39\n\nMultiply 39 by 6 (tens place, so it's actually 60). So 39*6 = 234, but shifted one position to the left: 2340\n\nAdd the two results: 39 + 2340 = 2379. Yep, same result.\n\nAlright, all methods are pointing to 2379. I feel confident that's the right answer. I think that's solid. No mistakes detected here. So the product of 39 and 61 is 2379.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 39 and 61 is \\boxed{2379}.\n"]
