00. Yep, same result.\n\nAlternatively, I know that 50 times 20 is 1000, so 50 times 22 is 50 more than that, right? Because 22 is 20 + 2. So 50*20 = 1000, plus 50*2 = 100, so total is 1100. That's consistent.\n\nI think all the different methods are pointing to the same answer, 1100. Maybe I can check using a calculator if I have one, but since this is mental math, let me see if there's another way to verify. \n\nAlternatively, think of 22 as 2*11. Then 50*2*11. 50*2 is 100, then 100*11 is 1100. Yep, that works too. \n\nAnother angle: 22 multiplied by 50. Since 50 is half of 100, maybe 22*100 is 2200, then half of that is 1100. Oh, that's a clever way. Because 50 is half of 100, so multiplying by 50 is the same as multiplying by 100 and then dividing by 2. So 22*100 = 2200 divided by 2 is 1100. Perfect, that also gives the same answer.\n\nHmm, all these different methods are confirming that the product is 1100. I don't see any discrepancies. Maybe I was overcomplicating by trying so many methods, but better safe than sorry. Let me just recap:\n\nMethod 1: Break 50 into 5*10, compute step by step: 22*5=110, 110*10=1100.\n\nMethod 2: Standard multiplication algorithm: 2*50 + 20*50 = 100 + 1000 = 1100.\n\nMethod 3: Distributive property over addition: 50*(20+2)=50*20 +50*2=1000+100=1100.\n\nMethod 4: Rearranging factors: 5*22*10 = (5*22)*10 = 110*10=1100.\n\nMethod 5: Using 50 as half of 100: 22*100/2=2200/2=1100.\n\nAll methods lead to the same result. Therefore, I can be confident that the product of 50 and 22 is indeed 1100. I think that's thorough enough. No mistakes detected in the calculations. The answer should be 1100.\n"]
