    {
      "index": 0,
      "text": ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 90 and 29. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers together, right? Let me recall how multiplication works. Since both numbers are pretty straightforward, maybe I can do this step by step.\n\nFirst, I know that 90 times 30 would be 2700 because 90 times 10 is 900, so 90 times 30 is three times that, which is 2700. But wait, the question is 90 times 29, not 30. So maybe I can subtract one 90 from 2700 to get the answer? Let me check that.\n\nIf 90 times 30 is 2700, then subtracting 90 once would give me 2700 - 90 = 2610. Is that right? Let me verify another way to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, I can break down 29 into 20 + 9. So, 90 times 20 is 1800, and 90 times 9 is 810. Then adding those two results together: 1800 + 810. Let's see, 1800 + 800 is 2600, and then +10 more is 2610. Okay, so that matches the first method. That's a good sign.\n\nWait, another way to do it might be using the standard multiplication algorithm. Let me write it out like:\n\n\u2003\u200390\n\u00d7\u00a029\n-------\nLet's multiply 90 by 9 first. 9 times 0 is 0, and 9 times 9 is 81. Wait, but since the 9 is in the tens place, actually, it's 9 times 90. Wait, maybe I need to adjust.\n\nHold on, actually, when multiplying 90 by 29, it's better to consider 29 as 29 ones. So, multiplying 90 \u00d7 29:\n\nFirst, multiply 90 by the units digit of 29, which is 9. So 90 \u00d7 9 = 810.\n\nThen multiply 90 by the tens digit, which is 2 (since 29 has a 2 in the tens place). But since that's the tens place, it's actually 20. So 90 \u00d7 20 = 1800.\n\nAdding those two results: 810 + 1800 = 2610. Yep, same answer as before. Okay, so that seems consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the distributive property in another way. Let's see, 29 is 30 -1. So 90 \u00d7 (30 -1) = 90\u00d730 - 90\u00d71 = 2700 - 90 = 2610. Exactly the first method I used. So all methods are pointing to 2610. That gives me more confidence.\n\nWait, but let me just do straight multiplication step by step to ensure there's no mistake.\n\nMultiplying 90 by 29:\n\nWrite 90 as 9 followed by a 0, and 29 as 29.\n\nMultiply 90 \u00d7 29:\n\nStart with the 9 (units place of 29) multiplied by 90:\n\n9 \u00d7 0 = 0, 9 \u00d7 9 = 81. So that gives 810. Then, since the next digit in 29 is the 2 (tens place), so we need to shift one position to the left when multiplying. So 2 \u00d7 90 = 180, but shifted one place becomes 1800. Then adding 810 + 1800 gives 2610. Exactly as before.\n\nHmm, seems solid. Let me check with a calculator method. Wait, since I don't have a calculator here, but maybe I can do another verification.\n\nIf I divide the answer by one of the numbers, I should get the other. Let's see, 2610 divided by 90. Let's do that division.\n\n2610 \u00f7 90. Well, 90 \u00d7 29 is the original problem, so dividing 2610 by 90 should give 29.\n\nDivide 2610 by 90: 90 goes into 2610 how many times?\n\nFirst, 90 \u00d7 20 = 1800. Subtract that from 2610: 2610 - 1800 = 810.\n\nThen 90 \u00d7 9 = 810. So 20 + 9 = 29. Perfect, that works out. So that's a good check.\n\nAlternatively, 2610 divided by 29 should equal 90. Let me try that division.\n\n29 into 2610. Let's see, 29 \u00d7 90. Well, 29 \u00d7 9 = 261, so 29 \u00d7 90 is 2610. So that works too. So division checks out both ways.\n\nTherefore, I think the correct answer is 2610. I can't see any mistakes in my calculations, and multiple methods confirm the same result. So I'm confident that's the right product.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 90 and 29 is calculated as follows:\n\nFirst, break down the multiplication using the distributive property:\n\\[\n90 \\times 29 = 90 \\times (30 - 1) = (90 \\times 30) - (90 \\times 1)\n\\]\n\\[\n= 2700 - 90 = 2610\n\\]\n\nVerification through alternative methods (e.g., decomposing 29 into 20 + 9, standard multiplication algorithm, and division checks) all confirm the result is consistent.\n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{2610}",
