final_output:  ['\n\nThe product of 21 and 61 is calculated as follows:\n\nFirst, break down the multiplication using the distributive property:\n\\[\n21 \\times 61 = 21 \\times (60 + 1) = (21 \\times 60) + (21 \\times 1)\n\\]\n\nCalculate each part:\n\\[\n21 \\times 60 = 1260 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 21 \\times 1 = 21\n\\]\n\nAdd the results together:\n\\[\n1260 + 21 = 1281\n\\]\n\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{1281}\\)', ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 21 and 61. Hmm, let me think. Multiplication, right? So 21 times 61. I remember that multiplying two numbers means adding one of them to itself the other number of times. But doing that directly might take a while. Maybe there's a quicker way.\n\nWait, maybe I can break it down using the distributive property. Like, 21 times 60 plus 21 times 1? Because 61 is 60 + 1. Let me try that. So first, 21 multiplied by 60. Hmm, 21 times 60. Well, 21 times 6 is 126, so if I add a zero at the end, that would be 1260. Right? Because multiplying by 60 is the same as multiplying by 6 and then by 10. So that part is 1260.\n\nThen, 21 times 1 is just 21. So adding those two results together: 1260 + 21. Let me add those. 1260 plus 20 is 1280, and then plus 1 more is 1281. So does that mean the product is 1281?\n\nWait a second, let me check my math again to be sure. Maybe I made a mistake in breaking it down. Let me try another method to confirm. How about using the standard multiplication algorithm?\n\nWriting it out:\n\n\u2003\xa021\n×\xa061\n-------\nFirst, multiply 21 by 1 (the units place of 61): 21 × 1 = 21.\n\nThen, multiply 21 by 60 (the tens place of 61, which is 6 in the tens place, so 6×10). So 21 × 6 = 126, and then add a zero at the end to make it 1260.\n\nNow add those two results: 21 + 1260 = 1281. Okay, same answer as before. That's a good sign.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use another approach, like the lattice method? Let's see. Although I might be overcomplicating, but just to verify.\n\nSetting up a lattice grid for 21 × 61. Since both are two-digit numbers. Wait, 21 is two digits, 61 is two digits. So the lattice would be a 2x2 grid.\n\nBreaking down 21 into 2 and 1, and 61 into 6 and 1. Then multiply each digit pair:\n\nTop left cell: 2 × 6 = 12\n\nTop right cell: 2 × 1 = 2\n\nBottom left cell: 1 × 6 = 6\n\nBottom right cell: 1 × 1 = 1\n\nThen, split each cell into diagonals and add along the diagonals. Let me visualize:\n\nFirst diagonal (from top right to bottom left): the digits from the right side of each cell. Wait, maybe I need to write this out step by step.\n\nWait, maybe this is getting too confusing. Since both previous methods gave me 1281, and I can't see an error in them, perhaps that's correct. Alternatively, I can do the multiplication straight:\n\n21\n×61\n------\nMultiply 21 by 1: 21\n\nMultiply 21 by 60: 1260 (since 21×6=126, then ×10 gives 1260)\n\nAdd 21 + 1260: 1281. Yep, same result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the formula (a + b)(c + d) = ac + ad + bc + bd? Let's see.\n\nWait, 21 is 20 + 1, and 61 is 60 + 1. So expanding (20+1)(60+1):\n\n20×60 + 20×1 + 1×60 + 1×1\n\nThat's 1200 + 20 + 60 + 1 = 1200 + 80 + 1 = 1281. Exactly the same. So all methods point to 1281.\n\nHmm, I think that's solid. Maybe another way: 21 × 61. If I switch the numbers, 61 × 21, same result. Let me try that.\n\n61 × 20 = 1220, plus 61 × 1 = 61. So 1220 + 61 = 1281. Yep, same answer again. Okay, definitely, I think that's right.\n\nWait, just to be thorough, maybe use a calculator? But since I don't have one here, let me do it step by step once more.\n\n21 × 61:\n\nBreak down 61 into 60 + 1, so 21×60 is 21×6×10. 21×6: 20×6=120, 1×6=6, so 120+6=126. Then ×10 is 1260. Then add 21×1=21, so 1260+21=1281. Yep.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use exponents? Not sure if that helps here, but perhaps 21 is 3×7 and 61 is a prime number. 3×7×61. Let's compute 3×7=21, then 21×61. Wait, that's back to the original problem. Hmm, not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, 21×60 is 1260, plus 21 is 1281. Yep, same as before.\n\nI think that's enough verification. All methods give the same result. So I can be confident the product is 1281.\n"]
