ait, maybe I need to visualize this better. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps better to stick with the standard method since it's straightforward here. \n\nIn any case, all the different methods are giving me 832. So I can be confident that the product of 64 and 13 is 832. \n\nJust to confirm with a calculator approach: 64*13. Let me do 64*10 which is 640, plus 64*3=192. 640+192. 640+192: 600+100=700, 40+90=130, 0+2=2. Wait, no, that's not the way. Let me add them properly. 640 + 192. \n\nStarting from the units digit: 0 +2=2, 4 +9=13, carry over 1, then 6 +1=7 plus the carried 1 is 8. So 832. Yep. \n\nAlternatively, 64*13. Let me think in terms of money to check. If I have 64 dollars 13 times. Well, 64*10=640, 64*3=192, so total is 832. \n\nAlternatively, maybe 13 is a well-known number, like in a baker's dozen, but not sure if that helps here. \n\nAlternatively, if I think of 64 as 8 squared, so 8^2 *13 = 64*13. Still, same result. \n\nAlternatively, think of 13 as (15 -2). So 64*(15-2)=64*15 -64*2. 64*15 is 960 (since 64*10=640, 64*5=320, so 640+320=960), and 64*2=128. So 960-128=832. \n\nThat's another method and still 832. \n\nHmm, seems like all methods are converging to the same answer. I think that's solid. \n\nTherefore, the product of 64 and 13 is 832.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 64 and 13 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Breakdown using distributive property**:\n   - \\( 64 \\times 13 = 64 \\times (10 + 3) \\)\n   - \\( = (64 \\times 10) + (64 \\times 3) \\)\n   - \\( = 640 + 192 \\)\n   - \\( = 832 \\)\n\n2. **Verification via standard multiplication**:\n   ```\n     64\n   \u00d7 13\n   -----\n     192  (64 \u00d7 3)\n   +640   (64 \u00d7 10, shifted left)\n   -----\n     832\n   ```\n\n3. **Alternative method (using 15 - 2)**:\n   - \\( 64 \\times 13 = 64 \\times (15 - 2) \\)\n   - \\( = (64 \\times 15) - (64 \\times 2) \\)\n   - \\( = 960 - 128 \\)\n   - \\( = 832 \\)\n\n**Final Answer**: \\(\\boxed{832}\\)",
