let me verify this another way to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Maybe using the distributive property: 63 times 34 can be broken down into (60 + 3)(30 + 4). Expanding that, it would be 60*30 + 60*4 + 3*30 + 3*4. Calculating each term:\n\n60*30 = 1800\n\n60*4 = 240\n\n3*30 = 90\n\n3*4 = 12\n\nAdding them up: 1800 + 240 = 2040; 2040 + 90 = 2130; 2130 +12 = 2142. Okay, that matches the previous result. So that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do it mentally another way. Let's see, 63*34. If I think of 34 as 30 +4, then 63*30 is 1890 and 63*4 is 252, adding them gives 1890+252=2142. Yep, same answer. \n\nHmm, seems consistent. Let me check with another method, like the lattice method. Not sure if I remember that, but let's try. \n\nDrawing a 2x2 grid since both numbers are two digits. The digits of 63 go on the top: 6 and 3. The digits of 34 go on the right: 3 and 4. Then divide each box into diagonals. \n\nFirst box: 6 and 3 (the first digits). 6*3=18, so write 18 in the top left and bottom right. Wait, maybe I need to split each cell into two parts with a diagonal. Let me try again:\n\nFirst cell (top left corner): 6*3. So 6*3 is 18. Split the cell diagonally, write 1 on the top left and 8 on the bottom right.\n\nSecond cell (top right corner): 3 (from 63) *3 (from 34). 3*3=9. So write 0 and 9? Wait, no. Wait, maybe I need to do each digit pair properly. Wait, maybe I'm mixing up the steps. Let me recall lattice multiplication steps properly.\n\nActually, maybe I should structure it correctly. The lattice is set up with the top number across the top and the right number down the side. Each digit of the top number is written above the columns, and each digit of the right number is written to the right of the rows. Then each cell is split by a diagonal.\n\nSo for 63 x 34:\n\n- The top number is 63, so the first column is 6, the second column is 3.\n\n- The right number is 34, so the first row is 3, the second row is 4.\n\nSo we have a 2x2 grid. Each cell will be for the multiplication of the corresponding digits.\n\nStarting with the first row (3 from 34) and first column (6 from 63):\n\n6 x 3 = 18. So in that top-left cell, we split into two parts, the top-left part of the diagonal is the tens place (1), and the bottom-right is the units (8).\n\nNext cell in the first row, second column: 3 (from 63) x 3 (from 34) = 9. So write 0 in the tens place and 9 in the units? Wait, 3x3 is 9, so that's a single digit. So the cell would have 0 on top and 9 on the bottom.\n\nMoving to the second row (4 from 34). First column (6 from 63): 6x4=24. So top part is 2, bottom 4.\n\nSecond cell in second row, second column: 3x4=12. So top part 1, bottom 2.\n\nNow, the grid diagonals should form the addition columns. Let me sketch this mentally:\n\nThe grid cells with their diagonals:\n\nTop row first cell: 1 / 8\n\nTop row second cell: 0 /9\n\nSecond row first cell: 2 /4\n\nSecond row second cell: 1 /2\n\nNow, the diagonals from top-right to bottom-left form the addition columns. The rightmost diagonal is just the bottom-right of the last cell (the 2 in the bottom right of the second row, second column). Then moving left, the next diagonal would be the 9 (from top row second cell's bottom) and the 4 (from second row first cell's bottom). Then the next diagonal is 8 (from top row first cell's bottom) and 1 (from second row second cell's top) and 2 (from second row first cell's top). Wait, this is getting confusing. Let me think again.\n\nActually, in lattice multiplication, the diagonals are arranged so that each diagonal represents a place value. Starting from the rightmost diagonal (units place), then tens, hundreds, etc.\n\nLet me try to visualize:\n\nThe cells are arranged as:\n\n[1/8] [0/9]\n\n[2/4] [1/2]\n\nThe diagonals would be:\n\nFirst (rightmost) diagonal: the bottom-right part of the bottom-right cell, which is 2.\n\nSecond diagonal (tens place): the bottom parts of the cells to the left and above, plus the top parts of the