cting 31 gives 1240 -31=1209. \n\nYes, that works too. \n\nAlternatively, 39\u00d730=1170, plus 39\u00d71=39 \u2192 1170+39=1209. \n\nAll right, so all these different methods are confirming that the product is indeed 1209. \n\nI think I've verified it thoroughly. The initial multiplication using the standard algorithm was correct. The confusion with the lattice method might be due to my own misunderstanding of the setup, but since other methods align, I can trust that 1209 is the right answer. \n\nTherefore, the product of 31 and 39 is 1209.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 31 and 39 can be calculated using multiple methods, all leading to the same result:\n\n1. **Standard Multiplication:**\n   ```\n     31\n   \u00d7 39\n   -------\n     279  (31 \u00d7 9)\n   +930   (31 \u00d7 30, shifted left)\n   -------\n    1209\n   ```\n\n2. **Distributive Property:**\n   \\[\n   31 \\times 39 = (30 + 1) \\times 39 = 30 \\times 39 + 1 \\times 39 = 1170 + 39 = 1209\n   \\]\n\n3. **Difference of Squares:**\n   \\[\n   31 \\times 39 = (35 - 4)(35 + 4) = 35^2 - 4^2 = 1225 - 16 = 1209\n   \\]\n\n4. **Alternative Distributive Approach:**\n   \\[\n   31 \\times 39 = 31 \\times (40 - 1) = 31 \\times 40 - 31 = 1240 - 31 = 1209\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer:** The product of 31 and 39 is **1209**. \n\n\\boxed{1209}",
