haps the hundreds place should be the next one. Let me think again. Maybe the diagonals are added starting from the bottom right, moving to the left. \n\nWait, maybe I should list the diagonals in the correct order. Let me visualize the grid with the diagonals:\n\nImagine the grid as:\n\nCell (1,1) [top-left]: 3×3=9 → split into 0/9\n\nCell (1,2) [top-right]:3×9=27 →2/7\n\nCell (2,1) [bottom-left]:1×3=3 →0/3\n\nCell (2,2) [bottom-right]:1×9=9 →0/9\n\nNow, the diagonals for addition would start from the bottom-right corner (cell 2,2) moving up-left. \n\nThe first diagonal (units place) is the lower-right triangle of cell (2,2): 9.\n\nThe second diagonal (tens place) includes the lower part of cell (2,1) (3) and the upper part of cell (1,2) (2). So 3 + 2 =5.\n\nThe third diagonal (hundreds place) includes the upper part of cell (2,1) (0), the lower part of cell (1,1) (9), and the upper part of cell (1,2)? Wait, no. Wait, maybe the next diagonal is the upper part of cell (2,1) (0) and the lower part of cell (1,1) (9). So 0 +9 =9.\n\nThen the next diagonal (thousands place) is the upper part of cell (1,1) (0). \n\nSo indeed, the digits are 9 (units), 5 (tens),9 (hundreds), 0 (thousands). So 0959, which is 959. That's not correct. \n\nHmm, so there must be an error in my application of the lattice method here. Maybe I made a mistake in splitting the products. Let's check the products again:\n\n3×3 is 9, so split into 0 and9. Correct.\n\n3×9 is27, so split into 2 and7. Correct.\n\n1×3 is3 → 0 and3. Correct.\n\n1×9 is9 →0 and9. Correct.\n\nHmm. Maybe the lattice method requires the numbers to be placed in a different orientation? Let me check another way. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the multiplicands should be written in the opposite direction? Like top is units first? Wait, maybe I reversed the digits. \n\nWait, in the lattice method, sometimes the top is the first number's digits from left to right, and the right side is the second number's digits from top to bottom. So for