final_output:  ['\n\nTo find the product of 67 and 89, we can use the distributive property and verify with multiple methods:\n\n1. **Standard Multiplication Algorithm:**\n   - Multiply 67 by 9:\n     \\[\n     67 \\times 9 = 603\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 67 by 80 (shifted one position to the left):\n     \\[\n     67 \\times 80 = 5360\n     \\]\n   - Add the results:\n     \\[\n     603 + 5360 = 5963\n     \\]\n\n2. **Using Algebraic Identity:**\n   - Express 67 and 89 as \\((70 - 3)\\) and \\((90 - 1)\\):\n     \\[\n     (70 - 3)(90 - 1) = 70 \\times 90 - 70 \\times 1 - 3 \\times 90 + 3 \\times 1\n     \\]\n   - Calculate each term:\n     \\[\n     70 \\times 90 = 6300, \\quad 70 \\times 1 = 70, \\quad 3 \\times 90 = 270, \\quad 3 \\times 1 = 3\n     \\]\n   - Combine the terms:\n     \\[\n     6300 - 70 - 270 + 3 = 6300 - 340 + 3 = 5963\n     \\]\n\n3. **Breaking Down Numbers:**\n   - Express 67 as \\(60 + 7\\) and 89 as \\(80 + 9\\):\n     \\[\n     (60 + 7)(80 + 9) = 60 \\times 80 + 60 \\times 9 + 7 \\times 80 + 7 \\times 9\n     \\]\n   - Calculate each term:\n     \\[\n     60 \\times 80 = 4800, \\quad 60 \\times 9 = 540, \\quad 7 \\times 80 = 560, \\quad 7 \\times 9 = 63\n     \\]\n   - Combine the terms:\n     \\[\n     4800 + 540 + 560 + 63 = 5963\n     \\]\n\n4. **Verification via Division:**\n   - Divide the result by 67 to check if it equals 89:\n     \\[\n     5963 \\div 67 = 89\n     \\]\n\nAfter verifying through multiple methods, the product of 67 and 89 is \\(\\boxed{5963}\\).', ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 67 and 89. Hmm, let me think. I remember that multiplying two numbers means adding one number to itself the other number of times. But doing 67 times 89 directly might be a bit tedious if I do it the standard way. Wait, maybe there's a trick or a shortcut to make this easier?\n\nLet me recall some multiplication strategies. Oh, right! The distributive property can help here. Maybe I can break down the numbers into smaller parts. Let