    {
      "index": 0,
      "text": ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 13 and 49. Hmm, let me think about how to approach this. Multiplying two numbers, right? 13 times 49. I remember that multiplication is essentially repeated addition, but adding 13 forty-nine times sounds tedious. There must be a better way.\n\nMaybe I can break down the numbers into smaller parts to make it easier. Let me recall the distributive property. Oh right, like (a \u00d7 b) = a \u00d7 (c + d) if b is split into c and d. Let's see, 49 is close to 50, which is a round number. Maybe I can use that. So 49 is 50 minus 1. So, 13 times (50 - 1). \n\nApplying the distributive property here, that would be 13\u00d750 minus 13\u00d71. Let me compute each part. 13 multiplied by 50. Well, 13 times 5 is 65, so 13 times 50 would be 650. Then subtract 13 times 1, which is 13. So 650 minus 13. Let me do that subtraction: 650 minus 10 is 640, then minus 3 more is 637. So is the answer 637?\n\nWait, let me check another way to be sure. Maybe the standard algorithm. Let's write it down vertically:\n\n\u2003\u00a013\n\u00d749\n------\nFirst multiply 13 by 9. 9\u00d73 is 27, carry over the 2. 9\u00d71 is 9, plus the 2 is 11. So that gives 117. Then multiply 13 by 40 (since the 4 is in the tens place). 13\u00d74 is 52, and since it's the tens place, it's 520. Then add 117 and 520 together. 117 + 520. 117 + 500 is 617, plus 20 more is 637. Okay, same result.\n\nAnother way: Maybe using some other decomposition. Let's see, 13 is 10 + 3. So 49\u00d710 plus 49\u00d73. 49\u00d710 is 490. 49\u00d73: 40\u00d73 is 120, 9\u00d73 is 27, so 120+27=147. Then 490 + 147. 490 + 100 is 590, plus 47 is 637. Yep, same answer again.\n\nHmm, all methods are giving me 637. I think that's correct. Let me just verify with a calculator in my mind. 13\u00d750 would be 650, subtract 13 gives 637. Yep. I can't see any mistakes here. So the product of 13 and 49 is definitely 637.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 13 and 49 is calculated as follows:\n\nFirst, recognize that 49 can be expressed as \\(50 - 1\\). Using the distributive property:  \n\\[\n13 \\times 49 = 13 \\times (50 - 1)\n\\]\n\\[\n= (13 \\times 50) - (13 \\times 1)\n\\]\n\\[\n= 650 - 13\n\\]\n\\[\n= 637\n\\]\n\n**Verification via standard multiplication:**  \n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n\\quad \\ 13 \\\\\n\\times 49 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\quad 117 \\ (\\text{13 \u00d7 9}) \\\\\n+ 520 \\ (\\text{13 \u00d7 40, shifted one position left}) \\\\\n\\hline\n\\quad 637 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the result.  \n\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{637}\\)",
