      "text": ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 28 and 79. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers can sometimes be tricky, especially if they're not too small. Let me recall how multiplication works. The product of two numbers is basically adding one number to itself as many times as the other number says. But doing that manually here would take too long. Maybe I should use the standard multiplication algorithm?\n\nAlright, let me write it down step by step. Let's see, 28 times 79. I can write this as 28 \u00d7 79. Now, in the standard method, you break it down into parts. Since 79 is 70 + 9, maybe I can do 28 times 70 plus 28 times 9. That should work because of the distributive property, right?\n\nFirst, let's compute 28 \u00d7 70. Hmm, 28 times 7 is 196, so if I multiply that by 10, it becomes 1960. Wait, no, actually 28 \u00d7 70 is the same as 28 \u00d7 7 \u00d7 10. Let me check that again. 28 \u00d7 7 is indeed 196, so 196 \u00d7 10 is 1960. Okay, that part seems right.\n\nNow, the other part is 28 \u00d7 9. Let me calculate that. 28 times 9... 28 \u00d7 10 is 280, so subtracting 28 gives 280 - 28 = 252. So 28 \u00d7 9 is 252. \n\nNow, adding those two results together: 1960 + 252. Let me add them. 1960 + 200 is 2160, and then +52 more would be 2212. Wait, is that right? Let me check again. 1960 + 252. 1960 + 200 is 2160, then add 52 to get 2212. Hmm, but I feel like that might not be correct. Maybe I made a mistake in one of the steps.\n\nLet me verify using another method to be sure. Maybe the standard vertical multiplication:\n\n\u2003\u00a028\n\u00d779\n-------\nFirst, multiply 28 by 9 (the units digit of 79):\n28 \u00d7 9 = 252. \n\nThen multiply 28 by 70 (the tens digit part, which is 7 in the tens place, so 70):\n28 \u00d7 70 = 1960.\n\nNow add those two products: 252 + 1960. Let's see, 252 + 1960. Adding the hundreds: 1900 + 60 = 1960, plus 252. 1960 + 200 is 2160, then +52 is 2212. So that gives the same result. Hmm, so maybe it is 2212?\n\nWait, but let me try another way to cross-verify. Maybe using the distributive property in a different way. Let's see, 28 \u00d7 79 can also be written as (30 - 2) \u00d7 79. \n\nExpanding that, it's 30\u00d779 - 2\u00d779. Let's compute those:\n\n30 \u00d7 79: 30 \u00d7 80 is 2400, so subtract 30 to get 2400 - 30 = 2370.\n\nThen 2 \u00d7 79 is 158. \n\nSo subtracting: 2370 - 158 = ?\n\n2370 minus 100 is 2270, minus 58 more is 2212. Okay, so that also gives 2212. Hmm, so that matches the previous result. \n\nAlternatively, maybe do 79 \u00d7 28 by breaking down 28 into 20 + 8. Let's try that:\n\n79 \u00d7 20 = 1580.\n\n79 \u00d7 8 = 632. \n\nAdding those together: 1580 + 632. Let's see: 1500 + 600 = 2100, 80 +32 = 112. So total is 2100 + 112 = 2212. \n\nSame answer again. Hmm, so maybe 2212 is correct. But I want to be absolutely sure. Let me try another approach, perhaps the lattice method or using a calculator-like step-by-step.\n\nAlternatively, maybe compute 28 \u00d7 80 first, then subtract 28. Because 79 is one less than 80. \n\n28 \u00d7 80: 28 \u00d7 8 = 224, so times 10 is 2240. \n\nThen subtract 28: 2240 - 28 = 2212. \n\nSame result again. So all methods are pointing towards 2212. \n\nWait, but just to make sure I didn't make a mistake in any of the intermediate steps. Let me check each calculation again:\n\nFirst method:\n\n28 \u00d7 70 = 1960 (since 28\u00d77=196, then \u00d710)\n28 \u00d7 9 = 252 (28\u00d710=280 minus 28 gives 252)\n1960 + 252: 1960 + 200 is 2160, plus 52 is 2212. Correct.\n\nSecond method:\n\n(30 -2)\u00d779 = 30\u00d779 -2\u00d779\n30\u00d779: 30\u00d770=2100, 30\u00d79=270, so total 2100+270=2370. Then 2\u00d779=158. 2370-158=2212. Correct.\n\nThird method:\n\n79\u00d720=1580, 79\u00d78=632. 1580+632=2212. Correct.\n\nFourth method:\n\n28\u00d780=2240, minus 28 gives 2212. Correct.\n\nAll methods lead to 2212, so I think that must be the correct product. I don't see any errors in my calculations now. So the answer should be 2212.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 28 and 79 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Break down the multiplication** using the distributive property:\n   \\[\n   28 \\times 79 = 28 \\times (70 + 9) = (28 \\times 70) + (28 \\times 9)\n   \\]\n   \n2. **Compute each part**:\n   - \\( 28 \\times 70 = 1960 \\) (since \\( 28 \\times 7 = 196 \\), then multiply by 10)\n   - \\( 28 \\times 9 = 252 \\) (since \\( 28 \\times 10 = 280 \\), subtract \\( 28 \\))\n\n3. **Add the results**:\n   \\[\n   1960 + 252 = 2212\n   \\]\n\n**Verification using alternative methods** (e.g., \\( (30 - 2) \\times 79 \\), breaking down \\( 28 \\times 80 - 28 \\), or vertical multiplication) all confirm the result.\n\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{2212}\\)",
