final_output:  ['\n\nThe product of 46 and 35 can be calculated using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n46 \\times 35 = 46 \\times (30 + 5) = (46 \\times 30) + (46 \\times 5)\n\\]\n\nFirst, compute \\(46 \\times 30\\):\n\\[\n46 \\times 30 = 46 \\times 3 \\times 10 = 138 \\times 10 = 1380\n\\]\n\nNext, compute \\(46 \\times 5\\):\n\\[\n46 \\times 5 = (40 \\times 5) + (6 \\times 5) = 200 + 30 = 230\n\\]\n\nAdd the two results together:\n\\[\n1380 + 230 = 1610\n\\]\n\n**Verification using alternative methods:**\n- **Standard multiplication:**\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{r}\n    \\quad 46 \\\\\n  \\times 35 \\\\\n  \\hline\n    \\quad 230 \\quad (\\text{46 × 5}) \\\\\n  + 1380 \\quad (\\text{46 × 30, shifted left by 1}) \\\\\n  \\hline\n    1610 \\\\\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n- **Mental math trick (using 50 − 4):**\n  \\[\n  46 \\times 35 = (50 - 4) \\times 35 = 50 \\times 35 - 4 \\times 35 = 1750 - 140 = 1610\n  \\]\n- **Breaking into smaller parts:**\n  \\[\n  (40 + 6) \\times 35 = 40 \\times 35 + 6 \\times 35 = 1400 + 210 = 1610\n  \\]\n\nAll methods confirm the result is consistent.\n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{1610}', ":\nOkay, let's see, I need to find the product of 46 and 35. Hmm, multiplication, right? Alright, so 46 times 35. I remember that multiplying two numbers can sometimes be done by breaking them down into smaller parts. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. Let me think... \n\nFirst, I can split 35 into 30 and 5. That way, 46 times 35 is the same as 46 times (30 + 5). Then, according to the distributive property, that would be 46*30 plus 46*5. That seems manageable. Let me calculate each part separately.\n\nStarting with 46 multiplied by 30. Hmm, 46 times 3 is 138, right? So if I multiply that by 10, since 30 is 3*10, then 138*10 is 1380. So 46*30 is 1380. Okay, that part makes sense.\n\nNow, the other part is 46 times 5. Let me work that out. 46*5... Well, 40*5 is 200, and 6*5 is 30. Adding those together gives 200 + 30 = 230. So 46*5 is 230.\n\nNow I need to add those two results together: 1380 + 230. Let's do that step by step. 1380 plus 200 is 1580, and then adding the remaining 30 gives 1580 + 30 = 1610. Wait, is that right? Let me check again. 1380 + 230. If I break down 230 into 200 + 30, adding 200 to 1380 gives 1580, then adding 30 more gets to 1610. Yeah, that seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method to verify. Let me try the standard multiplication algorithm. Writing it down:\n\n   46\nx 35\n------\nFirst, multiply 46 by 5. That's 230. Then multiply 46 by 30 (since the 3 in 35 is in the tens place, so it's actually 30). 46*30 is 1380. Then add them together: 230 + 1380 = 1610. Same result. Okay, that's good.\n\nWait another thought: Maybe using some other trick? Like, 46 times 35. I know that 45 times 35 is... Hmm, not sure if that helps. Alternatively, 46 is close to 50. Maybe 50 times 35 is 1750, then subtract 4 times 35. 4 times 35 is 140, so 1750 - 140 = 1610. Oh, that works too. That's another way to do it mentally. So that also gives 1610. Hmm, so all methods are pointing to 1610.\n\nJust to be thorough, maybe I can do the multiplication step by step the long way:\n\n       46\n     x35\n     -----\n       230  (this is 46 x 5)\n     +1380  (this is 46 x 30, shifted one position to the left)\n     -----\n      1610\n\nYep, that adds up. So I think the answer is 1610. Let me just check with a calculator in my mind. 40 times 35 is 1400, and 6 times 35 is 210, so 1400 + 210 is 1610. Wait, hold on, that's another way. Breaking 46 into 40 +6. So (40+6)*35 = 40*35 +6*35. 40*35 is 1400, and 6*35 is 210. 1400 +210 is indeed 1610. All these different methods are confirming the same answer. \n\nI think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. So the product of 46 and 35 is 1610.\n"]
