ossible. Well, since I can't use a calculator right now, but I can verify again step by step.\n\nAlternatively, maybe think of 60 as 50 + 10. Then 13*(50 +10) = 13*50 +13*10. 13*50 is 650 (since 10*50=500 and 3*50=150; 500+150=650), and 13*10 is 130. Adding those together, 650 +130 = 780. Yep, same answer again. \n\nAlright, I think I'm confident that the product of 13 and 60 is 780. All the different methods cross-check to the same result, so I don't think I made an error here. Probably that's the correct answer.\n"]
