    {
      "index": 0,
      "text": ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 32 and 77. Hmm, let me think. Multiplication, right? So 32 times 77. I remember that multiplying two numbers can sometimes be done by breaking them down into smaller parts. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. Let me try that.\n\nFirst, I can break down 77 into 70 and 7. So, 70 plus 7 equals 77. That way, I can multiply 32 by each part separately and then add the results together. Let me write that out:\n\n32 \u00d7 77 = 32 \u00d7 (70 + 7) = (32 \u00d7 70) + (32 \u00d7 7)\n\nAlright, now I need to compute each of those parts. Let's start with 32 times 70. Hmm, 32 times 70. Well, 70 is 7 times 10, so maybe I can do 32 \u00d7 7 first and then multiply by 10. Let's see:\n\n32 \u00d7 7 = 224. Then, multiplying by 10 gives 224 \u00d7 10 = 2240. So, 32 \u00d7 70 is 2240. Got that part.\n\nNow, the second part is 32 \u00d7 7. Wait, I just did that! 32 \u00d7 7 is 224. So that part is easy. \n\nNow, adding those two results together: 2240 + 224. Let me add them step by step. 2240 plus 200 is 2440, and then plus 24 more would be 2464. So, 2240 + 224 = 2464. \n\nWait a second, let me check that addition again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. 2240 plus 224. Breaking it down: 2000 + 200 = 2200, 40 + 24 = 64. So 2200 + 64 is 2264? Wait, no, that can't be right. Wait, hold on, maybe I messed up the breakdown.\n\nWait, 2240 is 2000 + 240, and 224 is 200 + 24. So adding them together: 2000 + 200 = 2200, 240 + 24 = 264. Then 2200 + 264 = 2464. Okay, that matches my first calculation. So maybe when I broke it down the second time, I did 2000 + 200 as 2200 and then 40 +24, but actually the 240 comes from 200 (from 2240) plus 40? Hmm, maybe I confused the numbers there, but the total is still 2464. Let me confirm with another method just to be sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can multiply 32 by 77 directly using the standard algorithm. Let me try that. \n\nFirst, write 32\n\u00d777\n------\nMultiply 32 by 7: 32\u00d77=224. Then multiply 32 by 70 (which is the 7 in the tens place, so it's 70). So 32\u00d770=2240. Then add them together: 224 + 2240 = 2464. Yep, same result. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method, like the lattice method or some other way to cross-verify. Let me try the lattice method quickly.\n\nFor lattice multiplication, you set up a grid. Since both numbers are two digits, it's a 2x2 grid. \n\nFirst, write 32 along the top and 77 along the right side. Then split each cell diagonally. \n\nMultiply 3 (tens place of 32) by 7 (tens place of 77): 3\u00d77=21. Write 21 in the top left cell, with 2 on the top and 1 below the diagonal.\n\nNext, 3 (tens) \u00d77 (units of 77): 3\u00d77=21. Write 21 in the top right cell.\n\nThen, 2 (units of 32) \u00d77 (tens of 77): 2\u00d77=14. Write 14 in the bottom left cell.\n\nFinally, 2\u00d77=14 in the bottom right cell.\n\nNow, add the numbers along the diagonals. Starting from the rightmost diagonal: just the 4 from the bottom right. Then next diagonal: 1 (from bottom right) +1 (from top right) +4 (from bottom left) = 6. Wait, let me visualize this better.\n\nWait, maybe I need to draw it properly. Let me see:\n\nThe grid would look like this after multiplication:\n\nTop row first cell (3\u00d77): 21\nTop row second cell (3\u00d77): 21\nBottom row first cell (2\u00d77):14\nBottom row second cell (2\u00d77):14\n\nWait, actually, perhaps I made a mistake in the placement. Let me correct that. The first digit of 32 is 3 (tens) and second is 2 (units). The first digit of 77 is 7 (tens) and second is 7 (units). So the grid should be:\n\nFirst row (3 and 2) multiplied by first column (7 and 7):\n\nFirst cell (3\u00d77 tens): 21\nSecond cell (3\u00d77 units):21\nThird cell (2\u00d77 tens):14\nFourth cell (2\u00d77 units):14\n\nWait, actually, the lattice is set up so that the top number's digits are on the top, and the right number's digits are on the right. Each cell is for the product of the corresponding digits. Then