 200 = 2200, 240 + 24 = 264. Then 2200 + 264 = 2464. Okay, that matches my first calculation. So maybe when I broke it down the second time, I did 2000 + 200 as 2200 and then 40 +24, but actually the 240 comes from 200 (from 2240) plus 40? Hmm, maybe I confused the numbers there, but the total is still 2464. Let me confirm with another method just to be sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can multiply 32 by 77 directly using the standard algorithm. Let me try that. \n\nFirst, write 32\n×77\n------\nMultiply 32 by 7: 32×7=224. Then multiply 32 by 70 (which is the 7 in the tens place, so it's 70). So 32×70=2240. Then add them together: 224 + 2240 = 2464. Yep, same result. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method, like the lattice method or some other way to cross-verify. Let me try the lattice method quickly.\n\nFor lattice multiplication, you set up a grid. Since both numbers are two digits, it's a 2x2 grid. \n\nFirst, write 32 along the top and 77 along the right side. Then split each cell diagonally. \n\nMultiply 3 (tens place of 32) by 7 (tens place of 77): 3×7=21. Write 21 in the top left cell, with 2 on the top and 1 below the diagonal.\n\nNext, 3 (tens) ×7 (units of 77): 3×7=21. Write 21 in the top right cell.\n\nThen, 2 (units of 32) ×7 (tens of 77): 2×7=14. Write 14 in the bottom left cell.\n\nFinally, 2×7=14 in the bottom right cell.\n\nNow, add the numbers along the diagonals. Starting from the rightmost diagonal: just the 4 from the bottom right. Then next diagonal: 1 (from bottom right) +1 (from top right) +4 (from bottom left) = 6. Wait, let me visualize this better.\n\nWait, maybe I need to draw it properly. Let me see:\n\nThe grid would look like this after multiplication:\n\nTop row first cell (3×7): 21\nTop row second cell (3×7): 21\nBottom row first cell (2×7):14\nBottom row second cell (2×7):14\n\nWait, actually, perhaps I made a mistake in the placement. Let me correct that. The first digit of 32 is 3 (tens) and second is 2 (units). The first digit of 77 is 7 (tens) and second is 7 (units). So the grid should be:\n\nFirst row (3 and 2) multiplied by first column (7 and 7):\n\nFirst cell (3×7 tens): 21\nSecond cell (3×7 units):21\nThird cell (2×7 tens):14\nFourth cell (2×7 units):14\n\nWait, actually, the lattice is set up so that the top number's digits are on the top, and the right number's digits are on the right. Each cell is for the product of the corresponding digits. Then you add diagonally.\n\nSo arranging it properly:\n\nThe top has 3 and 2 (the digits of 32), and the right side has 7 and 7 (digits of 77). The grid would be:\n\n       3    2\n    +----+----+\n  7 |21 |21 |\n    +----+----+\n  7 |14 |14 |\n    +----+----+\n\nNow, each cell is split by a diagonal line from top-right to bottom-left. The digits of the products are placed in the cells accordingly. For example, 21 would be written as 2 on the top half and 1 on the bottom half of the cell.\n\nNow, to add the diagonals:\n\nStarting from the rightmost diagonal (the bottom-right corner of the bottom-right cell), which is the units place. The only number there is 4 (from the 14 in the bottom-right cell's units part? Wait, maybe I need to think again.\n\nWait, each cell's right-top to left-bottom diagonal splits the cell into two. So for the cell with 14 (which is 2×7=14), the 1 would be in the top triangle and the 4 in the bottom. Wait, actually, maybe the multiplication results are split into tens and units. For example, in the first cell (3×7=21), the 2 is the tens and 1 is the units. So in the top-left cell, the top triangle has 2 and the lower triangle has 1.\n\nSimilarly, the top-right cell (3×7=21) would have 2 on top and 1 on the bottom.\n\nThe bottom-left cell (2×7=14) has 1 on top and 4 on the bottom.\n\nThe bottom-right cell (2×7=14) has 1 on top and 4 on the bottom.\n\nNow, when adding diagonally, starting from the bottom right:\n\nThe very first diagonal (the far right) has only the bottom part of the bottom-right cell, which is 4. \n\nNext diagonal to the left includes the bottom part of the top-right cell (1) and the top part of the bottom-right cell (1). Wait, maybe I need to visualize the diagonals properly.\n\nThe diagonals are from top-right to bottom-left. So the first diagonal (units place) is the lower right corner of the bottom-right cell: 4.\n\nThe next diagonal (tens place) includes the lower part of the top-right cell (1) and the upper part of the bottom-right cell (1), so 1+1=2. But also, the lower part of the bottom-left cell (4)? Wait, maybe I'm getting confused. Let me think step by step.\n\nActually, in lattice multiplication, the diagonals are added from right to left. Each diagonal corresponds to a digit in the result. Let me try again:\n\nLooking at the grid with the splits:\n\nTop-left cell: 2 (top) | 1 (bottom)\nTop-right cell: 2 (top) | 1 (bottom)\nBottom-left cell: 1 (top) | 4 (bottom)\nBottom-right cell: 1 (top) | 4 (bottom)\n\nNow, the diagonals are as follows:\n\nFirst diagonal (units place): the bottom part of the bottom-right cell: 4.\n\nSecond diagonal (tens place): the bottom part of the top-right cell (1) and the top part of the bottom-right cell (1). So 1 + 1 = 2. But also, the bottom part of the bottom-left cell (4)? Wait, maybe I need to see how the diagonals are formed.\n\nActually, each diagonal is formed by the lower triangles of the top cells and the upper triangles of the bottom cells. Let me try to list all the diagonals:\n\nStarting from the bottom right:\n\n1. The first diagonal (units place): the lower-right cell's lower triangle: 4.\n\n2. The second diagonal (tens place): the lower triangle of the top-right cell (1) and the upper triangle of the bottom-right cell (1). So 1 + 1 = 2.\n\n3. The third diagonal (hundreds place): the upper triangle of the top-right cell (2), the lower triangle of the bottom-left cell (4), and the upper triangle of the bottom-left cell (1)? Wait, this is getting confusing. Maybe I need to map each diagonal properly.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to add them as follows:\n\nArrange the numbers in the grid with their split cells, then sum along the diagonals from bottom to top, right to left.\n\nThe grid with the split cells:\n\nTop row first cell (3×7=21): \n   2\n  ---\n   1\n\nTop row second cell (3×7=21):\n   2\n  ---\n   1\n\nBottom row first cell (2×7=14):\n   1\n  ---\n   4\n\nBottom row second cell (2×7=14):\n   1\n  ---\n   4\n\nNow, the diagonals:\n\nStarting from the bottom right corner (the 4 in the lower half of the bottom-right cell):\n\n- First diagonal (units place): 4.\n\n- Second diagonal (tens place): the 1 (from the lower half of top-right cell) and the 1 (upper half of bottom-right cell). Wait, actually, the diagonals run from top-left to bottom-right? Maybe I need a different approach.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I should add the numbers along the diagonals that slope from top-right to bottom-left. Let me think of the grid as:\n\nThe four cells arranged in a 2x2 grid:\n\n[ (2/1)  (2/1) ]\n[ (1/4)  (1/4) ]\n\nWhere each cell is split into upper/lower.\n\nThe diagonals for addition are:\n\nFirst diagonal (the rightmost diagonal): the lower part of the top-right cell (1) and the lower part of the bottom-right cell (4). Wait, no, maybe not.\n\nAlternatively, the first diagonal (units digit) is the lower right cell's lower half (4). \n\nNext diagonal (tens place) is the lower half of the top-right cell (1) plus the upper half of the bottom-right cell (1). That's 1 +1 = 2.\n\nNext diagonal (hundreds place) is the upper half of the top-right cell (2), plus the lower half of the bottom-left cell (4), plus the upper half of the bottom-left cell (1)? Wait, this is getting too tangled. Maybe I need to look up the lattice method steps again mentally.\n\nAlternatively, maybe this is taking too long, and since I already have two methods giving me 2464, I can be more confident. But let me try another verification.\n\nAlternatively, 32 × 77. Let's think of 77 as 80 - 3. So, 32 × (80 - 3) = 32×80 - 32×3.\n\n32×80: 32×8=256, so 256×10=2560.\n\n32×3=96.\n\n2560 - 96 = 2560 - 100 +4 = 2460 +4 = 2464. Yep, same answer.\n\nAlright, so using distributive property in another way also gives me 2464. So that's three different methods all leading to the same result. I think that's solid. \n\nJust to be absolutely thorough, maybe do the multiplication step by step vertically:\n\n       32\n     ×77\n     -----\n       224  (this is 32 ×7)\n      2240  (this is 32 ×70, shifted one position to the left)\n     -----\n      2464\n\nAdding 224 and 2240 gives 2464. Yep, that's correct.\n\nAnother way: 30 ×77 = 2310, and 2×77=154. Then 2310 +154 = 2464. Yep, that works too.\n\nSo all methods confirm that the product of 32 and 77 is 2464. I think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, and cross-verifying with multiple methods gives the same result. So I'm confident the answer is 2464.\n"] 90%|█████████ | 450/500 [7:57:03<1:07:12, 80.66s/it]
