final_output:  ['\n\nTo find the product of 73 and 91, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy:\n\n1. **Standard Algorithm**:\n   - Multiply 73 by the units digit of 91 (1):\n     \\[\n     73 \\times 1 = 73\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 73 by the tens digit of 91 (9, which represents 90) and shift one position to the left:\n     \\[\n     73 \\times 90 = 6570\n     \\]\n   - Add the results:\n     \\[\n     73 + 6570 = 6643\n     \\]\n\n2. **Distributive Property**:\n   - Break down 91 into 90 and 1:\n     \\[\n     73 \\times 91 = 73 \\times (90 + 1) = 73 \\times 90 + 73 \\times 1\n     \\]\n   - Calculate each part:\n     \\[\n     73 \\times 90 = 6570 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 73 \\times 1 = 73\n     \\]\n   - Add the results:\n     \\[\n     6570 + 73 = 6643\n     \\]\n\n3. **Expanding Both Numbers**:\n   - Break down both numbers into tens and units:\n     \\[\n     (70 + 3) \\times (90 + 1)\n     \\]\n   - Use the distributive property:\n     \\[\n     70 \\times 90 + 70 \\times 1 + 3 \\times 90 + 3 \\times 1\n     \\]\n   - Calculate each term:\n     \\[\n     70 \\times 90 = 6300, \\quad 70 \\times 1 = 70, \\quad 3 \\times 90 = 270, \\quad 3 \\times 1 = 3\n     \\]\n   - Add the results:\n     \\[\n     6300 + 70 + 270 + 3 = 6643\n     \\]\n\n4. **Verification with Prime Factors**:\n   - Express 91 as \\(7 \\times 13\\):\n     \\[\n     73 \\times 91 = 73 \\times (7 \\times 13) = (73 \\times 7) \\times 13\n     \\]\n   - Calculate step-by-step:\n     \\[\n     73 \\times 7 = 511 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 511 \\times 13 = 6643\n     \\]\n\nAll methods consistently yield the result 6643. Therefore, the product of 73 and 91 is \\(\\boxed{6643}\\).', ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 73 and 91. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers... I remember that multiplication is just repeated addition, but doing that here would take too long. Maybe I can use the standard algorithm for multiplication? Let me recall how that works.\n\nFirst, I should write d