irst diagonal (units place) is the bottom-right part of the top-right cell (7) and the bottom-right part of the bottom-right cell (3). Wait, actually, maybe it's better to draw it mentally step by step.\n\nThe lattice method steps:\n\nAfter multiplying, each cell has the product split into tens and units across the diagonal. Then you add the numbers along each diagonal, starting from the bottom right.\n\nSo:\n\nFirst, the top row:\n\nFirst cell (7*9=63): split into 6 (tens) and 3 (units).\n\nSecond cell (7*1=7): split into 0 (tens) and 7 (units).\n\nSecond row:\n\nFirst cell (3*9=27): split into 2 (tens) and 7 (units).\n\nSecond cell (3*1=3): split into 0 (tens) and 3 (units).\n\nNow, the diagonals:\n\nThe rightmost diagonal (units place) consists of the units digits from the bottom-right of each cell in the last column. Wait, maybe the diagonals are arranged so that each diagonal corresponds to a place value.\n\nLet me see, the diagonals go from top-left to bottom-right. The first diagonal (units place) is the bottom-right part of the bottom-right cell (3). The next diagonal (tens place) includes the bottom-right of the top-right cell (7) and the top-left of the bottom-right cell (0). Then the next diagonal (hundreds place) includes the top-left of the top-right cell (0), the bottom-right of the top-left cell (3), and the top-left of the bottom-left cell (2). The next diagonal (thousands place) is the top-left of the top-left cell (6). Wait, this is getting confusing.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to arrange the diagonals as follows:\n\nThe rightmost diagonal is the bottom-right of the top-right cell (7) and the bottom-right of the bottom-right cell (3). Wait, maybe I need to look up the exact steps again mentally.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the sum is done by adding along the diagonals starting from the lower right corner. Let me try:\n\nStarting from the bottom right corner, the first diagonal is just the 3 (from 3*1). Then moving to the next diagonal, we 