s. Because 51 is 50+1, so 33*50 is 1650, plus 33*1 is 33, totaling 1683. \n\nHmm, so all these methods are giving me the same result, 1683. That seems consistent. Maybe I should just confirm with straight multiplication. Let me do the vertical multiplication:\n\n\u2003\u00a051  \n\u00d7\u00a033  \n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500  \nFirst multiply 51 by 3: 51\u00d73=153  \nThen multiply 51 by 30 (so shift one position to the left): 51\u00d730=1530  \nAdd them together:  \n\u00a0153  \n+1530  \n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500  \n1683  \n\nYep, that's the same as before. So all methods point to 1683. I think that's the correct answer. Let me just do a quick check using estimation to see if it makes sense. 50 times 30 is 1500, and 50 times 33 would be 1650, so 51 times 33 should be a bit more than 1650. Since 1683 is 33 more than 1650, that makes sense because 51 is one more than 50, so adding 33 (which is 1 times 33) gives the extra. So that checks out. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using calculator verification? Well, since I don't have a calculator here, but I can do another method. Let me try breaking down 33 into 32 +1. Then 51\u00d732 +51\u00d71. 51\u00d732: 51\u00d730=1530, plus 51\u00d72=102, so 1530+102=1632. Then add 51: 1632 +51=1683. Again, same result. \n\nAlright, I think that's solid. All different methods give me 1683, so I'm confident that's the correct product of 51 and 33.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 51 and 33 is calculated as follows:\n\n**Method 1: Standard Multiplication**  \n```\n   51  \n\u00d7 33  \n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500  \n  153   (51 \u00d7 3)  \n+1530   (51 \u00d7 30, shifted one position left)  \n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500  \n 1683  \n```\n\n**Method 2: Distributive Property**  \n\\[\n(50 + 1)(30 + 3) = 50 \\times 30 + 50 \\times 3 + 1 \\times 30 + 1 \\times 3  \n= 1500 + 150 + 30 + 3 = 1683\n\\]\n\n**Method 3: Simplified Breakdown**  \n\\[\n51 \\times 33 = 51 \\times (30 + 3) = (51 \\times 30) + (51 \\times 3) = 1530 + 153 = 1683\n\\]