st adds a zero at the end of the number. So 11 times 10 is 11 with a zero added, making it 110. That's a quick trick for multiplying by 10. \n\nWait, is that always true? Let me think. For whole numbers, yes. Multiplying any whole number by 10 appends a zero. So 5\u00d710=50, 12\u00d710=120, so 11\u00d710=110. Yep, that works. \n\nI can also visualize it. If I have 11 items in a row and 10 such rows, the total number is 11 multiplied by 10. So like 10 rows of 11 stars each. Each row has 11, so 10 rows would be 110 stars. \n\nAlternatively, using exponents, 10 is 10^1 and 11 is 11, but not sure if that helps here. Maybe breaking down into prime factors? 11 is a prime number, and 10 is 2\u00d75. So 11\u00d72\u00d75. Then grouping them as (11\u00d72)\u00d75 =22\u00d75=110, or 11\u00d7(2\u00d75)=11\u00d710=110. Either way.\n\nAlternatively, using a calculator, but since this is mental math, I don't need a calculator here. All the methods I know point to the answer being 110. I don't see any mistakes in my reasoning. Let me just confirm once more: 11 multiplied by 10. 11 times 10 is 110. Yes, that's correct.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 11 and 10 is calculated by multiplying the two numbers together. \n\n\\[\n11 \\times 10 = 110\n\\]\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n1. **Multiplication Basics:** Multiplying 11 by 10 means adding 11 to itself 10 times.\n2. **Calculation:** \n   - \\( 11 \\times 10 = 110 \\).\n3. **Shortcut:** Multiplying any number by 10 appends a zero to the end of that number. Thus, \\( 11 \\times 10 = 110 \\).\n\n**Answer:** 110",
