      "text": ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 15 and 77. Let me think about how to do this step by step. First, I remember that the product means multiplication, right? So, basically, I need to multiply 15 by 77. Hmm, how should I approach this?\n\nWell, maybe I can break it down into smaller parts. Let me recall the standard multiplication method. If I write 15 times 77, that's the same as 15 multiplied by 70 plus 15 multiplied by 7. Wait, is that right? Yeah, because 77 is 70 + 7, so using distributive property: 15*(70 +7) = 15*70 +15*7. That should work.\n\nLet me calculate 15*70 first. Hmm, 15 times 70. Since 70 is 7*10, maybe I can do 15*7 first and then multiply by 10. Let's see, 15*7. 10*7 is 70, and 5*7 is 35, so 70+35 is 105. So 15*7 is 105. Then, multiplying by 10 gives 105*10 = 1050. Okay, so 15*70 is 1050.\n\nNow the other part is 15*7. Wait, I just did that part. 15*7 is 105. So adding those two results together: 1050 + 105. Let me add those. 1050 plus 100 is 1150, and then plus 5 more is 1155. So the total is 1155?\n\nWait a second, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Let me do the multiplication another way to confirm. Maybe using the standard algorithm.\n\nMultiplying 15 by 77:\n\nFirst, write 15  \n           x77  \n       --------\n           105  (this is 15 x 7)  \n          1050  (this is 15 x 70, shifted one position to the left)  \n       --------\n          1155\n\nYes, that adds up to 1155. Hmm, seems right. Alternatively, maybe I can do 15 times 77 as (10 +5) times 77. Let's see, that would be 10*77 +5*77. 10*77 is 770, and 5*77 is 385. Adding those together: 770 + 385. 700 + 300 is 1000, and 70 +85 is 155. So 1000 +155 is 1155. Yep, same result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using another method, like the lattice method? Let me try that quickly. Although I might get confused, but let's see. For 15 x77.\n\nBreaking it down into digits: 1 and 5 for 15, and 7 and 7 for 77. So setting up a 2x2 grid. Multiply each digit pair and add diagonally.\n\nFirst digit 1 (tens place) and 7 (tens place): 1*7=7, that's the top left. Then 1 (tens) and 7 (units): 1*7=7, top right. Then 5 (units) and 7 (tens):5*7=35, bottom left. 5 and 7: 35, bottom right.\n\nThen, add diagonally from the right. The rightmost cell is the units: 5 (from 35). Then moving left, the next diagonal is 7 (from top right) plus 3 (from 35's tens place) plus 7 (from bottom right's tens? Wait, maybe I'm mixing up. Let me think again.\n\nWait, maybe the lattice method is a bit more involved. Let me visualize it properly. The grid would have:\n\nFirst row for 1 (tens) and 5 (units), and columns for 7 (tens) and 7 (units). So the grid cells would be:\n\nTop row: 1 x 7 (tens) and 1 x7 (units).  \nSecond row: 5 x7 (tens) and 5x7 (units).  \nWait, actually, maybe I should structure it as:\n\nThe multiplicands are 15 (two digits) and 77 (two digits), so a 2x2 grid. Each cell will have the product of the corresponding digits. Let me write it out:\n\nDigits of 15: 1 (tens) and 5 (units).  \nDigits of 77: 7 (tens) and 7 (units).  \nSo the grid:\n\n       7 (tens)    7 (units)\n1 (tens)|    7        7\n5 (units)|  35       35\n\nWait, actually, each cell is the product of the row digit and column digit. So first row (1):\n\n1*7 (tens place) = 7 (so written as 07?) and 1*7 (units) = 07. Wait, maybe as two-digit numbers. For example, 1*7 is 07, split into tens and units. Similarly, 5*7 is 35, so 3 and 5. Hmm, perhaps better to think of each product as two digits, even if the first is zero. Let me try again.\n\nFirst cell (top left): 1 (tens) *7 (tens) = 70. Wait, actually, maybe I'm overcomplicating. Let me look up the lattice method steps mentally. Each cell is split diagonally, and the product's digits are placed in the upper and lower triangles. So for 1*7 (tens digit of 15 and tens digit of 77), that's 7, which is a single digit, so it goes in the upper part. Wait, perhaps this is getting too confusing. Maybe I should stick to the previous methods which gave me 1155. Since two different methods gave me the same answer, I can be more confident.\n\nAlternatively, let me use another approach: 15*77 is the same as 77*15. Maybe 77*10 is 770, plus 77*5 which is 385. Adding those gives 770+385. 700+300 is 1000, 70+85 is 155, so total 1155 again. Yep.\n\nAlternatively, use exponents? Not sure. 15 is 3*5, 77 is 7*11. So 3*5*7*11. Let me compute that step by step. 3*7 is 21, 5*11 is 55. Then 21*55. Hmm, 21*55. Let me compute that: 20*55 +1*55 = 1100 +55=1155. So that works too. Wow, that's another way. So all methods are converging to 1155. I think that's solid.\n\nWait, just to be thorough, maybe do straight multiplication:\n\n   15  \nx77  \n------  \nMultiply 15 by 7 (units place): 15*7=105.  \nMultiply 15 by 70 (tens place, so shift one position): 15*70=1050.  \nAdd them: 105 + 1050 = 1155. Yep.\n\nAlternatively, using the vertical multiplication:\n\n     15  \n   x77  \n   -----  \n     105   (15 x7)  \n    105    (15x70, but shifted one to the left, so it's actually 1050)  \n   ------  \n    1155  \n\nYes, that's correct. So all methods confirm that 15 multiplied by 77 is 1155. I think that's the right answer. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, and cross-verifying with different methods gives the same result. So I'm confident the product is 1155.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 15 and 77 is \\boxed{1155}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 15 and 77, we can use the distributive property and break down the multiplication into smaller parts:\n\n1. Calculate \\(15 \\times 70\\):\n   \\[\n   15 \\times 70 = 15 \\times (7 \\times 10) = (15 \\times 7) \\times 10 = 105 \\times 10 = 1050\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(15 \\times 7\\):\n   \\[\n   15 \\times 7 = 105\n   \\]\n\n3. Add the results of the two calculations:\n   \\[\n   1050 + 105 = 1155\n   \\]\n\nWe can verify this result using another method by breaking down the numbers differently:\n1. Express 77 as \\(70 + 7\\) and use the distributive property:\n   \\[\n   15 \\times 77 = 15 \\times (70 + 7) = (15 \\times 70) + (15 \\times 7) = 1050 + 105 = 1155\n   \\]\n\nAnother verification using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n- Multiply 15 by 7 (units place):\n  \\[\n  15 \\times 7 = 105\n  \\]\n- Multiply 15 by 70 (tens place, shifted one position to the left):\n  \\[\n  15 \\times 70 = 1050\n  \\]\n- Add the results:\n  \\[\n  105 + 1050 = 1155\n  \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 15 and 77 is \\(\\boxed{1155}\\).",
