 7110 = 7347. Yep, same result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use a calculator? Wait, but since I'm supposed to do this manually, but I think the multiple methods all confirming 7347 is solid. Let me just ensure that in my initial calculation I didn't make any arithmetic errors.\n\nFirst partial product: 93\u00d79. 90\u00d79=810, 3\u00d79=27, so 810+27=837. Correct.\n\nSecond partial product: 93\u00d770. Since 7 is in the tens place, so 7\u00d710. So 93\u00d77=651, then \u00d710 gives 6510. Then adding 837+6510=7347. All checks out.\n\nAnother sanity check: Estimate. 93 is close to 90, 79 is close to 80. 90\u00d780=7200. The actual numbers are both slightly higher (93 is 3 more, 79 is 1 less). So the product should be a bit more than 7200. 7347 is 147 more, which seems reasonable because (90+3)(80-1)=90\u00d780 -90\u00d71 +3\u00d780 -3\u00d71=7200-90+240-3=7200+147=7347. Exactly. So that's right.\n\nTherefore, I'm confident the product of 93 and 79 is 7347.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 93 and 79 is calculated as follows:\n\n**Step 1:** Multiply 93 by 9 (ones place of 79):\n\\[\n93 \\times 9 = 837\n\\]\n\n**Step 2:** Multiply 93 by 70 (tens place of 79, shifted left by one digit):\n\\[\n93 \\times 70 = 6,\\!510\n\\]\n\n**Step 3:** Add the partial products:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n  \\hphantom{0}837 \\\\\n+ 6,\\!510 \\\\\n\\hline\n 7,\\!347 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n**Verification using the distributive property:**\n\\[\n(90 + 3)(70 + 9) = 90 \\times 70 + 90 \\times 9 + 3 \\times 70 + 3 \\times 9 = 6,\\!300 + 810 + 210 + 27 = 7,\\!347\n\\]\n\n**Final Answer:**  \nThe product of 93 and 79 is \\boxed{7347}.",
