    {
      "index": 0,
      "text": ":\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 87 and 10. Hmm, product means multiplication, right? Let me think. Multiplying by 10 is usually straightforward because you just add a zero at the end. Wait, is that always true? Let me check with a smaller number first. Like 5 times 10 is 50. Yeah, that works. So if I take 87 and multiply by 10, I should just add a zero to the end of 87. That would make it 870. Let me verify that another way. \n\nAlternatively, I can break it down. 87 times 10 is the same as 87 added to itself 10 times. But that's a bit tedious. Maybe I can use the distributive property. 87 is 80 + 7. So, 10 times 80 is 800, and 10 times 7 is 70. Then add those two results together: 800 + 70 equals 870. Yep, that matches. \n\nAnother way to think about it is shifting the decimal. Multiplying by 10 moves the decimal point one place to the right. Since 87 is a whole number, the decimal is after the 7, so moving it right would make it 870.0, which is 870. \n\nWait, is there any case where multiplying by 10 doesn't just add a zero? Maybe with decimals? Like 0.5 times 10 is 5. But in this case, 87 is a whole number, so it should work. \n\nI think all methods point to the same answer: 870. Let me just do the straight multiplication to be sure. \n\n   87\nx 10\n-----\n   00  (87 x 0)\n+870   (87 x 10, shifted one position to the left)\n-----\n  870\n\nYep, that adds up. So the product is definitely 870. I can't see any mistakes here. All the different methods confirm it. So I'm confident the answer is 870.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 87 and 10 is calculated by multiplying the two numbers together. \n\n**Step-by-Step Calculation:**\n1. **Multiplication by 10 Rule:** When multiplying any number by 10, simply add a zero to the end of the number.  \n   \\( 87 \\times 10 = 870 \\).\n\n2. **Verification via Distributive Property:**  \n   Break down 87 into \\( 80 + 7 \\):  \n   \\( (80 + 7) \\times 10 = 80 \\times 10 + 7 \\times 10 = 800 + 70 = 870 \\).\n\n3. **Decimal Shift Method:**  \n   Multiplying by 10 shifts the decimal point one place to the right:  \n   \\( 87.0 \\times 10 = 870.0 \\).\n\n**Final Answer:**  \n\\boxed{870}",
